window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"GI-1":{"unit":"GI-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01","title":"Gastrointestinal Embryology","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the gut from a simple endodermal tube to the adult digestive tract and its glands. It covers the three gut divisions and their arteries, midgut herniation and rotation, separation of the esophagus from the airway, budding of the liver and pancreas, colonization of the gut wall by neural crest, and recanalization of the duodenum. Board questions usually describe a newborn with vomiting, drooling, a midline sac, or delayed meconium and ask which developmental step failed. They also use the gut divisions to explain blood supply and why visceral pain is felt in the epigastric, periumbilical, or suprapubic region. If you know what each step normally does, every defect follows from it.","prerequisites":["Germ layers and embryonic folding in weeks 3 and 4","Neural crest formation and migration (NE-1)","Ventral branches of the abdominal aorta","Organization of sympathetic, parasympathetic, and visceral afferent fibers (NE-9)","Peritoneum, mesenteries, and retroperitoneal organs (GI-4)"],"connections":[{"to":"Gastrointestinal blood supply (GI-5)","how":"The celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric territories are the foregut, midgut, and hindgut, and their junctions are the watershed zones."},{"to":"The gut in the body cavities (GI-4)","how":"Rotation and fixation of the mesenteries explain which organs end up secondarily retroperitoneal."},{"to":"Respiratory embryology (PU-1)","how":"The lung bud and trachea arise from the same foregut tube as the esophagus, so their separation defects are shared."},{"to":"Motility, digestion, and absorption (GI-6)","how":"The neural crest derived enteric plexuses are the circuits that coordinate peristalsis and secretion."},{"to":"Congenital gastrointestinal disease (GI-13-H)","how":"Atresias, malrotation, abdominal wall defects, and Hirschsprung disease are developmental failures described here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Primitive gut","def":"The endodermal tube formed when embryonic folding incorporates the dorsal part of the yolk sac in week 4."},{"term":"Vitelline duct","def":"The narrow connection between the midgut and the yolk sac, attached to the apex of the midgut loop until it normally regresses."},{"term":"Septum transversum","def":"A plate of mesoderm between the heart and the yolk stalk into which the liver grows and which contributes to the diaphragm and ventral mesentery."},{"term":"Physiologic umbilical herniation","def":"The normal temporary passage of the midgut loop into the umbilical cord from week 6 until about week 10."},{"term":"Malrotation","def":"Incomplete or abnormal rotation of the midgut loop that leaves the bowel malpositioned on a narrow mesentery."},{"term":"Midgut volvulus","def":"Twisting of the malrotated midgut around the axis of the superior mesenteric artery, causing obstruction and ischemia."},{"term":"Tracheoesophageal septum","def":"The partition formed by fusion of the tracheoesophageal ridges that separates the ventral airway from the dorsal esophagus."},{"term":"Recanalization","def":"Reopening of a gut lumen that was temporarily filled by proliferating epithelium."},{"term":"Annular pancreas","def":"A ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of the duodenum formed by a bifid ventral pancreatic bud."},{"term":"Pancreas divisum","def":"Failure of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts to fuse, so most pancreatic juice drains through the minor papilla."},{"term":"Aganglionosis","def":"Absence of myenteric and submucosal ganglion cells in a segment of bowel, the defining lesion of Hirschsprung disease."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C01","obj":"GI-1.1","title":"Gut tube divisions, their arteries, and referred visceral pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"During week 4, cephalocaudal and lateral folding of the embryo draws the dorsal part of the yolk sac inside as the primitive gut. Endoderm forms the epithelial lining and the secretory cells of the liver and pancreas; the surrounding splanchnic mesoderm forms the connective tissue, smooth muscle, and visceral peritoneum. The tube is closed cranially by the oropharyngeal membrane and caudally by the cloacal membrane, and the midgut stays open to the yolk sac through the vitelline duct."},{"type":"p","text":"Each abdominal division is supplied by one unpaired ventral branch of the aorta, and these three arteries develop from the vitelline arteries that once supplied the yolk sac. Because each artery grows with its segment, the arterial borders are the embryologic borders. The foregut ends just distal to the major duodenal papilla, where the bile duct enters, and the midgut ends at the junction of the proximal two thirds and distal third of the transverse colon. The junctions receive blood from both neighbors, such as the pancreatic head through the superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. Where territories meet, as at the splenic flexure, collateral flow is thin and the wall is vulnerable to ischemia when perfusion falls (GI-5)."},{"type":"table","title":"The three gut divisions","cols":["Division","Adult derivatives (abdominal)","Artery","Parasympathetic","Pain referred to"],"rows":[["Foregut","Abdominal esophagus, stomach, duodenum to the major papilla, liver, gallbladder, pancreas","Celiac trunk","Vagus nerve","Epigastrium"],["Midgut","Duodenum distal to the papilla, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, proximal two thirds of transverse colon","Superior mesenteric artery","Vagus nerve","Periumbilical region"],["Hindgut","Distal third of transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum, anal canal above the pectinate line","Inferior mesenteric artery","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4)","Suprapubic region"]],"caption":"The pharynx and thoracic esophagus are also foregut but take blood from other arteries. The spleen is supplied by the celiac trunk but is mesodermal, forming in the dorsal mesogastrium, so it is not a gut derivative."},{"type":"p","text":"Visceral pain fibers from most of the gut run back along the sympathetic splanchnic nerves to the spinal segments that supplied each division in the embryo: roughly T5 to T9 for the foregut, T10 to T11 for the midgut, and T12 to L2 for the hindgut. The exception is the distal sigmoid colon and rectum, whose pain fibers travel with the pelvic splanchnic nerves to S2 to S4. In the dorsal horn they converge on neurons that also receive input from skin of the same segments, so the brain projects the sensation onto those dermatomes. The umbilicus lies in the T10 dermatome, which is why midgut pain is periumbilical."},{"type":"p","text":"Visceral afferents are sparse and bilateral and respond to distension, ischemia, and inflammation rather than cutting. Visceral pain is therefore dull, cramping, poorly localized, felt in the midline, and often accompanied by nausea, sweating, and pallor. When inflammation reaches the parietal peritoneum, which is supplied by somatic nerves of the body wall, the pain becomes sharp and localizes over the organ."},{"type":"flow","title":"How visceral pain is referred","steps":["Distension, ischemia, or inflammation stimulates visceral afferents","Fibers travel with sympathetic splanchnic nerves","Enter the cord at the segment of that gut division","Converge on dorsal horn neurons shared with skin input","Brain localizes pain to the midline dermatome"],"caption":"Convergence of visceral and somatic input on the same spinal neurons explains why gut pain is felt on the body wall."},{"type":"example","title":"Why appendicitis pain moves","text":"Obstruction of the appendix lumen distends its wall. The appendix is midgut, so its afferents enter the cord at about T10, and the first pain is dull, periumbilical, and accompanied by nausea. Over several hours inflammation reaches the somatically innervated parietal peritoneum in the right iliac fossa, so the pain shifts to the right lower quadrant and becomes sharp, localized, and worse with movement."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C02","obj":"GI-1.2","title":"Midgut herniation, rotation, and their failures","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The midgut forms a U shaped primary intestinal loop suspended by dorsal mesentery. The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) runs in that mesentery as the axis of the loop, and the vitelline duct attaches at its apex. The cranial limb becomes the distal duodenum, jejunum, and most of the ileum; the caudal limb becomes the distal ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. A small swelling on the caudal limb, the cecal bud, marks the future cecum."},{"type":"p","text":"In week 6 the loop lengthens quickly while the large liver and the mesonephric kidneys fill the small abdominal cavity. Lacking room, the loop passes into the extraembryonic coelom within the umbilical cord. This physiologic herniation is normal. While in the cord, the loop turns 90 degrees counterclockwise, viewed from the front, around the SMA. Around week 10, as liver growth slows, the mesonephros regresses, and the abdomen enlarges, the loops return. The jejunum returns first and settles on the left, and the loop completes a further 180 degrees, for 270 degrees in total. The cecum returns last, lies at first below the liver, and then descends to the right iliac fossa."},{"type":"flow","title":"Normal midgut rotation","steps":["Week 6: midgut loop herniates into the umbilical cord","First 90 degrees counterclockwise around the SMA axis","Cranial limb coils into jejunum and ileum","About week 10: loops return, jejunum first","Further 180 degrees during return, 270 degrees in total","Cecum returns last and descends to the right iliac fossa","Colonic mesenteries fuse with the posterior wall"],"caption":"Rotation viewed from the front. Fusion of the colonic mesenteries makes the ascending and descending colon secondarily retroperitoneal."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal rotation places the third part of the duodenum behind the SMA, with the duodenojejunal junction left of midline, and gives the small bowel mesentery a long diagonal root that keeps the bowel from twisting."},{"type":"p","text":"In malrotation the loop stops short of 270 degrees. With nonrotation (only the first 90 degrees) the small bowel lies on the right and the colon on the left. With incomplete rotation the cecum stays high near the midline, and peritoneal (Ladd) bands run from it across the second part of the duodenum to the right posterior wall, where they can compress the duodenum. The dangerous feature is the mesentery: it stays short, narrow, and centered on the SMA, so the whole midgut can twist around the vessel. This midgut volvulus obstructs the bowel and occludes the SMA and superior mesenteric vein, threatening ischemia from the distal duodenum to the transverse colon. It most often presents in the first month of life, though it can occur at any age, with sudden bilious vomiting, since the block lies below the papilla. A contrast study shows the duodenojejunal junction right of midline."},{"type":"p","text":"If the herniated loops fail to return, the infant has an omphalocele: bowel, and sometimes liver, in a midline sac into which the cord inserts. It often accompanies trisomies 13 and 18, Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, and cardiac and neural tube defects."},{"type":"table","title":"Omphalocele and gastroschisis","cols":["Feature","Omphalocele","Gastroschisis"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Physiologic hernia fails to return","Lateral body wall fails to close"],["Location","Midline, through the umbilical ring","Beside the umbilicus, usually on the right"],["Covering","Sac of amnion and peritoneum","None; bowel bathed in amniotic fluid"],["Cord insertion","Into the sac","Normal, beside the defect"],["Associated anomalies","Frequent, including aneuploidy","Uncommon; bowel often thickened and inflamed"]],"caption":"Both raise maternal serum alpha fetoprotein."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn's contrast study shows the duodenojejunal junction to the right of the spine. Which segments of bowel are at risk of infarction, and which are spared?","explain":"The narrow mesentery of malrotation lets the midgut twist around the SMA, so the whole SMA territory from the distal duodenum to the distal transverse colon is at risk, while the hindgut supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery is spared.","check":"Compare with the malrotation paragraph and the C01 table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C03","obj":"GI-1.3","title":"Separation of the esophagus from the airway","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Early in week 4 the respiratory (laryngotracheal) diverticulum buds from the ventral wall of the foregut just below the pharynx. As it grows caudally, longitudinal tracheoesophageal ridges grow in from the lateral walls of the foregut and fuse in the midline to form the tracheoesophageal septum. The septum divides the tube into a ventral part, which becomes the larynx, trachea, and lung buds, and a dorsal part, which becomes the esophagus. The two remain connected only at the laryngeal inlet."},{"type":"flow","title":"Normal division of the foregut","steps":["Respiratory diverticulum buds from the ventral foregut","Tracheoesophageal ridges grow in from the lateral walls","Ridges fuse into the tracheoesophageal septum","Ventral tube becomes larynx, trachea, and lung buds","Dorsal tube becomes the esophagus"],"caption":"Airway and esophagus share one origin, so a faulty septum leaves them joined or leaves the esophagus interrupted."},{"type":"p","text":"The esophagus is short at first and lengthens quickly as the heart and lungs descend into the thorax. Its epithelium proliferates and partly fills the lumen before recanalizing, and its muscle comes from the surrounding mesenchyme: striated in the upper esophagus and smooth in the lower esophagus, with a mixed zone between. The vagus supplies both, the striated part directly and the smooth part through the enteric plexus. If the esophagus fails to lengthen enough, part of the stomach is pulled through the diaphragm as a congenital hiatal hernia."},{"type":"p","text":"When the septum deviates posteriorly, or the dorsal foregut wall is pushed forward, the division is incomplete. The result is esophageal atresia, in which the esophagus ends blindly, a tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF), an abnormal channel between the esophagus and trachea, or both. The defect occurs in about 1 in 3,000 births. In esophageal atresia, swallowed amniotic fluid cannot reach the intestine, where it is normally absorbed and returned to the mother through the placenta, so polyhydramnios is common. About half of affected infants have other anomalies, and the defect belongs to the VACTERL association: vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal, and limb anomalies."},{"type":"table","title":"Main forms of esophageal atresia and fistula","cols":["Form","Anatomy","Key findings and their basis"],"rows":[["Atresia with distal fistula (most common, about 85 to 90%)","Blind upper pouch; lower esophagus joins the trachea near the carina","Drooling and choking or cyanosis at the first feed as the pouch overflows into the larynx; a feeding tube coils in the pouch on radiograph; gas in the stomach because air passes through the fistula; gastric acid can reflux into the lungs"],["Isolated atresia (no fistula)","Blind upper pouch; lower esophagus does not reach the airway","Drooling and polyhydramnios; gasless abdomen because no air can reach the stomach"],["Isolated fistula without atresia (H type)","Continuous esophagus with a fistula to the trachea","Coughing with feeds and recurrent aspiration pneumonia; often recognized later because swallowing is possible"]],"caption":"Gas below the diaphragm means the lower esophagus connects to the airway; a gasless abdomen means it does not."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn drools, cannot pass a feeding tube, and has no gas anywhere in the abdomen on radiograph. Which form of the defect is present?","explain":"Isolated esophageal atresia without a distal fistula. Without a connection between the trachea and lower esophagus, swallowed or inhaled air cannot reach the stomach, so the bowel is gasless.","check":"Compare with the table of forms of esophageal atresia and fistula."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C04","obj":"GI-1.4","title":"Liver, biliary tree, and pancreas","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The liver begins in weeks 3 to 4 as the hepatic diverticulum, an endodermal outgrowth from the ventral wall of the distal foregut where the duodenum will form. It grows into the septum transversum. There its endodermal cords become hepatocytes and the lining of the intrahepatic bile ducts, and they surround the vitelline and umbilical veins, which break up to form the hepatic sinusoids. Mesoderm of the septum transversum forms the connective tissue of the liver, and the ventral mesentery derived from it becomes the lesser omentum and the falciform ligament. Hepatocytes begin to secrete bile at about week 12."},{"type":"p","text":"The stalk joining the liver bud to the foregut narrows to become the bile duct, and a small ventral outgrowth from it forms the gallbladder and cystic duct. The bile duct first opens on the ventral wall of the duodenum; when the duodenum rotates to the right and becomes C shaped, its opening is carried to the dorsomedial wall, so the adult bile duct passes behind the duodenum. In biliary atresia the extrahepatic ducts are destroyed by a progressive inflammatory and fibrosing process around the time of birth, rather than by simple failure of a developmental step. Because bile cannot reach the gut, infants develop conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, dark urine, and pale stools in the first weeks, followed by biliary cirrhosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The pancreas forms from two endodermal buds of the duodenum. The dorsal bud grows into the dorsal mesentery; the ventral bud lies beside the bile duct. When the duodenum rotates, the ventral bud travels with the bile duct around the back of the duodenum and comes to lie below and behind the dorsal bud. The parenchyma fuses, and the duct systems join so that the main pancreatic duct drains with the bile duct at the major papilla. Islets develop from the same endoderm in the third month."},{"type":"flow","title":"Development of the pancreas","steps":["Dorsal and ventral buds arise from duodenal endoderm","Duodenum rotates to the right and becomes C shaped","Ventral bud and bile duct swing behind the duodenum","Ventral bud lies below and behind the dorsal bud","Parenchyma fuses and the two duct systems join","Main duct and bile duct drain at the major papilla"],"caption":"The ventral bud migrates with the bile duct, which is why the two share the major papilla."},{"type":"table","title":"Contributions of the two pancreatic buds","cols":["Bud","Parenchyma formed","Duct formed","Drains at"],"rows":[["Ventral","Uncinate process and lower part of the head","Proximal part of the main pancreatic duct","Major papilla, with the bile duct"],["Dorsal","Upper head, neck, body, and tail","Distal main duct; its proximal part persists as the accessory duct","Minor papilla (accessory duct)"]],"caption":"The adult main pancreatic duct is the whole ventral duct joined to the distal dorsal duct."},{"type":"p","text":"Two defects follow from this sequence. In annular pancreas the ventral bud is bifid; one half migrates normally while the other passes around the opposite side of the duodenum, so a ring of pancreatic tissue encircles the second part of the duodenum. The ring can narrow or obstruct the duodenum in a newborn or present later in life, and it is associated with Down syndrome and duodenal atresia. In pancreas divisum the parenchyma fuses but the ducts do not. The dorsal duct then drains most of the gland through the small minor papilla, while the ventral duct drains only the uncinate process and lower head. It is the most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas and is usually silent, but in a minority the high flow through a narrow opening is thought to raise ductal pressure and contribute to recurrent acute pancreatitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C05","obj":"GI-1.5","title":"Neural crest and the enteric nervous system","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The enteric nervous system comes from neural crest. Most of it arises from vagal neural crest at the level of the hindbrain, with a smaller sacral crest contribution to the hindgut. These cells enter the foregut and migrate in a cranial to caudal wave through the gut mesenchyme, reaching the distal rectum during the early fetal period. They first form the myenteric plexus between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers, and some then move inward to form the submucosal plexus."},{"type":"table","title":"Cells that control the gut wall","cols":["Component","Location","Origin","Main role"],"rows":[["Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","Between circular and longitudinal muscle","Neural crest","Motility: peristalsis and muscle tone"],["Submucosal (Meissner) plexus","Submucosa","Neural crest","Secretion, absorption, and local blood flow"],["Interstitial cells of Cajal","Within the muscle layers","Mesenchyme, not neural crest","Pacemaker slow waves"]],"caption":"Enteric neurons include excitatory neurons that release acetylcholine and inhibitory neurons that release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)."},{"type":"p","text":"Migration depends on signaling. Gut mesenchyme secretes glial cell line derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF), which binds the RET receptor tyrosine kinase on crest cells and drives their survival, proliferation, and migration. Endothelin 3 acting on the endothelin B receptor (EDNRB) keeps precursors dividing so the pool is large enough to colonize the whole gut, and the transcription factor SOX10 is needed for the crest cells to survive. Melanocytes also come from neural crest, so EDNRB, endothelin 3, and SOX10 mutations can combine aganglionosis with patchy depigmentation and deafness (Waardenburg syndrome type 4)."},{"type":"p","text":"Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon) results when the wave stops before reaching the end of the gut. Loss of function mutations in RET are the most common identified cause. Because colonization proceeds cranial to caudal, the aganglionic segment always begins at the internal anal sphincter and extends proximally a variable distance, most often only through the rectosigmoid. It affects about 1 in 5,000 births, is about four times more common in boys, and about 10% of cases occur in children with Down syndrome."},{"type":"flow","title":"From RET mutation to megacolon","steps":["RET loss of function reduces GDNF signaling","Neural crest wave stops before the distal bowel","Distal segment lacks myenteric and submucosal ganglia","No inhibitory NO and VIP neurons, so segment cannot relax","Peristalsis stops at the narrow segment: functional obstruction","Proximal normal colon dilates and hypertrophies"],"caption":"The diseased segment is the narrow one; the dilated colon above it is normal bowel stretched by retained stool."},{"type":"p","text":"The aganglionic segment stays tonically contracted, and the rectoanal inhibitory reflex, relaxation of the internal sphincter when the rectum is distended, is absent. Newborns fail to pass meconium in the first 48 hours and develop abdominal distension and vomiting; a rectal examination may release an explosive gush of gas and stool. A contrast enema shows a narrow distal segment and a transition zone to dilated colon. Rectal suction biopsy shows no ganglion cells in the submucosa and thickened acetylcholinesterase positive nerve fibers from extrinsic nerves. Enterocolitis from stasis is the most feared complication. Acquired megacolon in Chagas disease is the microbiologic parallel: Trypanosoma cruzi destroys ganglia that had formed normally."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Could a failure of neural crest migration leave the transverse colon aganglionic while the rectum has normal ganglia?","explain":"Essentially no. Crest cells colonize the gut from cranial to caudal, so any arrest leaves the most distal bowel uncolonized. The rectum is always involved, and skip lesions are extremely rare.","check":"Compare with the description of migration and the Hirschsprung paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C06","obj":"GI-1.6","title":"Duodenal recanalization and duodenal atresia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The duodenum forms from the end of the foregut and the beginning of the midgut, with the junction just distal to the opening of the bile duct, which is why it receives both celiac and superior mesenteric blood. As the stomach rotates, the duodenum becomes C shaped, swings to the right, and most of it becomes secondarily retroperitoneal when its mesentery fuses with the posterior wall. During the second month its epithelium proliferates so rapidly that it fills the lumen. Vacuoles then form within the epithelial plug and coalesce, and the lumen is restored by the end of the embryonic period."},{"type":"p","text":"If recanalization fails, the duodenum is completely closed (atresia) or narrowed by a web or diaphragm (stenosis). Most duodenal atresias lie in the second part just distal to the major papilla, so bile enters the obstructed segment and the vomitus is bilious; a lesion above the papilla gives nonbilious vomiting. The fetus cannot carry swallowed amniotic fluid to the absorptive intestine, so polyhydramnios is common. A plain radiograph shows the double bubble: gas in the stomach and in the dilated proximal duodenum, with no gas beyond in complete atresia. About 30% of affected infants have trisomy 21, and annular pancreas, malrotation, and cardiac defects often coexist."},{"type":"p","text":"Atresia of the jejunum and ileum has a different cause. These segments become atretic after an intrauterine vascular accident, such as volvulus or occlusion of a mesenteric branch, that leads to ischemic necrosis and resorption of a segment of bowel. They produce several dilated loops, more distension, and are not associated with Down syndrome. Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is not a developmental failure; the pyloric muscle thickens after birth."},{"type":"table","title":"Localizing obstruction in a vomiting newborn","cols":["Condition","Onset and vomitus","Imaging","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Esophageal atresia with distal TEF","First feed; regurgitation of unchanged feed, drooling","Plain film: tube coils in the upper pouch; gas in the stomach","Faulty tracheoesophageal septum"],["Duodenal atresia","First day; usually bilious","Plain film: double bubble, no distal gas","Failed recanalization"],["Malrotation with volvulus","Often first weeks, sudden; bilious","Upper GI contrast study: duodenojejunal junction right of midline; distal gas may be present","Narrow mesentery twists around the SMA"],["Jejunoileal atresia","First day; bilious, with distension","Plain film: several dilated loops","Intrauterine vascular accident"],["Hirschsprung disease","First days; delayed meconium, then bilious vomiting","Contrast enema: narrow distal segment, transition zone, dilated colon above","Failed neural crest migration"],["Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","About 2 to 8 weeks; projectile, nonbilious","Plain film: large gastric bubble, little distal gas","Postnatal pyloric muscle hypertrophy"]],"caption":"Bile in the vomitus places the obstruction below the major papilla; nonbilious vomiting places it above."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a double bubble","text":"A newborn whose mother had polyhydramnios vomits bile stained fluid a few hours after the first feed. The abdomen is not distended, and the radiograph shows a double bubble with no gas beyond. Bile places the block below the major papilla. The two bubbles are the stomach and the dilated proximal duodenum, and the absence of distal gas means the obstruction is complete rather than a stenosis. Polyhydramnios shows that swallowed fluid never reached the absorptive bowel, and the flat abdomen shows the block is high. This is duodenal atresia from failed recanalization of the second part, and the infant should be examined for features of trisomy 21. If gas were present beyond the duodenum, malrotation with volvulus would have to be considered, because a twist can be partial and still strangle the entire midgut."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each defect in this chapter is a normal step that failed, and the step tells you the anatomy, the signs, and the associations. Pathology explains the timing and pattern of each defect: failed recanalization gives duodenal atresia, while a vascular accident gives jejunoileal atresia, and failed return gives omphalocele, while failed body wall closure gives gastroschisis. Genetics and syndromes link the gut to the rest of the body, as with trisomy 21 in duodenal atresia, annular pancreas, and Hirschsprung disease, and the VACTERL association with tracheoesophageal fistula."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and microbiology show why these structures matter after birth. The enteric plexuses are the circuits that coordinate peristalsis, and losing their inhibitory neurons, whether by failed migration or by Trypanosoma cruzi infection, produces the same functional obstruction and dilation. The limits of embryologic reasoning are real: the timing of weeks and the frequencies of associated anomalies vary between sources, and conditions such as biliary atresia and pyloric stenosis appear in newborns but are not simple failures of a developmental step."}],"summary":["Foregut, midgut, and hindgut are supplied by the celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, and inferior mesenteric artery; the borders lie just distal to the major papilla and at the distal third of the transverse colon.","Visceral pain travels with sympathetic afferents to the spinal segments of each division and is referred to the epigastrium, umbilicus, or suprapubic region; it localizes only when the parietal peritoneum is involved.","The midgut herniates into the cord in week 6, rotates 270 degrees counterclockwise around the SMA (90 degrees outside, 180 degrees during return), and returns by about week 10.","Malrotation leaves a narrow mesentery that can twist around the SMA, causing bilious vomiting and ischemia of the whole midgut; Ladd bands can compress the duodenum.","Omphalocele is failed return of the physiologic hernia into a sac of amnion and peritoneum, often with aneuploidy; gastroschisis is an uncovered body wall defect beside the umbilicus.","The tracheoesophageal septum separates the ventral airway from the dorsal esophagus; posterior deviation gives atresia, fistula, or both, most often a blind upper pouch with a distal fistula.","The ventral pancreatic bud rotates behind the duodenum with the bile duct; a bifid ventral bud gives annular pancreas, and failed duct fusion gives pancreas divisum with drainage through the minor papilla.","Neural crest colonizes the gut cranial to caudal under GDNF and RET signaling, so Hirschsprung disease always involves the rectum and produces a narrow aganglionic segment below a dilated normal colon.","Failed recanalization of the second part of the duodenum gives bilious vomiting, polyhydramnios, a double bubble, and a strong association with trisomy 21; jejunoileal atresia follows vascular accidents."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen: abdominal viscera, their arterial supply, innervation, and referred visceral pain"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, congenital abnormalities including atresia, Hirschsprung disease, and abdominal wall defects; The Pancreas, congenital anomalies"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"General Principles of Gastrointestinal Function: Motility, Nervous Control, and Blood Circulation"}]},"GI-2":{"unit":"GI-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01","title":"Gastrointestinal Histology","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter teaches the microscopic structure of the digestive tube and its glands: the four-layer wall and its nerve plexuses, the epithelium of each region, the cells of the gastric gland, the features that identify each intestinal segment, the liver lobule and acinus, and the exocrine pancreas and salivary glands. Board questions usually show a section or describe a cell and ask what it makes, where it sits, or what fails when it is lost. Each structure here is explained by its job, so that a finding such as high gastrin with low acid, or necrosis around the central vein, can be reasoned out rather than memorized.","prerequisites":["Basic tissue types: epithelium, connective tissue, smooth and skeletal muscle","Cell organelles and the secretory pathway (rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi, secretory granules)","Gross anatomy of the digestive tract, peritoneum, and retroperitoneum (GI-1)","Germ layers and derivatives of the foregut, midgut, and hindgut","Hematoxylin and eosin staining: what basophilia and eosinophilia indicate"],"connections":[{"to":"Gastrointestinal physiology (secretion and motility)","how":"The cells and plexuses identified here are the effectors of gastric acid secretion, enzyme release, and peristalsis."},{"to":"Biochemistry (vitamin B12 and drug metabolism)","how":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells and cytochrome P450 in zone 3 hepatocytes link gastric and liver histology to B12 deficiency and toxic metabolites."},{"to":"Immunology (mucosal immunity)","how":"Lamina propria lymphocytes, Peyer patches, and M cells form the gut-associated lymphoid tissue that samples antigen and drives IgA responses."},{"to":"Pathology of the esophagus, stomach, and liver","how":"Barrett metaplasia, autoimmune gastritis, and zonal hepatic necrosis are explained directly by the histology of each organ."},{"to":"Embryology (neural crest and the cloaca)","how":"Neural crest migration forms the enteric plexuses, and the endoderm and ectoderm junction at the pectinate line sets the anal canal's epithelium and drainage."}],"terms":[{"term":"Muscularis mucosae","def":"The thin layer of smooth muscle that forms the deepest part of the mucosa and separates it from the submucosa."},{"term":"Enteric nervous system","def":"The intrinsic neurons of the gut wall, organized as the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses, that can run secretion and motility without input from the brain."},{"term":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","def":"Pacemaker cells associated with the myenteric plexus that generate the slow waves of gut smooth muscle."},{"term":"Metaplasia","def":"Reversible replacement of one differentiated cell type by another that better withstands a chronic stress."},{"term":"Pectinate (dentate) line","def":"The line in the anal canal where hindgut endoderm meets the ectoderm of the proctodeum, marking a change in epithelium, blood supply, lymph drainage, and nerves."},{"term":"Oxyntic gland","def":"The acid-secreting gland of the gastric fundus and body, containing parietal, chief, mucous neck, enterochromaffin-like, and D cells."},{"term":"Intrinsic factor","def":"A glycoprotein from parietal cells that binds vitamin B12 and is required for its uptake in the terminal ileum."},{"term":"Brunner glands","def":"Submucosal glands of the duodenum that secrete alkaline mucus to protect the mucosa from gastric acid."},{"term":"M cell","def":"A specialized epithelial cell over Peyer patches that transports luminal antigens and microbes to underlying immune cells."},{"term":"Space of Disse","def":"The narrow space between the sinusoidal endothelium and hepatocytes, into which hepatocyte microvilli project and where stellate cells live."},{"term":"Hepatic acinus","def":"The functional unit of the liver, centered on the terminal branches of a portal triad and divided into zones 1 to 3 by distance from that blood supply."},{"term":"Centroacinar cell","def":"A pale duct cell, unique to the pancreas, that lies inside the acinus where the intercalated duct begins."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C01","obj":"GI-2.1","title":"The four layers of the gut wall and the enteric plexuses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"From the esophagus to the anal canal, the digestive tube is built on one plan of four concentric layers. Learning that plan once lets you read any section: identify the layers first, then look for the local changes in epithelium, glands, folds, and lymphoid tissue that identify the segment (C02 and C04)."},{"type":"p","text":"The mucosa has three parts. The epithelium protects, secretes, and absorbs. The lamina propria beneath it is loose connective tissue with capillaries, lymphatics (the lacteals of the small intestine), and many lymphocytes and plasma cells, which makes the mucosa a major immune organ. The muscularis mucosae, a thin sheet of smooth muscle, moves the mucosa independently of the wall and marks the mucosa's lower border. That border matters in the colon, where the lamina propria has few lymphatics: a carcinoma confined above the muscularis mucosae rarely spreads, while one that crosses it into the submucosa can reach lymphatics and metastasize."},{"type":"p","text":"The submucosa is dense connective tissue carrying larger vessels and lymphatics, glands in the esophagus and duodenum, and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. The muscularis externa has an inner circular layer that narrows the lumen and an outer longitudinal layer that shortens the segment; the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies between them and coordinates the two into peristalsis, with interstitial cells of Cajal setting the slow-wave rhythm. Each plexus sits beside what it controls: the submucosal plexus regulates glandular secretion, mucosal blood flow, and the muscularis mucosae, and the myenteric plexus regulates motility."},{"type":"table","title":"Layers of the gut wall","cols":["Layer","Contents","Regional variations"],"rows":[["Mucosa","Epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae","Epithelium changes by region; villi only in the small intestine"],["Submucosa","Dense connective tissue, vessels, lymphatics, Meissner plexus","Glands in the esophagus and duodenum (Brunner); Peyer patches extend into it in the ileum"],["Muscularis externa","Inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle, Auerbach plexus between them","Stomach adds an inner oblique layer; upper esophagus is skeletal muscle; colon gathers longitudinal muscle into taeniae coli"],["Serosa or adventitia","Serosa: mesothelium on connective tissue. Adventitia: connective tissue only","Serosa over intraperitoneal organs; adventitia over the thoracic esophagus and the nonperitoneal surfaces of retroperitoneal segments"]],"caption":"The circular layer thickens locally to form sphincters such as the pylorus and the internal anal sphincter."},{"type":"p","text":"The outer coat depends on the peritoneum. Organs suspended in the peritoneal cavity are covered by serosa, a mesothelium that lets them slide. Where peritoneum is absent, as around the thoracic esophagus and behind retroperitoneal segments such as most of the duodenum and the ascending and descending colon, adventitia anchors the organ instead. With no serosal barrier, esophageal cancer spreads readily into the mediastinum."},{"type":"p","text":"Enteric neurons derive from neural crest cells that migrate down the gut from cranial to caudal. Inhibitory motor neurons, which release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide, are needed for smooth muscle to relax, so losing them causes failure of relaxation rather than paralysis. In achalasia, loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons keeps the lower esophageal sphincter closed; Trypanosoma cruzi (Chagas disease) destroys the plexus with similar effects."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hirschsprung disease from missing ganglion cells","steps":["Neural crest cells fail to complete migration","Distal colon lacks ganglion cells in both plexuses","No inhibitory neurons, so the segment cannot relax","Tonically contracted segment causes functional obstruction","Bowel above dilates (megacolon); meconium passage is delayed"],"caption":"The aganglionic segment always begins at the rectum and extends proximally for a variable length."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A rectal suction biopsy samples only mucosa and submucosa. How can it diagnose Hirschsprung disease when the myenteric plexus lies deeper?","explain":"The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies in the submucosa, and in Hirschsprung disease both plexuses lack ganglion cells. Absent submucosal ganglion cells, usually with hypertrophied nerve trunks, are enough.","check":"The location of each plexus in the layer table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C02","obj":"GI-2.2","title":"Epithelium along the tube and Barrett metaplasia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The epithelium in each region matches its main stress. Where food or stool rubs the surface, many cell layers protect it and are shed and replaced from below. Where the job is secretion or absorption, a single layer of columnar cells keeps the distance to blood short."},{"type":"table","title":"Epithelium by region","cols":["Region","Epithelium","Functional reason"],"rows":[["Esophagus","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Resists abrasion from swallowed food; submucosal glands lubricate"],["Stomach","Simple columnar; every surface cell secretes mucus (no goblet cells)","Mucus and bicarbonate gel protect against acid"],["Small intestine","Simple columnar enterocytes with brush border, plus goblet cells","Absorption across a large surface"],["Colon and rectum","Simple columnar in straight crypts, many goblet cells","Water and salt absorption; mucus lubricates drying stool"],["Anal canal above pectinate line","Simple columnar (colorectal type), then a narrow transitional zone","Continuous with rectal mucosa"],["Anal canal below pectinate line","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous, becoming keratinized skin at the anal verge","Resists friction during defecation"]],"caption":"Abrupt junctions occur at the gastroesophageal junction and the pectinate line."},{"type":"p","text":"At the gastroesophageal junction the pale squamous mucosa meets the red columnar gastric mucosa at an irregular squamocolumnar junction (Z line). Reflux of acid, pepsin, and bile bathes the squamous side, which lacks the surface mucus and bicarbonate layer that protects the stomach. With chronic injury the distal esophagus can undergo metaplasia: the squamous lining is replaced by columnar epithelium of intestinal type, which resists acid better. Endoscopically, salmon-colored tongues of mucosa extend upward from the junction."},{"type":"p","text":"Barrett esophagus is diagnosed on biopsy by intestinal metaplasia: columnar epithelium containing goblet cells. Goblet cells are the key, because normal esophageal and gastric surface epithelium contains none. Metaplasia protects in the short term, but the cells continue to divide under inflammatory stress and accumulate mutations (TP53 loss is common), so the lining can progress through dysplasia to adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus. Squamous cell carcinoma, by contrast, arises from squamous epithelium, usually in the middle third, and is linked to tobacco and alcohol."},{"type":"flow","title":"From reflux to esophageal adenocarcinoma","steps":["Chronic reflux of acid, pepsin, and bile","Repeated injury to distal squamous epithelium","Intestinal metaplasia: columnar cells with goblet cells","Accumulating mutations produce dysplasia","Invasive adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus"],"caption":"Each step is a histologic stage that a biopsy can identify."},{"type":"p","text":"The pectinate line marks where the endoderm of the hindgut meets the ectoderm of the proctodeum. Because two embryologic territories meet there, the epithelium, vessels, lymphatics, and nerves all change at the same line, and the table below lets you predict the behavior of hemorrhoids and cancers on either side."},{"type":"table","title":"Above versus below the pectinate line","cols":["Feature","Above","Below"],"rows":[["Origin","Hindgut endoderm","Ectoderm of the proctodeum"],["Epithelium","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous"],["Artery","Superior rectal (inferior mesenteric)","Inferior rectal (internal pudendal)"],["Vein","Superior rectal to the portal system","Inferior rectal to the systemic (caval) system"],["Lymph","Internal iliac and inferior mesenteric nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes"],["Nerves and pain","Visceral; insensitive to cutting","Somatic (inferior rectal branch of pudendal); painful"],["Hemorrhoids","Internal: painless bleeding","External: painful, especially if thrombosed"],["Typical carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma, often HPV related"]],"caption":"The portal and systemic venous connection here is a site of portosystemic anastomosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C03","obj":"GI-2.3","title":"Gastric gland cells and autoimmune gastritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The stomach has three glandular regions. The cardia and the antrum (pyloric region) have mainly mucous glands, and the antral glands also contain G cells. The fundus and body have oxyntic glands, which make acid and enzymes. An oxyntic gland opens into a gastric pit lined by surface mucous cells; below it lie the isthmus, which holds the stem cells, then the neck with mucous neck cells and many parietal cells, then the base with chief cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Cells of the gastric glands","cols":["Cell","Location","Appearance on H&E","Product"],"rows":[["Surface mucous","Surface and pits, whole stomach","Columnar, pale apical mucin","Mucus and bicarbonate"],["Mucous neck","Neck of oxyntic glands","Small, pale mucin granules","Mucus"],["Parietal","Fundus and body, mostly upper half of gland","Large, round, central nucleus, pink cytoplasm (fried egg)","HCl and intrinsic factor"],["Chief","Fundus and body, gland base","Basophilic base, apical granules","Pepsinogen and gastric lipase"],["ECL","Fundus and body","Not distinct on H&E","Histamine"],["D","Body and antrum","Not distinct on H&E","Somatostatin"],["G","Antrum (also duodenum)","Not distinct on H&E","Gastrin into blood"]],"caption":"Parietal and chief cells are confined to the fundus and body; G cells are in the antrum."},{"type":"p","text":"Appearance follows function. Parietal cells are pink because they are packed with mitochondria that supply ATP to the H+/K+-ATPase. At rest the pump is stored in tubulovesicles; on stimulation by acetylcholine, gastrin, and histamine these fuse with deep intracellular canaliculi, multiplying the secretory surface. Carbonic anhydrase supplies H+, and the matching bicarbonate exits into blood in exchange for Cl-, producing the postprandial alkaline tide. Chief cells are basophilic because of rough endoplasmic reticulum making pepsinogen, which acid converts to pepsin."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Acid feedback on gastrin","steps":["Meal buffers acid; antral pH rises","G cells release gastrin into blood","Gastrin stimulates ECL histamine and parietal acid","Antral pH falls","D cells release somatostatin","Somatostatin suppresses G cells; gastrin falls"],"caption":"Acid in the antrum is the main brake on gastrin. Removing acid removes the brake."},{"type":"p","text":"Autoimmune gastritis is directed at the oxyntic mucosa. CD4 T cells attack the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase, and antibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor appear. Fundic and body glands atrophy and are partly replaced by intestinal metaplasia, while the antrum, which has no parietal cells, is spared. This distribution distinguishes it from Helicobacter pylori gastritis, which usually begins in the antrum."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicted secretions in autoimmune gastritis","cols":["Secretion","Change","Reason"],"rows":[["HCl","Falls to achlorhydria","Parietal cells destroyed"],["Intrinsic factor","Falls","Parietal cells destroyed; blocking antibodies"],["Pepsinogen I","Falls","Made by chief and mucous neck cells of oxyntic glands"],["Gastrin","Rises","Antral G cells intact; no acid to trigger somatostatin"],["ECL cells (histamine source)","Rises","Gastrin is trophic to ECL cells; hyperplasia can form neuroendocrine tumors"]],"caption":"Every loss is a body and fundus product; every rise comes from the spared antrum or from gastrin acting downstream."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of intrinsic factor prevents B12 uptake in the terminal ileum. Because the liver stores several years of B12, pernicious anemia appears late, with megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts. Achlorhydria also reduces nonheme iron absorption and raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the laboratory pattern","text":"A woman with hypothyroidism has fatigue, macrocytic anemia, and reduced vibration sense. Serum gastrin is very high and pepsinogen I is low. First, high gastrin has two possible sources: a gastrinoma, or loss of acid feedback. Low pepsinogen I says chief cells of the oxyntic mucosa are gone, which favors atrophy rather than a tumor; a gastrinoma would bring high acid and ulcers, not atrophy. Atrophy of the body and fundus also removes parietal cells, explaining achlorhydria and lost intrinsic factor, which explains B12 deficiency, macrocytosis, and dorsal column signs. Coexisting autoimmune thyroid disease fits autoimmune gastritis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C04","obj":"GI-2.4","title":"Identifying the duodenum, jejunum, ileum, and colon","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The small intestine enlarges its absorptive surface at three scales. Plicae circulares are permanent folds of mucosa with a submucosal core; villi are finger-like projections of mucosa, each with a capillary network and a central lacteal; and microvilli form the brush border of each enterocyte. Together they expand the surface several hundredfold compared with a flat tube. Between the villi, tubular crypts (of Lieberkuhn) extend down to the muscularis mucosae. Stem cells near the crypt base renew the epithelium every few days, and Paneth cells at the very base, with bright eosinophilic granules, release lysozyme and defensins that shape the crypt's microbial environment."},{"type":"p","text":"The segments share this plan, so they are told apart by a few added features, each explained by the segment's job. The duodenum receives acidic chyme from the stomach, so Brunner glands in its submucosa secrete alkaline mucus to protect the mucosa until pancreatic bicarbonate arrives. The jejunum does most nutrient absorption, so it has the tallest and most closely spaced plicae and long villi. The ileum faces a larger bacterial load and carries Peyer patches: aggregated lymphoid follicles in the lamina propria that bulge into the submucosa, on the side opposite the mesentery. The epithelium over them contains M cells, which pass antigens to dendritic cells and lymphocytes and start IgA responses. The terminal ileum also absorbs the B12 and intrinsic factor complex and reabsorbs bile acids."},{"type":"p","text":"The colon absorbs water and salt and lubricates stool, so it has no villi and no plicae circulares, only straight crypts crowded with goblet cells. Its outer longitudinal muscle is gathered into three taeniae coli, which pucker the wall into haustra."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing features of each segment","cols":["Feature","Duodenum","Jejunum","Ileum","Colon"],"rows":[["Villi","Broad, leaf-like","Long, finger-like","Shorter","None"],["Plicae circulares","Begin beyond the first part","Tallest, most numerous","Fewer, absent distally","None"],["Submucosal glands","Brunner glands","None","None","None"],["Lymphoid tissue","Scattered","Scattered","Peyer patches","Solitary follicles"],["Goblet cells","Fewer","More","More still","Most abundant"],["Paneth cells","Present","Present","Most numerous","Normally absent beyond the proximal colon"]],"caption":"Villi separate small intestine from colon; Brunner glands mark the duodenum; Peyer patches mark the ileum."},{"type":"example","title":"Naming a segment from a section","text":"A slide shows simple columnar epithelium with tall villi and crypts. Large lymphoid follicles with germinal centers fill the lamina propria and push into the submucosa, and the submucosa contains no glands. Work in order. Villi are present, so this is small intestine, not colon or appendix. No submucosal glands rules out the duodenum. Organized lymphoid follicles extending into the submucosa are Peyer patches, so the segment is ileum. If the same section had tall closely packed plicae without large follicles, it would be jejunum. A small lymphoid aggregate alone does not prove ileum, because solitary follicles occur throughout the gut."},{"type":"p","text":"These features predict where disease appears. Celiac disease injures the proximal small bowel, where gluten arrives first, flattening villi and lengthening crypts; iron and folate, absorbed proximally, become deficient. Salmonella Typhi and Yersinia enter through M cells, so typhoid ulcers form over Peyer patches and can perforate. Crohn disease favors the terminal ileum, causing B12 deficiency and bile acid loss. Paneth cells appearing in the distal colon signal chronic mucosal injury, as in ulcerative colitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C05","obj":"GI-2.5","title":"The hepatic lobule, acinus, and zonal injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes form plates one to two cells thick separated by sinusoids. The liver receives about 75% of its blood from the portal vein, carrying absorbed nutrients and toxins, and about 25% from the hepatic artery, which is richer in oxygen. Branches of both, with a bile duct and lymphatics, run together in each portal triad (portal tract). Blood from both mixes in the sinusoids, flows toward the central vein, and leaves through the hepatic veins. Bile moves in the opposite direction, from canaliculi between hepatocytes toward the bile duct in the triad."},{"type":"p","text":"The same tissue can be divided in two useful ways. The classic lobule is a hexagon centered on a central vein with portal triads at its corners; it is easy to see on a slide. The acinus is centered instead on the terminal vessels running between two triads and extends to the two nearest central veins. Because it follows the direction of blood flow, the acinus explains injury patterns."},{"type":"p","text":"The sinusoidal endothelium has open fenestrations and almost no basement membrane, so plasma, including large proteins and lipoproteins, passes freely into the space of Disse, where hepatocyte microvilli exchange material with it. Two other cell types live at this interface."},{"type":"table","title":"Cells and spaces of the sinusoid","cols":["Structure","Location","Normal role","Role in disease"],"rows":[["Sinusoidal endothelium","Lines sinusoids","Fenestrated, open filter","Loses fenestrations and gains a basement membrane in cirrhosis"],["Space of Disse","Between endothelium and hepatocytes","Plasma contacts hepatocyte microvilli","Filled with collagen in fibrosis, impairing exchange"],["Kupffer cells","Within the sinusoid lumen","Macrophages clearing gut bacteria, debris, old red cells","Release cytokines that activate stellate cells"],["Stellate (Ito) cells","In the space of Disse","Store vitamin A in lipid droplets","Become myofibroblasts that lay down collagen"]],"caption":"In chronic injury, TGF-beta and other signals turn quiescent stellate cells into collagen-producing myofibroblasts, the central event in fibrosis and cirrhosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Zone 1 hepatocytes lie next to the triad and receive blood first, so they get the most oxygen and nutrients; they are rich in enzymes of gluconeogenesis, fatty acid oxidation, and urea synthesis. Zone 3 hepatocytes surround the central vein and receive blood last, after upstream cells have extracted much of the oxygen, so their oxygen tension is lowest. Zone 3 also contains the most cytochrome P450, including CYP2E1. Both facts make zone 3 the most vulnerable to hypoperfusion and to drugs that become toxic when metabolized. Shock causes centrilobular necrosis, right heart failure causes centrilobular congestion (nutmeg liver), and alcohol injury is also concentrated there. Zone 1 meets portal blood first, and periportal necrosis is typical of eclampsia; zone 2 necrosis is classic for yellow fever."},{"type":"flow","title":"Acetaminophen injury in zone 3","steps":["Overdose saturates glucuronidation and sulfation","CYP2E1 oxidizes excess drug to reactive NAPQI","Zone 3 glutathione is depleted","NAPQI binds proteins, damaging mitochondria","Centrilobular (zone 3) hepatocyte necrosis"],"caption":"Injury is greatest where the oxidizing enzyme is most concentrated and glutathione is first exhausted."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a chronic heavy drinker develop liver injury after a smaller acetaminophen overdose than a nondrinker?","explain":"Chronic alcohol use induces CYP2E1, so more NAPQI forms, and poor nutrition lowers glutathione stores, so less is neutralized. Both changes act in zone 3.","check":"The zone 3 enzyme content and the acetaminophen flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C06","obj":"GI-2.6","title":"Exocrine pancreas, islets, and salivary glands","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pancreas and the salivary glands are both branched glands whose secretory acini drain through a tree of ducts. An acinus makes the protein product; the ducts modify the water and electrolytes."},{"type":"p","text":"The pancreatic acinar cell is a classic protein-exporting cell. Its base is deeply basophilic from rough endoplasmic reticulum, and its apex is filled with eosinophilic zymogen granules. These store inactive proenzymes (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase) with amylase and lipase, plus a trypsin inhibitor that protects the cell. Pale centroacinar cells sit inside the acinus where the intercalated duct begins; with the duct cells they secrete bicarbonate-rich fluid through CFTR in response to secretin, flushing the enzymes to the duodenum. Premature activation of trypsin inside acinar cells causes autodigestion in acute pancreatitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Islets of Langerhans are pale, rounded clusters scattered among the acini, making up only about 1% to 2% of pancreatic mass. They look pale because endocrine cells lack the dense rough endoplasmic reticulum and large zymogen granules of acinar cells. They have no ducts and secrete into a rich network of fenestrated capillaries: beta cells (insulin) are the majority, with alpha (glucagon), delta (somatostatin), and PP cells. Because islets and acini are separate, disease can remove one and spare the other. In cystic fibrosis, defective CFTR leaves secretions thick, ducts plug, and acini atrophy and fibrose, while islets persist until late, when diabetes may develop."},{"type":"p","text":"Salivary acini come in two types. Serous cells have round basal nuclei, basophilic cytoplasm, and granules of watery, protein-rich secretion containing amylase, so serous acini stain dark. Mucous cells look pale and foamy, with nuclei flattened against the base, because the mucin they store is lost during tissue processing. In mixed glands, serous cells can cap mucous tubules as serous demilunes. Myoepithelial cells around the acini contract to expel saliva. Striated ducts, lined by cells with basal infoldings and many mitochondria, reabsorb Na+ and Cl- and secrete K+ and bicarbonate, which makes saliva hypotonic."},{"type":"table","title":"Telling the salivary glands and pancreas apart","cols":["Gland","Acini","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Parotid","Almost entirely serous","Striated ducts; adipocytes increase with age; no islets"],["Submandibular","Mixed, mostly serous","Mucous tubules with serous demilunes"],["Sublingual","Mixed, mostly mucous","Pale mucous acini dominate"],["Exocrine pancreas","Serous","Islets and centroacinar cells; no striated ducts"]],"caption":"Parotid and pancreas both look purely serous, so islets and centroacinar cells identify the pancreas."},{"type":"p","text":"Shared histology explains shared disease. Mumps virus targets serous glands, causing parotitis and sometimes pancreatitis, and either can raise serum amylase. In Sjogren syndrome, lymphocytes destroy salivary and lacrimal acini, producing dry mouth and eyes."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and anatomy set the structure: neural crest migration builds the enteric plexuses, and the meeting of endoderm and ectoderm sets the anal canal's pectinate line. The peritoneal relationship of each segment decides whether it carries serosa or adventitia and therefore how a tumor spreads."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology, biochemistry, immunology, and pathology then follow from the cells. Parietal cells link the stomach to B12 metabolism and autoimmunity; the gastrin and somatostatin loop explains why atrophy raises gastrin; M cells and Peyer patches link the ileum to mucosal immunity and to enteric infection; and the oxygen and enzyme gradient across the acinus explains zonal necrosis. Section features overlap, so a single lymphoid aggregate or a few goblet cells can mislead; identify a section from several features together."}],"summary":["The gut wall has mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia; the Meissner plexus in the submucosa controls secretion and blood flow, and the Auerbach plexus between the muscle layers controls motility.","Hirschsprung disease is absence of ganglion cells in both plexuses from failed neural crest migration, so the distal segment cannot relax.","Stratified squamous epithelium lines the esophagus and the anal canal below the pectinate line; simple columnar epithelium lines the stomach through the upper anal canal.","Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells caused by chronic reflux and is the precursor of distal esophageal adenocarcinoma.","Parietal cells (HCl, intrinsic factor) and chief cells (pepsinogen) are in the fundus and body; G cells (gastrin) are in the antrum.","Autoimmune gastritis destroys body and fundus glands: acid, intrinsic factor, and pepsinogen I fall, gastrin rises from the spared antrum, and B12 deficiency follows.","Villi mean small intestine; Brunner glands mean duodenum, tall plicae mean jejunum, Peyer patches mean ileum, and goblet-rich crypts without villi mean colon.","Blood flows from portal triad to central vein, so zone 3 has the least oxygen and the most CYP2E1 and is injured first by ischemia and acetaminophen.","Kupffer cells are sinusoidal macrophages; stellate cells in the space of Disse store vitamin A and become collagen-producing myofibroblasts in fibrosis.","Pancreatic acini have basophilic bases and zymogen granules; pale islets and centroacinar cells distinguish the pancreas from the purely serous parotid."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract (esophagus, stomach, and small and large intestine)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder, patterns of hepatic injury"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System, hindgut and the anal canal"}]},"GI-3":{"unit":"GI-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01","title":"Gastrointestinal Organs and Glands","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the gross anatomy of the stomach and duodenum, the peritoneal relationships of the abdominal gut, the liver and biliary tree, the features that set the colon apart from the small intestine, and the three major salivary glands. Board questions rarely ask for a structure in isolation. They give a finding, such as bilious or nonbilious vomiting, jaundice with or without pancreatitis, flank bruising, right lower quadrant pain, or facial weakness with a parotid mass, and expect you to place the lesion. If you know where each organ sits, what lies next to it, and whether peritoneum covers it, you can explain those findings by location.","prerequisites":["Development of the foregut, midgut, and hindgut and gut rotation (GI-1)","Layers of the gut wall: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa or adventitia (GI-2)","Branches of the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery (GI-5)","Visceral versus somatic pain pathways","Cranial nerves VII and IX and the parasympathetic ganglia of the head"],"connections":[{"to":"The gut in the body cavities (GI-4)","how":"The peritoneal sacs, omenta, and ligaments described there are the same folds that make organs in this chapter intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal blood supply (GI-5)","how":"The major duodenal papilla marks the change from celiac to superior mesenteric supply, and the gastroduodenal artery behind the duodenal bulb explains bleeding from posterior ulcers."},{"to":"Gallbladder diseases (GI-13-D)","how":"The level at which a stone lodges in the biliary tree decides whether the patient has colic, cholecystitis, jaundice, cholangitis, or pancreatitis."},{"to":"Salivary gland disease (GI-13-A)","how":"Duct anatomy and the tight parotid capsule explain the pain of parotitis and why stones favor the submandibular gland."},{"to":"Pancreatic disease and neoplasms (GI-13-B, GI-13-K)","how":"The bile duct runs in or behind the pancreatic head, so a mass there causes obstructive jaundice."}],"terms":[{"term":"Intraperitoneal organ","def":"An organ almost completely covered by visceral peritoneum and suspended by a mesentery or ligament, which leaves it mobile."},{"term":"Secondarily retroperitoneal organ","def":"An organ that once had a mesentery but was pressed against the posterior body wall during development, so that its peritoneum fused with the parietal peritoneum and it became fixed."},{"term":"Duodenal bulb","def":"The dilated, mobile first 2 cm or so of the superior part of the duodenum, just beyond the pylorus."},{"term":"Major duodenal papilla","def":"The elevation on the posteromedial wall of the descending duodenum where the hepatopancreatic ampulla opens."},{"term":"Hepatopancreatic ampulla (of Vater)","def":"The short dilated channel formed where the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct unite before opening into the duodenum."},{"term":"Sphincter of Oddi","def":"The smooth muscle sphincter around the terminal bile duct, the terminal pancreatic duct, and the ampulla that controls flow into the duodenum."},{"term":"Porta hepatis","def":"The transverse fissure on the visceral surface of the liver where the portal vein and hepatic artery enter and the hepatic ducts and lymphatics leave."},{"term":"Bare area of the liver","def":"The part of the diaphragmatic surface of the liver that lies directly against the diaphragm without a peritoneal covering, bounded by the layers of the coronary ligament."},{"term":"Taeniae coli","def":"Three flat bands into which the outer longitudinal muscle of the cecum and colon is gathered."},{"term":"Haustra","def":"The sacculations of the colonic wall produced because the taeniae are shorter than the bowel they run along."},{"term":"McBurney point","def":"The surface point one third of the way along a line from the right anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus, marking the usual position of the base of the appendix."},{"term":"Parotid duct","def":"The duct of the parotid gland (Stensen duct), which crosses the masseter, pierces the buccinator, and opens opposite the upper second molar."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01-C01","obj":"GI-3.1","title":"Parts of the stomach and the four parts of the duodenum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The stomach is the dilated part of the foregut between the esophagus and the duodenum. Its regions are defined by position. The cardia surrounds the cardial orifice where the esophagus enters. The fundus is the dome that rises above the level of that orifice under the left hemidiaphragm, which is why swallowed air collects there in an upright patient. The body is the large central part, and the pyloric part follows it: the wide pyloric antrum, the narrower pyloric canal, and the pylorus, a thickened ring of circular smooth muscle that meters chyme into the duodenum. The short lesser curvature faces right and the long greater curvature faces left; the angular incisure, a notch on the lesser curvature, marks where body becomes antrum."},{"type":"p","text":"The regions differ in function as well as shape. Parietal cells, which secrete acid and intrinsic factor, and chief cells, which secrete pepsinogen, are concentrated in the fundus and body. Gastrin-secreting G cells lie in the antrum. This is why resection or atrophy of the body and fundus impairs acid and intrinsic factor secretion, while antral disease affects gastrin."},{"type":"p","text":"The duodenum is about 25 cm long and curves in a C around the head of the pancreas. Its parts are best learned by what each one touches, because questions turn on those relationships. The major duodenal papilla lies on the posteromedial wall of the descending (second) part. Just distal to it, the duodenum changes from foregut to midgut, and its arterial supply changes from celiac branches (superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries) to superior mesenteric branches (inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries). A minor papilla about 2 cm above the major one drains the accessory pancreatic duct."},{"type":"table","title":"The four parts of the duodenum","cols":["Part","Approximate length and level","Key relations","Clinical relevance"],"rows":[["First (superior)","About 5 cm, at L1","Bulb is mobile; gastroduodenal artery and common bile duct pass behind it","Most duodenal ulcers; posterior ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery"],["Second (descending)","About 7 to 10 cm, right of L1 to L3","Major and minor papillae; head of pancreas medially; right kidney behind","Site where bile and pancreatic juice enter; foregut to midgut junction"],["Third (horizontal)","About 6 to 8 cm, crosses L3","Superior mesenteric artery (SMA) and vein in front; aorta and inferior vena cava behind","Compression between SMA and aorta (SMA syndrome)"],["Fourth (ascending)","About 5 cm, rises to L2","Ends at the duodenojejunal flexure, held by the suspensory muscle (ligament of Treitz)","Landmark dividing upper from lower GI bleeding"]],"caption":"Lengths and vertebral levels are approximate and vary with body habitus and posture."},{"type":"p","text":"Location also explains the color of vomitus. Bile enters at the second part, so an obstruction proximal to the papilla, such as infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, produces nonbilious vomiting, while most duodenal atresias lie distal to the papilla and produce bilious vomiting. Ulcers favor the bulb because it receives acidic chyme from the pylorus before pancreatic bicarbonate arrives at the papilla. An anterior bulb ulcer tends to perforate into the peritoneal cavity, whereas a posterior ulcer tends to bleed from the gastroduodenal artery."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An infant vomits forcefully after every feed, and the vomitus contains no bile. Where along the stomach and duodenum must the obstruction lie?","explain":"Proximal to the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum, because bile only enters the lumen there; the pylorus is the classic site.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on bilious and nonbilious vomiting and the duodenum table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01-C02","obj":"GI-3.2","title":"Intraperitoneal and retroperitoneal organs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The peritoneum is a serous membrane: parietal peritoneum lines the abdominal wall, and visceral peritoneum covers organs. An intraperitoneal organ is almost completely wrapped in visceral peritoneum and hangs from a mesentery or peritoneal ligament, so it moves with posture and peristalsis. A retroperitoneal organ lies behind the parietal peritoneum, covered by it only on its anterior surface, and is fixed to the posterior body wall."},{"type":"p","text":"Retroperitoneal organs come in two kinds. Primarily retroperitoneal organs, such as the kidneys, ureters, suprarenal glands, aorta, and inferior vena cava, developed behind the peritoneum and never had a mesentery. Secondarily retroperitoneal organs began on a mesentery and were later pressed against the posterior wall as the gut rotated and grew. Their mesentery fused with the parietal peritoneum to form a fusion fascia."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a gut segment becomes secondarily retroperitoneal","steps":["Gut segment hangs on dorsal mesentery, fully intraperitoneal","Rotation and growth press it against the posterior wall","Posterior visceral peritoneum and mesentery fuse with parietal peritoneum","Fusion fascia forms behind the segment","Segment is fixed, covered by peritoneum only in front"],"caption":"Sequence that fixes duodenum parts 2 to 4, most of the pancreas, and the ascending and descending colon."},{"type":"table","title":"Peritoneal status of the abdominal gut and its glands","cols":["Organ or segment","Peritoneal status","Detail"],"rows":[["Stomach, jejunum, ileum","Intraperitoneal","Suspended by omenta and the mesentery proper"],["Duodenum, part 1","Partly intraperitoneal","Bulb (first 2 cm) is intraperitoneal and mobile; the distal 3 cm is retroperitoneal and fixed"],["Duodenum, parts 2 to 4","Secondarily retroperitoneal","Fixed around the pancreatic head"],["Pancreas","Secondarily retroperitoneal except the tail","Tail runs in the splenorenal ligament to the splenic hilum"],["Cecum and appendix","Intraperitoneal","Cecum often lacks a true mesentery but is largely covered; appendix has a mesoappendix"],["Ascending and descending colon","Secondarily retroperitoneal","Mesocolon fused to the posterior wall"],["Transverse and sigmoid colon","Intraperitoneal","Keep the transverse and sigmoid mesocolons"],["Rectum","Changes along its length","Upper third covered in front and on the sides, middle third in front only, lower third below the peritoneum"],["Liver and gallbladder","Intraperitoneal","Except the bare area of the liver and the gallbladder surface attached to the liver"],["Spleen","Intraperitoneal","Held by the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments"]],"caption":"Duodenum, pancreas, and rectum change status along their length."},{"type":"p","text":"The distinction has clinical consequences because the retroperitoneum is outside the peritoneal cavity. Blood or enzyme-rich fluid from a retroperitoneal organ tracks through retroperitoneal fat instead of spreading over the peritoneal surfaces, so peritoneal signs may be slight or delayed. Hemorrhagic pancreatitis can track to the flanks and produce flank bruising (Grey Turner sign). A perforation of a retroperitoneal duodenal segment, or a crush injury of the duodenum against the vertebral column in blunt trauma, can leak into the retroperitoneum without free air under the diaphragm, while an anterior perforation of the intraperitoneal bulb releases air into the peritoneal cavity. Mobility follows the same rule: intraperitoneal segments on long mesenteries, such as the sigmoid colon, can twist (volvulus), whereas fixed retroperitoneal segments cannot."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: where did the air go?","text":"Two patients have perforated duodenal ulcers. In the first, an upright chest film shows air under the right hemidiaphragm and the abdomen is rigid. In the second, there is no free air, but CT shows gas around the right kidney. Step 1: free subdiaphragmatic air means the leak entered the peritoneal cavity, so the ulcer was on the anterior wall of the intraperitoneal duodenal bulb. Step 2: gas around the kidney means the leak entered the retroperitoneum, so the perforation was in a retroperitoneal segment, the fixed distal first part or the second part. Step 3: the second patient has fewer peritoneal signs because the retroperitoneum lacks the parietal peritoneal surface that, when irritated, produces guarding and rigidity."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01-C03","obj":"GI-3.3","title":"The liver and the path of bile to the duodenum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The liver, the largest gland in the body (about 1.5 kg), has four anatomic lobes. The falciform ligament divides the right lobe from the left lobe on the diaphragmatic surface. On the visceral surface, the caudate lobe lies above the porta hepatis between the inferior vena cava and the fissure for the ligamentum venosum, and the quadrate lobe lies below the porta between the gallbladder fossa and the fissure for the ligamentum teres. Surgeons use a functional division instead: a plane from the gallbladder fossa to the inferior vena cava (Cantlie line) separates functional right and left livers, each with its own portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct branches. The quadrate lobe then belongs to the left liver, and the caudate lobe drains directly into the inferior vena cava."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver develops within the ventral mesentery, so its peritoneal folds (falciform, coronary, and triangular ligaments and the lesser omentum) are remnants of that mesentery. The two layers of the coronary ligament separate widely on the posterosuperior surface and leave the bare area, where liver tissue touches the diaphragm directly. The porta hepatis is the hilum of the liver. The portal vein and proper hepatic artery enter there and the right and left hepatic ducts leave, all carried in the free edge of the lesser omentum, the hepatoduodenal ligament. In that ligament the bile duct lies anterior and to the right, the hepatic artery anterior and to the left, and the portal vein posterior. The hepatic veins do not use the porta; they leave the posterior surface directly into the inferior vena cava."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of bile from hepatocyte to duodenum","steps":["Hepatocytes secrete bile into bile canaliculi","Canals of Hering drain into interlobular bile ducts in portal tracts","Right and left hepatic ducts leave at the porta hepatis","They unite as the common hepatic duct","Cystic duct joins to form the common bile duct","Bile duct passes behind duodenal part 1 and the pancreatic head","Joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla","Enters duodenal part 2 at the major papilla through the sphincter of Oddi"],"caption":"Between meals the sphincter is closed, so bile backs up the cystic duct into the gallbladder for storage and concentration."},{"type":"p","text":"The sphincter of Oddi includes separate muscle around the end of the bile duct, the end of the pancreatic duct, and the ampulla. Cholecystokinin (CCK), released by the duodenum when fat and protein arrive, contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter, so bile reaches the lumen when it is needed for fat digestion."},{"type":"p","text":"Because all bile funnels into one duct that shares an outlet with the pancreas, the level of an obstruction predicts its effects (table). A common bile duct block raises conjugated bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase (ALP), darkens the urine (conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and filtered), and makes stools pale because no bilirubin reaches the gut to form stercobilin. A tumor of the pancreatic head compresses the bile duct slowly, so the gallbladder distends without inflammation and may be palpable and painless (Courvoisier sign)."},{"type":"table","title":"Level of biliary obstruction and its consequences","cols":["Site of block","Bilirubin and ALP","Gallbladder","Pancreas"],"rows":[["Cystic duct","Normal","Distends; colic or cholecystitis","Unaffected"],["Common hepatic duct","Conjugated bilirubin and ALP rise","Not filled with new bile","Unaffected"],["Common bile duct above the ampulla","Conjugated bilirubin and ALP rise","May distend","Unaffected"],["Ampulla of Vater","Conjugated bilirubin and ALP rise","May distend","Pancreatic duct can be blocked: pancreatitis"]],"caption":"Each step downstream adds territory: gallbladder, liver, then pancreas."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A stone lodges in the cystic duct. Why does the patient have pain but no jaundice?","explain":"Bile from the liver still flows down the common hepatic and common bile ducts to the duodenum; only the gallbladder is cut off, and it contracts painfully against the stone.","check":"Compare with the obstruction table and the bile flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01-C04","obj":"GI-3.4","title":"Features of the colon and the position of the appendix","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both the small and large intestine have an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle, but in the cecum and colon the longitudinal layer is gathered into three flat bands about 1 cm wide, the taeniae coli (named taenia mesocolica, taenia omentalis, and taenia libera). Because the taeniae are shorter than the wall they run along, they pull it into a series of pouches, the haustra. Small fat-filled tags of peritoneum, the omental appendices (appendices epiploicae), hang from the colonic surface. None of these three features is found on the jejunum or ileum, so any one of them identifies colon at surgery or in a specimen."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the colon from the small intestine","cols":["Feature","Small intestine (jejunum and ileum)","Colon"],"rows":[["Outer longitudinal muscle","Continuous layer","Three taeniae coli"],["Wall contour","Smooth tube","Haustra (sacculations)"],["Serosal fat tags","Absent","Omental appendices"],["Mucosal folds","Plicae circulares with villi","No plicae circulares or villi; straight crypts rich in goblet cells"],["Folds on contrast imaging","Plicae cross the full width of the lumen","Haustral folds cross only part of the lumen"],["Position and caliber","Central, mobile, narrower","Frames the abdomen, wider, partly fixed"]],"caption":"Taeniae, haustra, and omental appendices are the gross features; the rest support the distinction."},{"type":"p","text":"The taeniae begin at the base of the appendix and run along the colon to the rectosigmoid junction, where they spread out into a continuous longitudinal layer again. This is why the rectum and the appendix lack taeniae and haustra. The wall between the taeniae has a thinner muscle coat, and where the vasa recta penetrate it the wall is weakest, so colonic diverticula (herniations of mucosa and submucosa without muscle) form at these points, most often in the sigmoid colon."},{"type":"p","text":"The vermiform appendix is a narrow blind tube, about 6 to 10 cm long, arising from the posteromedial wall of the cecum below the ileocecal junction. Its tip is variable: most often retrocecal, but it may lie in the pelvis, below the cecum, or beside the terminal ileum. Its base, however, is constant, because all three taeniae converge on it, and a surgeon who follows the taeniae down the cecum will reach it. The surface marking of the base is McBurney point, one third of the way along a line from the right anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus. The appendix is supplied by the appendicular artery, a branch of the ileocolic artery running in the mesoappendix. It is an end artery, so when obstruction and swelling compress it the wall becomes ischemic and can necrose and perforate."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why appendicitis pain moves","steps":["Lumen obstructed by fecalith or lymphoid hyperplasia","Distension stimulates visceral afferents entering the cord at about T10","Poorly localized periumbilical pain","Inflammation spreads through the wall to the serosa","Parietal peritoneum, with somatic innervation, is irritated","Sharp pain localizes to the right lower quadrant near McBurney point"],"caption":"Visceral pain is referred to the dermatome of the gut segment; somatic pain is felt at the site."},{"type":"p","text":"The variable tip changes the findings. A retrocecal appendix lies on the iliopsoas, so extending the right hip stretches the inflamed tissue and causes pain (psoas sign), while anterior tenderness may be mild. A pelvic appendix can lie on the obturator internus, so internal rotation of the flexed right hip causes pain (obturator sign), and tenderness may be felt mainly on rectal examination."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01-C05","obj":"GI-3.5","title":"The major salivary glands, their ducts, and the facial nerve in the parotid","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three paired major glands produce most of the roughly 1 to 1.5 L of saliva secreted daily; minor glands in the lips, cheeks, and palate add the rest. The glands differ in cell type: serous acini secrete a watery fluid rich in salivary amylase, and mucous acini secrete viscous mucins. The parotid is purely serous, the submandibular gland is mixed but mostly serous, and the sublingual gland is mixed but mostly mucous."},{"type":"table","title":"The major salivary glands","cols":["Gland","Location","Duct opening","Secretion","Secretomotor nerve"],"rows":[["Parotid","Over the masseter, in front of and below the ear","Parotid (Stensen) duct, opposite the upper second molar","Serous","CN IX via the otic ganglion"],["Submandibular","Submandibular triangle, around the posterior border of the mylohyoid","Submandibular (Wharton) duct, at the sublingual caruncle beside the lingual frenulum","Mixed, mostly serous","CN VII via chorda tympani and the submandibular ganglion"],["Sublingual","Floor of the mouth under the sublingual fold","Many small ducts along the sublingual fold","Mixed, mostly mucous","CN VII via chorda tympani and the submandibular ganglion"]],"caption":"The parotid is the largest gland and the sublingual the smallest."},{"type":"p","text":"The parotid duct crosses the masseter about a fingerbreadth below the zygomatic arch, then pierces the buccinator; pus appears at its opening in bacterial parotitis. The submandibular duct is long and runs upward from the gland to the floor of the mouth, and its saliva is more viscous and more alkaline, with more calcium; these features slow flow and favor precipitation, which is why about 80 to 90% of salivary stones form in this gland. The lingual nerve loops under the submandibular duct, so it is at risk when the duct is opened."},{"type":"p","text":"The facial nerve (CN VII) leaves the skull through the stylomastoid foramen, enters the posterior surface of the parotid, and divides inside the gland into a plexus that gives five terminal branches: temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical. These branches emerge from the anterior border of the gland and fan out to the muscles of facial expression. The nerve only passes through; it does not supply the parotid, whose secretomotor fibers come from CN IX. From superficial to deep, the facial nerve plexus, the retromandibular vein, and the external carotid artery all lie within the gland."},{"type":"flow","title":"Secretomotor pathway to the parotid gland","steps":["Inferior salivatory nucleus in the medulla","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) and its tympanic branch","Lesser petrosal nerve","Synapse in the otic ganglion","Postganglionic fibers ride the auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Parotid acini secrete serous saliva"],"caption":"Submandibular and sublingual glands use CN VII: chorda tympani, lingual nerve, submandibular ganglion."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the nerve runs through the gland, parotid disease can reach it. Most salivary tumors arise in the parotid, and most are benign; the commonest, pleomorphic adenoma, pushes nerve branches aside. A malignant tumor such as mucoepidermoid carcinoma can infiltrate the nerve, so facial weakness with a parotid mass suggests malignancy. Damage within the gland, from tumor or surgery, gives a lower motor neuron palsy that includes the forehead. A late complication of parotidectomy is gustatory sweating (Frey syndrome): cut parasympathetic fibers of the auriculotemporal nerve regrow into the sweat glands of the overlying skin, so eating causes local sweating and flushing. The gland is also enclosed in a tight fascial sheath from the investing layer of deep cervical fascia, so inflammation such as mumps raises pressure and causes pain that worsens with chewing."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a firm parotid mass cannot wrinkle the forehead, close the eye tightly, or raise the mouth corner on that side. Upper or lower motor neuron pattern, and why is the mass concerning?","explain":"Lower motor neuron: the nerve is damaged in the gland, distal to the brainstem, so every branch including the temporal is affected. Benign tumors usually displace the nerve, so paralysis suggests an invading malignancy.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on parotid tumors."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains most of this chapter's anatomy. The stomach and the liver develop in the dorsal and ventral mesenteries, and gut rotation presses the duodenum, pancreas, and ascending and descending colon against the posterior wall, where their mesenteries fuse. That single event decides which organs are mobile, which bleed into the retroperitoneum, and which can twist. The major papilla sits at the foregut to midgut junction, so it also marks the change in arterial supply (celiac to superior mesenteric) and the boundary between nonbilious and bilious vomiting. The taeniae and haustra reflect how the colonic muscle is arranged, and the points where vessels pierce that muscle are where diverticula form."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and physiology then work through location. A gallstone causes colic, jaundice, or pancreatitis according to the duct it blocks, and the effects follow bile and bilirubin physiology: conjugated bilirubin in the urine, no stercobilin in the stool. Pain from the appendix or gallbladder changes character when inflammation reaches somatically innervated parietal peritoneum. In the head, the facial nerve's course through the parotid and the separate CN IX and CN VII secretomotor pathways explain why a parotid tumor can cause facial palsy even though the facial nerve does not supply the gland, and why mumps causes a painful, tense gland."}],"summary":["The stomach has cardia, fundus, body, and pyloric part (antrum, canal, pylorus); acid and intrinsic factor come from the fundus and body, gastrin from the antrum.","The duodenum has four parts: the ulcer-prone bulb, the descending part with the major papilla, the horizontal part between the superior mesenteric artery and aorta, and the ascending part ending at the suspensory ligament.","Obstruction proximal to the major papilla gives nonbilious vomiting; obstruction distal to it gives bilious vomiting.","Secondarily retroperitoneal organs (duodenum parts 2 to 4, pancreas except the tail, ascending and descending colon) lost their mesenteries by fusion; the rectum is covered progressively less toward its lower third.","Retroperitoneal bleeding or perforation tracks behind the peritoneum, giving flank signs and fewer peritoneal signs, without free air under the diaphragm.","The liver has right, left, caudate, and quadrate anatomic lobes; the porta hepatis transmits the portal vein, hepatic artery, and hepatic ducts, while the hepatic veins drain directly to the inferior vena cava.","Bile flows from the hepatic ducts to the common hepatic duct, joins the cystic duct as the common bile duct, and meets the pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater under the sphincter of Oddi.","Taeniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices identify the colon; the taeniae converge at the constant base of the appendix, marked at McBurney point.","The parotid duct opens opposite the upper second molar; the submandibular duct opens beside the lingual frenulum and is the usual site of stones.","The facial nerve divides into its five branches inside the parotid without supplying it, so parotid malignancy or surgery can cause lower motor neuron facial palsy."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen: peritoneum and peritoneal cavity, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, liver, biliary ducts and gallbladder"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head: parotid region and oral region (salivary glands)"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System: mesenteries, duodenum, liver and gallbladder"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Digestive Tract; Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract (salivary and biliary secretion)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck (salivary gland tumors); Liver and Gallbladder"}]},"GI-4":{"unit":"GI-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01","title":"The Gut in the Body Cavities","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter places the gut within the body cavities: the course of the esophagus through the neck and mediastinum, the nerve supply of the tongue, the peritoneal sacs and the ligaments that carry vessels to the upper abdominal organs, the inguinal region where hernias form, and the dependent spaces where fluid collects. Board questions here usually give a finding, such as a lodged pill, a deviated tongue, a groin bulge, or fluid on ultrasound, and ask which structure or space is involved. Most of this anatomy follows from development: rotation of the stomach, the pharyngeal arch origins of the tongue, and descent of the gonads explain the relations you need to remember.","prerequisites":["Foregut, midgut, and hindgut and the rotation of the stomach (GI-1)","Pharyngeal arches and the cranial nerve of each arch","Layers of the anterior abdominal wall, from skin to parietal peritoneum","Gross anatomy of the stomach, duodenum, liver, spleen, and pancreas (GI-3)","Branches of the celiac trunk and the tributaries of the portal vein"],"connections":[{"to":"Gut development (GI-1)","how":"Gastric rotation creates the lesser sac and turns the vagal trunks, and the liver and spleen take their ligaments from the ventral and dorsal mesenteries."},{"to":"Gut blood supply and portal circulation (GI-5)","how":"The lower esophagus, the falciform ligament, and the short gastric veins are sites where portal and systemic veins connect and enlarge in portal hypertension."},{"to":"Motility disorders (GI-13-F)","how":"Zenker diverticulum and dysphagia from extrinsic compression arise at the narrowings and relations described for the esophagus."},{"to":"Peritoneal disease (GI-13-I)","how":"Ascites, peritonitis, and abscesses spread along the gutters and pool in the dependent recesses described here."},{"to":"Neuroanatomy of the cranial nerves","how":"Lesions of CN V3, VII, IX, X, and XII each leave a distinct pattern of tongue sensation, taste, and movement."}],"terms":[{"term":"Posterior mediastinum","def":"The part of the thoracic midline space behind the pericardium, containing the esophagus, descending aorta, azygos veins, and thoracic duct."},{"term":"Upper esophageal sphincter","def":"The cricopharyngeal part of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor, a band of skeletal muscle that keeps the top of the esophagus closed between swallows."},{"term":"Chorda tympani","def":"A branch of the facial nerve that carries taste from the anterior two thirds of the tongue and parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular ganglion."},{"term":"Peritoneal ligament","def":"A double fold of peritoneum connecting an organ to another organ or to the abdominal wall, carrying vessels and nerves between its layers."},{"term":"Lesser sac (omental bursa)","def":"The part of the peritoneal cavity behind the stomach and lesser omentum, opening into the greater sac only through the omental foramen."},{"term":"Omental (epiploic) foramen","def":"The opening between the lesser and greater sacs, bounded in front by the hepatoduodenal ligament and behind by the inferior vena cava."},{"term":"Portal triad","def":"The hepatic portal vein, proper hepatic artery, and common bile duct, carried together in the hepatoduodenal ligament."},{"term":"Processus vaginalis","def":"A fetal pouch of peritoneum that precedes the testis through the abdominal wall and normally closes after descent."},{"term":"Inguinal (Hesselbach) triangle","def":"The area of the lower anterior abdominal wall bounded by the inferior epigastric vessels, the lateral edge of rectus abdominis, and the inguinal ligament."},{"term":"Paracolic gutter","def":"A peritoneal channel lateral to the ascending or descending colon that connects the upper abdomen with the pelvis."},{"term":"Dependent recess","def":"The lowest part of a body cavity for a given posture, where free fluid collects under gravity."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C01","obj":"GI-4.1","title":"Course and narrowings of the esophagus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The esophagus is a muscular tube about 25 cm long that carries a bolus from the pharynx to the stomach. It begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage, level with C6, where the cricopharyngeal part of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor forms the upper esophageal sphincter. Its muscle changes from skeletal in roughly the upper third to smooth in the lower third, so diseases of skeletal muscle disturb the start of swallowing while loss of myenteric neurons disturbs the lower esophagus."},{"type":"p","text":"In the neck the esophagus lies behind the trachea and in front of the vertebral column, slightly to the left. It enters the superior mediastinum through the thoracic inlet, still between the trachea and the vertebrae, and then descends in the posterior mediastinum behind the left main bronchus, the pericardium, and the left atrium. The descending aorta lies at first to its left and then behind it, as the esophagus moves forward and to the left to reach the esophageal hiatus, formed by fibers of the right crus of the diaphragm at T10. A short abdominal segment joins the cardia of the stomach at about T11."},{"type":"table","title":"The three esophageal narrowings","cols":["Narrowing","Level","Distance from the incisor teeth","What narrows the lumen","Clinical relevance"],"rows":[["Cervical (pharyngoesophageal junction)","C6","About 15 cm","Tone of the cricopharyngeus","Coins and food lodge here; Zenker diverticulum forms just above"],["Thoracic (bronchoaortic)","About T4 to T5","About 22 to 23 cm (arch) and 27 to 28 cm (bronchus)","Aortic arch, then left main bronchus, pressing from outside","Tablets stall: pill esophagitis"],["Diaphragmatic","T10","About 40 cm","Hiatus in the right crus and lower sphincter tone","Food impaction; hiatal hernia passes here"]],"caption":"Endoscopists report lesion position by distance from the incisors."},{"type":"p","text":"At each narrowing a muscle ring or a firm neighboring structure keeps the lumen from widening, so a slowly moving bolus, coin, or tablet tends to stop there. A tablet held against the mucosa dissolves in place and releases a concentrated irritant solution that ulcerates the wall (pill esophagitis), classically at the aortic arch when a pill is taken with little water at bedtime. The same relations explain extrinsic compression: an enlarged left atrium, as in mitral stenosis, indents the anterior wall of the middle esophagus on a barium swallow and can cause dysphagia, as can an aortic aneurysm or enlarged subcarinal lymph nodes."},{"type":"p","text":"Just above the cricopharyngeus, the posterior pharyngeal wall has a weak area between the thyropharyngeal and cricopharyngeal parts of the inferior constrictor (Killian triangle). When the cricopharyngeus does not relax fully, swallowing pressure pushes mucosa and submucosa out through this gap as a Zenker diverticulum, a false diverticulum that traps food and causes regurgitation and halitosis. At the lower end, a sudden pressure rise against a closed sphincter during forceful vomiting can tear the full wall (Boerhaave syndrome), usually on the left posterolateral side just above the diaphragm."},{"type":"p","text":"The two vagus nerves form the esophageal plexus on the lower esophagus and leave it as two trunks through the hiatus. Because the stomach rotates during development, the left vagus becomes the anterior trunk and the right vagus the posterior trunk. The lower esophagus is also a portosystemic junction: esophageal tributaries of the left gastric vein (portal) connect with veins draining to the azygos system (systemic), so portal hypertension dilates these submucosal veins into varices (GI-5)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 70-year-old with mitral stenosis has difficulty swallowing solid food. Where would a barium swallow show the esophagus indented, and why there?","explain":"On the anterior wall of the middle thoracic esophagus, below the aortic and bronchial impressions, because the left atrium (in the middle mediastinum) lies directly in front of the esophagus, which descends in the posterior mediastinum.","check":"The course paragraph and the narrowings table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C02","obj":"GI-4.2","title":"Innervation of the tongue and lesion patterns","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The nerve supply of the tongue follows its embryology. The mucosa of the anterior two thirds develops from the first pharyngeal arch, so its general sensation (touch, pain, and temperature) is carried by the nerve of that arch, the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3), through its lingual branch. The posterior third develops mainly from the third arch, so the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) carries both general sensation and taste there, including the vallate papillae just in front of the V-shaped sulcus terminalis. A small area at the root near the epiglottis, from the fourth arch, is supplied by the vagus (CN X) through the internal laryngeal nerve."},{"type":"p","text":"Taste from the anterior two thirds does not travel centrally with V3. It is carried by the chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve (CN VII) that leaves the facial canal, crosses the middle ear, and joins the lingual nerve in the infratemporal fossa. Its taste fibers have their cell bodies in the geniculate ganglion and end, like the taste fibers of CN IX and CN X, in the nucleus of the solitary tract. The chorda tympani also carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers, which leave the lingual nerve to synapse in the submandibular ganglion that supplies the submandibular and sublingual glands."},{"type":"p","text":"The tongue muscles develop from occipital somites and bring their own nerve, the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII), which supplies all intrinsic muscles and the extrinsic genioglossus, hyoglossus, and styloglossus. The exception is palatoglossus, which develops with the muscles of the soft palate and is supplied by the vagus through the pharyngeal plexus. Each genioglossus pulls the tongue forward and toward the opposite side, so when both contract equally the tongue protrudes in the midline."},{"type":"table","title":"Nerves of the tongue and the deficit from a lesion","cols":["Nerve","Supplies","Deficit from a lesion"],"rows":[["Lingual nerve (CN V3)","Touch, pain, temperature, anterior two thirds","Numbness of the anterior two thirds; taste also lost if injured beyond the chorda tympani junction"],["Chorda tympani (CN VII)","Taste, anterior two thirds; secretomotor to submandibular and sublingual glands","Loss of taste on the anterior two thirds; less saliva from those glands"],["Glossopharyngeal (CN IX)","Sensation and taste, posterior third","Loss of both on the posterior third; no gag when that side is touched (afferent limb)"],["Vagus (CN X)","Palatoglossus; sensation and taste at the root","Palate droops on that side, uvula deviates to the other side"],["Hypoglossal (CN XII)","All other tongue muscles","Tongue deviates toward the lesion, with atrophy and fasciculations"]],"caption":"Deficits are on the side of the lesion. Sensation and taste of the anterior two thirds travel in different nerves; the posterior third uses CN IX for both."},{"type":"p","text":"Two motor patterns are tested most. In a lower motor neuron lesion of CN XII, the genioglossus on the injured side is paralyzed, so the healthy muscle pushes the protruded tongue toward the weak side, and denervation produces atrophy and fasciculations on that side. In an upper motor neuron lesion, such as a cortical stroke, corticobulbar input to genioglossus is mainly crossed, so the tongue deviates away from the side of the brain lesion, without atrophy."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a lesion from the tongue examination","text":"After removal of a lower right third molar, a patient has lost touch and taste on the right anterior two thirds of the tongue, with normal posterior third sensation, gag reflex, and protrusion. Touch loss localizes to the lingual nerve; taste loss as well places the injury beyond the point where the chorda tympani joins it, which fits the lingual nerve lying against the inner mandible beside the third molar. A facial nerve lesion in the facial canal above the chorda tympani instead removes taste but keeps touch, with facial weakness. A lesion at the stylomastoid foramen spares taste, because the chorda tympani has already left."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C03","obj":"GI-4.3","title":"Greater and lesser sacs, omenta, and the omental foramen","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Parietal peritoneum lines the abdominal wall and visceral peritoneum covers the organs, with the peritoneal cavity, a potential space holding a thin film of fluid, between them. Double folds connecting an organ to the wall or to another organ are called mesenteries, ligaments, or omenta, and they carry the organ's vessels, nerves, and lymphatics. The cavity has a large greater sac and a small lesser sac (omental bursa)."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesser sac is a product of gastric rotation. The stomach originally hangs between a dorsal mesogastrium and a ventral mesogastrium. As it rotates about 90 degrees clockwise around its long axis (viewed from above), its left side comes to face forward and the dorsal mesogastrium is swept to the left, opening a recess behind the stomach. The liver grows into the ventral mesogastrium, which becomes the falciform ligament in front of the liver and the lesser omentum between the liver and the stomach. The dorsal mesogastrium elongates into the greater omentum."},{"type":"flow","title":"How the lesser sac forms","steps":["Stomach hangs between dorsal and ventral mesogastria","Stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise about its long axis","Dorsal mesogastrium swings left, opening a space behind","Space persists as the lesser sac, open only at the omental foramen"],"caption":"The same rotation brings the left vagus to the front of the stomach."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesser omentum runs from the lesser curvature and the first 2 cm of the duodenum to the liver. Its larger part, the hepatogastric ligament, attaches to the fissure for the ligamentum venosum, and its thick right free edge, the hepatoduodenal ligament, runs to the porta hepatis. The greater omentum hangs like an apron from the greater curvature, folds back on itself, and attaches to the transverse colon; its layers usually fuse, so the lesser sac extends only a short way into it. The omentum is mobile and adheres to inflamed organs, which helps wall off local infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Boundaries of the lesser sac and the omental foramen","cols":["Boundary","Lesser sac","Omental (epiploic) foramen"],"rows":[["Anterior","Lesser omentum, posterior stomach, gastrocolic ligament","Hepatoduodenal ligament (portal triad)"],["Posterior","Pancreas, left kidney and adrenal, transverse mesocolon","Inferior vena cava, right crus"],["Superior","Caudate lobe of the liver, diaphragm","Caudate lobe of the liver"],["Inferior","Recess between greater omental layers, often fused","First part of the duodenum"],["Left and right","Left: spleen and its ligaments; right: omental foramen","Opens between the two sacs"]],"caption":"The omental foramen is the only natural communication between the sacs."},{"type":"p","text":"The hepatoduodenal ligament carries the portal triad: the common bile duct in front on the right, the proper hepatic artery in front on the left, and the hepatic portal vein behind both. Because the ligament forms the front wall of the omental foramen and the inferior vena cava its back wall, compressing the free edge (Pringle maneuver) stops hepatic arterial and portal inflow to the liver, while bleeding from the hepatic veins or vena cava, which lie outside the ligament, continues."},{"type":"p","text":"These relations decide where fluid goes when the stomach is breached. A perforated ulcer on the posterior wall of the stomach releases its contents into the lesser sac, where they may stay contained or escape through the omental foramen into the right subhepatic space, whereas an anterior perforation spills directly into the greater sac and releases free air under the diaphragm. Fluid from acute pancreatitis also gathers in the lesser sac, because the pancreas forms its posterior wall, and a pseudocyst there can push the stomach forward."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An ulcer perforates through the posterior wall of the stomach. Why may early signs of spreading peritonitis be limited, and where can the fluid leave the space it first enters?","explain":"Gastric contents enter the lesser sac, which is closed except at the omental foramen, so fluid can only escape to the right through the foramen into the right subhepatic space and hepatorenal recess.","check":"The boundaries table and the paragraph on perforation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C04","obj":"GI-4.4","title":"Peritoneal ligaments and the vessels they carry","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each peritoneal ligament carries vessels between its two layers, so knowing its contents predicts which artery an ulcer erodes, which veins enlarge in portal hypertension, and which structure is at risk when an organ is freed. The ventral mesentery gives rise to the falciform ligament and the lesser omentum, which reach the liver. The dorsal mesogastrium gives rise to the gastrosplenic, splenorenal, and gastrocolic ligaments, which reach the greater curvature, the spleen, and the transverse colon."},{"type":"table","title":"Peritoneal ligaments of the upper abdomen","cols":["Ligament","Origin","Connects","Contents","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Falciform","Ventral mesentery","Liver to anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm","Ligamentum teres hepatis in its free edge; paraumbilical veins","Paraumbilical veins enlarge in portal hypertension; separates right and left subphrenic spaces"],["Hepatoduodenal","Ventral mesentery (lesser omentum)","Liver to first part of the duodenum","Portal vein, proper hepatic artery, common bile duct","Front wall of the omental foramen"],["Gastrohepatic (hepatogastric)","Ventral mesentery (lesser omentum)","Liver to lesser curvature","Left and right gastric arteries and veins","Lesser curvature ulcers can erode the left gastric artery; left gastric vein feeds esophageal varices"],["Gastrocolic","Dorsal mesogastrium (greater omentum)","Greater curvature to transverse colon","Right and left gastroepiploic (gastro-omental) vessels","Front wall of the lower lesser sac"],["Gastrosplenic","Dorsal mesogastrium","Greater curvature to splenic hilum","Short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels","Route for fundal varices when the splenic vein is blocked"],["Splenorenal","Dorsal mesogastrium","Splenic hilum to posterior wall over the left kidney","Splenic artery and vein, tail of the pancreas","Pancreatic tail reaches the splenic hilum and is at risk when the spleen is freed"]],"caption":"Ligaments from the ventral mesentery reach the liver; those from the dorsal mesogastrium reach the greater curvature, spleen, and colon."},{"type":"p","text":"The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm and marks the surface boundary between the anatomical right and left lobes. Its free lower edge contains the ligamentum teres hepatis, the fibrous remnant of the left umbilical vein, which carried oxygenated placental blood to the fetal liver and closes after birth. Small paraumbilical veins run beside it, linking the left branch of the portal vein with veins of the abdominal wall around the umbilicus. When portal pressure rises, these portosystemic channels enlarge and radiate from the umbilicus as caput medusae (GI-5)."},{"type":"p","text":"The gastrohepatic ligament carries the left gastric artery, a branch of the celiac trunk, and the right gastric artery, usually from the proper hepatic artery, which join along the lesser curvature, with companion veins that drain into the portal system. The left gastric vein also receives esophageal tributaries, so it is the main portal source of esophageal varices. A penetrating ulcer on the lesser curvature can erode the left gastric artery, whereas an ulcer in the posterior wall of the duodenal bulb erodes the gastroduodenal artery, which runs behind the duodenum outside these ligaments."},{"type":"p","text":"The spleen develops within the dorsal mesogastrium, so it stays tied to the stomach by the gastrosplenic ligament and to the posterior wall by the splenorenal ligament. The gastrosplenic ligament carries the short gastric vessels to the fundus and the left gastroepiploic vessels to the greater curvature. The splenorenal ligament carries the splenic artery and vein to the hilum together with the tail of the pancreas, the one part of the pancreas that is not retroperitoneal. Because the splenic vein runs directly behind the body and tail of the pancreas, pancreatitis or pancreatic cancer can cause it to thrombose, and splenic blood must then return through the short gastric veins."},{"type":"flow","title":"Splenic vein thrombosis and fundal varices","steps":["Pancreatitis or tumor involves the pancreatic body and tail","The splenic vein behind the pancreas thromboses","Splenic blood diverts into the short gastric veins","Veins in the gastric fundus dilate","Isolated fundal varices with normal portal vein pressure"],"caption":"This left-sided (sinistral) portal hypertension is local: the liver and portal vein are normal, so esophageal varices are usually absent."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C05","obj":"GI-4.5","title":"Inguinal canal, Hesselbach triangle, and groin hernias","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The inguinal canal is an oblique passage about 4 cm long running inferomedially just above the medial half of the inguinal ligament. It carries the spermatic cord in males or the round ligament of the uterus in females, plus the ilioinguinal nerve, which enters partway along rather than through the deep ring. The canal exists because the testis, preceded by the processus vaginalis, descended through the wall and took a covering from each layer: external spermatic fascia (external oblique), cremaster (internal oblique), and internal spermatic fascia (transversalis fascia)."},{"type":"p","text":"The deep (internal) ring is an opening in the transversalis fascia just above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. The superficial (external) ring is a split in the external oblique aponeurosis just above and lateral to the pubic tubercle. Because the rings are offset, each weak point is backed by a strong wall, and straining contracts the arching roof muscles to flatten the canal like a valve."},{"type":"table","title":"Walls of the inguinal canal","cols":["Wall","Formed by","Reinforcement"],"rows":[["Anterior","External oblique aponeurosis","Internal oblique laterally, in front of the deep ring"],["Posterior","Transversalis fascia","Conjoint tendon (falx inguinalis) medially, behind the superficial ring"],["Roof","Arching fibers of internal oblique and transversus abdominis","Contract and lower the roof during straining"],["Floor","Inguinal ligament, the rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis","Lacunar ligament medially"]],"caption":"Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels the posterior wall is only transversalis fascia and a variable conjoint tendon."},{"type":"p","text":"Viewed from inside, the inferior epigastric vessels raise the lateral umbilical fold. The inguinal (Hesselbach) triangle lies medial to them, bounded laterally by these vessels, medially by the lateral edge of rectus abdominis, and inferiorly by the inguinal ligament. The fossa lateral to the fold leads to the deep ring."},{"type":"p","text":"Each hernia type follows a weak point. An indirect hernia follows the congenital route of testicular descent: it enters the deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels through a processus vaginalis that did not close, travels inside all three cord coverings, and can reach the scrotum or labium majus. A direct hernia is acquired: transversalis fascia in the Hesselbach triangle weakens with age and repeated straining, and viscera bulge straight through medial to the vessels, outside the inner cord coverings. A femoral hernia passes below the inguinal ligament through the femoral ring, medial to the femoral vein. That ring is small and bounded by unyielding structures (inguinal ligament in front, pectineal ligament behind, lacunar ligament medially, femoral vein laterally), so femoral hernias are the most likely to incarcerate and strangulate."},{"type":"table","title":"Groin hernias compared","cols":["Feature","Indirect inguinal","Direct inguinal","Femoral"],"rows":[["Entry","Deep inguinal ring","Hesselbach triangle in the posterior wall","Femoral ring"],["Relation to inferior epigastric vessels","Lateral","Medial","Below the inguinal ligament, medial to the femoral vein"],["Relation to pubic tubercle","Above and medial","Above and medial","Below and lateral"],["Basis","Patent processus vaginalis","Weak transversalis fascia","Small rigid femoral ring"],["Typical patient","Infants and young males; most common overall","Older men","Women more than men, though indirect is still the most common hernia in women"],["Strangulation risk","Moderate","Low (wide neck)","Highest"]],"caption":"The inferior epigastric vessels separate the inguinal types; the inguinal ligament separates inguinal from femoral."},{"type":"example","title":"Naming a hernia from its landmarks","text":"A 68-year-old man with a chronic cough has a groin bulge above the inguinal ligament and medial to the pubic tubercle that does not enter the scrotum; from inside the abdomen, the defect lies medial to the lateral umbilical fold. Above the ligament and medial to the tubercle means inguinal, not femoral. The lateral umbilical fold is raised by the inferior epigastric vessels, so a defect medial to it lies in the Hesselbach triangle: a direct hernia, fitting chronic coughing against fascia weakened by age. A defect lateral to the fold would be at the deep ring, an indirect hernia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C06","obj":"GI-4.6","title":"Dependent peritoneal spaces and abscess formation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Free fluid in the peritoneal cavity, whether ascites, blood, pus, or bile, follows the peritoneal attachments and gravity and collects in the lowest recess for the patient's position. The transverse mesocolon divides the cavity into supracolic and infracolic compartments, and the root of the small bowel mesentery divides the infracolic compartment into right and left spaces. The paracolic gutters, lateral to the ascending and descending colon, connect the upper abdomen with the pelvis."},{"type":"p","text":"In the supracolic compartment, the falciform ligament separates right and left subphrenic spaces between the diaphragm and the liver. Below the right lobe lies the right subhepatic space, whose posterior extension between the liver and right kidney is the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch). When the patient is supine it is the most dependent part of the upper abdomen, and it opens into the right subphrenic space above, the right paracolic gutter below, and the lesser sac through the omental foramen. On the left, the phrenicocolic ligament, from the splenic flexure to the diaphragm, partly closes the top of the left gutter, so fluid moves less freely between the left upper quadrant and the pelvis."},{"type":"p","text":"When the patient is upright, the lowest part of the cavity is in the pelvis: the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) between rectum and uterus, and the rectovesical pouch between rectum and bladder in men. Blood from a ruptured ectopic pregnancy and pus from appendicitis, diverticulitis, or pelvic inflammatory disease gather here. The rectouterine pouch lies just behind the posterior vaginal fornix and the rectovesical pouch in front of the rectum, so a pelvic collection can be felt on vaginal or rectal examination."},{"type":"table","title":"Where peritoneal fluid collects","cols":["Space","Location","When it fills","Typical collection"],"rows":[["Hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch)","Between right lobe of liver and right kidney","Supine","Blood after trauma; fluid from the lesser sac; abscess"],["Right subphrenic","Between diaphragm and right lobe","Fluid drawn up the right gutter","Abscess after perforation, appendicitis, or surgery"],["Left subphrenic","Between diaphragm and left lobe, stomach, spleen","Less often; phrenicocolic ligament limits inflow","Abscess after gastric, splenic, or colonic perforation or surgery"],["Rectouterine pouch","Between rectum and uterus","Upright","Ectopic pregnancy blood; pelvic abscess"],["Rectovesical pouch","Between rectum and bladder","Upright","Pelvic abscess"]],"caption":"Supine collections favor the hepatorenal recess; upright ones favor the pelvic pouches."},{"type":"p","text":"Fluid does not only fall. Each breath lowers pressure beneath the diaphragm, and lymphatic openings in the diaphragmatic peritoneum absorb fluid, so peritoneal fluid is drawn upward toward the subphrenic spaces, mainly along the open right paracolic gutter. This is why subphrenic abscesses follow perforated ulcers, appendicitis, and abdominal surgery, why they are more common on the right, and why pelvic infection can reach the liver capsule (perihepatitis in Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome). The central diaphragmatic peritoneum is supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5), so its irritation is referred to the shoulder tip, whose skin is supplied by the supraclavicular nerves (C3, C4); the peripheral diaphragm is supplied by lower intercostal nerves, so irritation there is felt over the lower chest and upper abdominal wall."},{"type":"flow","title":"From a ruptured appendix to a right subphrenic abscess","steps":["Appendix perforates in the right iliac fossa","Pus enters the right paracolic gutter","Breathing draws fluid up the open right gutter","Pus reaches the hepatorenal recess and right subphrenic space","Abscess irritates phrenic-supplied diaphragmatic peritoneum: shoulder-tip pain"],"caption":"In an upright patient, gravity carries pus down the same gutter into the pelvis instead."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman with pelvic inflammatory disease develops right upper quadrant pain that worsens on deep breathing. How did the infection reach the liver, and why the right side?","explain":"Infected fluid travels up the right paracolic gutter, which opens freely into the hepatorenal recess and right subphrenic space, drawn upward by respiratory pressure changes; the phrenicocolic ligament limits this spread on the left.","check":"The paragraph on upward spread and the table of spaces."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains nearly every relation in this chapter. Rotation of the stomach creates the lesser sac and turns the vagal trunks, the liver growing into the ventral mesentery gives the falciform ligament and the route of the portal triad, the spleen forming in the dorsal mesogastrium gives its ligaments and its tie to the pancreatic tail, the arch origins of the tongue explain its split sensory supply, and descent of the testis explains the inguinal canal and the indirect hernia. When an anatomical rule seems arbitrary, its developmental origin usually makes sense of it."},{"type":"p","text":"The same anatomy routes disease. Portal hypertension opens collaterals in the lower esophagus, the falciform ligament, and the short gastric veins; perforation and pancreatitis fill the lesser sac; and infections from the appendix, colon, or fallopian tubes, whether mixed gut flora or Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis, collect in the dependent spaces. The limits are real: vessel origins, the extent of the lesser sac, and fusion of the omental layers vary between people, and fluid distribution also depends on volume, adhesions, and how long the patient has been in one position, so imaging findings are read together with the clinical picture."}],"summary":["The esophagus runs from C6 behind the trachea and left atrium to the hiatus at T10, with narrowings at the cricopharyngeus, the aortic arch and left main bronchus, and the diaphragm.","Swallowed objects and tablets stall at the narrowings; an enlarged left atrium indents the middle esophagus from the front.","Anterior two thirds of the tongue: touch by the lingual nerve (V3), taste by the chorda tympani (VII); posterior third: touch and taste by CN IX.","CN XII moves the tongue except palatoglossus (CN X); a lower motor neuron CN XII lesion makes the tongue deviate toward the lesion.","Gastric rotation forms the lesser sac behind the stomach, which opens to the greater sac only through the omental foramen.","The hepatoduodenal ligament forms the front of the omental foramen and carries the bile duct (front right), hepatic artery (front left), and portal vein (behind); the IVC lies behind the foramen.","Falciform carries the ligamentum teres, gastrohepatic the gastric arteries, gastrosplenic the short gastric vessels, and splenorenal the splenic vessels and pancreatic tail.","Indirect hernias enter the deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels; direct hernias cross the Hesselbach triangle medial to them; femoral hernias pass below the inguinal ligament and strangulate most often.","Fluid pools in the hepatorenal recess when supine and in the rectouterine or rectovesical pouch when upright, and breathing draws it up the right gutter to the subphrenic spaces.","Subphrenic irritation of the central diaphragm is referred to the shoulder tip through the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5)."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, posterior mediastinum and the esophagus"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, the inguinal region and the peritoneum and peritoneal cavity"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, oral region and the tongue"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive system (mesenteries and rotation of the stomach) and head and neck (development of the tongue)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Gastrointestinal Tract"}]},"GI-5":{"unit":"GI-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01","title":"Gastrointestinal Blood Supply","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows blood into, through, and out of the gut. It covers the three unpaired aortic branches and their territories, the watershed zones of the colon, the portal venous system and the escape routes portal blood takes when the liver obstructs it, the two territories of the anal canal, and the dual inflow of the liver. Board questions usually give a bleeding ulcer, a hypotensive episode, a sign on the abdominal wall, or an enlarged groin node and ask for the vessel, segment, or drainage path responsible. If you know which embryonic gut segment each organ comes from and that portal blood must cross a second capillary bed in the liver, most of these questions can be reasoned out.","prerequisites":["Foregut, midgut, and hindgut divisions and their derivatives (GI-1)","Peritoneal relations: hepatoduodenal ligament, lesser sac, and retroperitoneal organs (GI-4)","Liver lobule, acinus, and sinusoid histology (GI-2)","Pressure, flow, and resistance relationships and Starling forces in capillaries","Visceral versus somatic afferent innervation"],"connections":[{"to":"Hepatic diseases (GI-13-C)","how":"Cirrhosis is the usual cause of portal hypertension, and its complications (varices, splenomegaly, ascites, encephalopathy) follow from the venous anatomy in this chapter."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal vascular diseases (GI-13-J)","how":"Acute and chronic mesenteric ischemia, ischemic colitis, and variceal bleeding are applications of the arterial territories and anastomoses taught here."},{"to":"Inflammatory gastrointestinal diseases (GI-13-G)","how":"The site of a peptic ulcer predicts whether it bleeds from a named artery or perforates into the peritoneal cavity."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal neoplasms (GI-13-K)","how":"Venous drainage explains why gut cancers metastasize first to the liver, and lymph drainage across the pectinate line predicts nodal spread of anal cancers."},{"to":"Cardiovascular physiology","how":"Shock, heart failure, and atrial fibrillation emboli injure the gut and liver through the same flow, pressure, and watershed principles that apply in other organs."}],"terms":[{"term":"Watershed zone","def":"A region at the border between two arterial territories, supplied by the most distal branches of each, where perfusion pressure is lowest during a global fall in flow."},{"term":"Marginal artery","def":"A continuous arterial channel along the mesenteric border of the colon formed by anastomoses between the colic branches of the SMA and IMA."},{"term":"Portal system","def":"A venous arrangement in which blood passes through two capillary beds in series; the hepatic portal vein carries blood from the gut capillaries to the liver sinusoids."},{"term":"Sinusoid","def":"A wide, fenestrated hepatic capillary without a continuous basement membrane, where portal and arterial blood mix and exchange with hepatocytes across the space of Disse."},{"term":"Portal hypertension","def":"Abnormally high pressure in the portal venous system, caused by increased resistance to flow before, within, or after the liver."},{"term":"Hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG)","def":"The difference between wedged (sinusoidal) and free hepatic venous pressure; normally about 1 to 5 mmHg."},{"term":"Portosystemic anastomosis","def":"A site where a tributary of the portal vein communicates with a systemic vein, providing a bypass around the liver when portal pressure rises."},{"term":"Varix","def":"A dilated, tortuous vein; in portal hypertension, thin-walled submucosal veins at portosystemic anastomoses enlarge into varices."},{"term":"Caput medusae","def":"Dilated superficial abdominal wall veins radiating from the umbilicus, fed by paraumbilical veins in portal hypertension."},{"term":"Pectinate line","def":"The serrated line formed by the bases of the anal valves, marking the junction of hindgut endoderm above and proctodeal ectoderm below."},{"term":"Hepatic arterial buffer response","def":"Dilation of hepatic arterioles when portal flow falls, mediated by accumulation of adenosine that portal flow normally washes away."},{"term":"Peribiliary vascular plexus","def":"The capillary network around the intrahepatic bile ducts, supplied by branches of the hepatic artery."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C01","obj":"GI-5.1","title":"The celiac trunk and its branches","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three unpaired arteries leave the front of the abdominal aorta to supply the gut, and each serves one embryonic segment. The celiac trunk arises at about T12, just below the aortic hiatus, and supplies the foregut derivatives: the abdominal esophagus, stomach, duodenum to the major papilla, liver, gallbladder, and much of the pancreas. The spleen, which develops in the dorsal mesogastrium, is supplied by the same trunk. After a short course the trunk divides into three branches."},{"type":"table","title":"Branches of the celiac trunk","cols":["Branch","Course","Main branches","Supplies"],"rows":[["Left gastric","Up to the esophagogastric junction, then along the lesser curvature","Esophageal branches","Lower esophagus and lesser curvature"],["Splenic","Tortuous, along the upper border of the pancreas, behind the stomach","Pancreatic branches, short gastrics, left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic)","Body and tail of pancreas, fundus, upper greater curvature, spleen"],["Common hepatic","To the right, then divides near the duodenum","Proper hepatic (right and left hepatic, usually right gastric) and gastroduodenal","Liver, gallbladder (cystic artery, usually from right hepatic), pylorus, lesser curvature"],["Gastroduodenal (from common hepatic)","Descends behind the first part of the duodenum","Right gastro-omental, superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries","First part of duodenum, pancreatic head, lower greater curvature"]],"caption":"Each curvature of the stomach carries a pair of arteries that anastomose: left and right gastric along the lesser curvature, left and right gastro-omental along the greater."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the stomach receives arteries from every celiac branch and these join along both curvatures, gastric infarction is rare. The splenic artery, by contrast, divides into segmental branches within the spleen that behave as end arteries, so emboli produce wedge-shaped splenic infarcts. The superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries from the gastroduodenal artery anastomose around the pancreatic head with the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the SMA. This arcade marks the foregut to midgut boundary and is the main collateral between the celiac and SMA territories, so slow narrowing of one origin can be bypassed through the other."},{"type":"h","text":"Ulcer location predicts the bleeding vessel"},{"type":"p","text":"A peptic ulcer erodes whatever lies beneath it. The gastroduodenal artery runs directly behind the first part of the duodenum, so a posterior duodenal bulb ulcer can open it and cause brisk hematemesis or melena with hemodynamic instability. A lesser curvature gastric ulcer can erode the left gastric artery. An anterior duodenal ulcer has no large artery behind it, and its base faces the free peritoneal cavity, so it is more likely to perforate, releasing gas that collects under the diaphragm on an upright radiograph and causing chemical then bacterial peritonitis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a posterior duodenal bulb ulcer and another with an anterior duodenal ulcer both deteriorate suddenly. Which is more likely to show free air under the diaphragm, and which a falling hemoglobin?","explain":"The anterior ulcer perforates into the peritoneal cavity, so gas escapes and rises under the diaphragm. The posterior ulcer sits on the gastroduodenal artery and erodes it, so it bleeds into the lumen and the hemoglobin falls.","check":"The paragraph on ulcer location and the course of the gastroduodenal artery in the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C02","obj":"GI-5.2","title":"Mesenteric arteries, the marginal artery, and watershed ischemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) arises at about L1, passes behind the neck of the pancreas and in front of the third part of the duodenum, and supplies the midgut: the duodenum below the major papilla, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) arises at about L3 and supplies the hindgut: the distal third of the transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon, and upper rectum."},{"type":"table","title":"Mesenteric artery territories","cols":["Artery","Aortic level","Branches","Territory"],"rows":[["SMA","About L1","Inferior pancreaticoduodenal, jejunal and ileal, ileocolic, right colic, middle colic","Distal duodenum to proximal two thirds of transverse colon"],["IMA","About L3","Left colic, sigmoid, superior rectal","Distal third of transverse colon to upper rectum"],["Internal iliac branches","Pelvis","Middle rectal; inferior rectal via internal pudendal","Lower rectum and anal canal"]],"caption":"The rectum has three arterial sources, which is why it is usually spared in low-flow ischemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Branches of the colic arteries join near the mesenteric border of the colon to form the marginal artery, a continuous channel from the ileocolic region to the rectosigmoid. It links the SMA and IMA, so a slowly occluding IMA (as in atherosclerosis) can be bypassed. The link is weakest at two points. At the splenic flexure, the terminal branches of the middle colic (SMA) and left colic (IMA) meet, and the marginal artery there is thin or sometimes discontinuous. At the rectosigmoid junction, the last sigmoid artery meets the superior rectal artery with few connecting vessels."},{"type":"p","text":"These watershed zones sit at the far end of two territories, so pressure has already fallen along each supply before reaching them. When systemic pressure or cardiac output falls, as in shock, heart failure, dehydration, or after major surgery, they are the first to lose adequate flow. The mucosa is injured first because it has the highest metabolic demand and lies at the end of the penetrating vessels. Ischemic colitis typically presents in an older patient with sudden cramping left-sided abdominal pain followed within about a day by bloody diarrhea, because sloughing mucosa bleeds. Imaging may show thumbprinting, the rounded indentations produced by submucosal edema and hemorrhage. Most cases are nonocclusive and confined to the mucosa; transmural infarction leads to perforation and peritonitis."},{"type":"flow","title":"From hypotension to ischemic colitis","steps":["Cardiac output or systemic pressure falls","Mesenteric vasoconstriction diverts flow to heart and brain","Watershed zones receive the least perfusion pressure","Mucosa, with the highest demand, becomes ischemic first","Mucosal necrosis and sloughing","Cramping pain, then bloody diarrhea"],"caption":"Splenic flexure and rectosigmoid are affected first; the rectum is spared by its dual supply."},{"type":"p","text":"Ischemic colitis differs from acute mesenteric ischemia of the small bowel. An embolus from the left atrium in atrial fibrillation enters the SMA because it leaves the aorta at an acute angle, and it often lodges near the origin of the middle colic artery. Visceral pain fibers sense ischemia while the parietal peritoneum is not yet involved, so pain is severe but the abdomen is soft: pain out of proportion to the examination. Lactate rises as the bowel shifts to anaerobic metabolism. Chronic narrowing of two or more mesenteric origins produces postprandial pain (intestinal angina), because digestion demands a rise in flow the narrowed vessels cannot deliver, so patients fear food and lose weight."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C03","obj":"GI-5.3","title":"The portal venous pathway through the liver","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Venous blood from the abdominal gut, spleen, pancreas, and gallbladder does not go straight to the inferior vena cava (IVC). It collects in the hepatic portal vein and passes through a second capillary bed, the liver sinusoids, before returning to the heart. Absorbed nutrients, drugs, hormones such as insulin and glucagon, and bacterial products therefore reach hepatocytes first, which allows first-pass metabolism, nutrient storage, and clearance of gut-derived toxins such as ammonia and endotoxin."},{"type":"p","text":"The superior mesenteric vein (SMV) and splenic vein join behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein. The inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein, though it may enter the SMV or the confluence. The portal vein passes behind the first part of the duodenum and ascends in the free edge of the hepatoduodenal ligament, where it lies posterior to the common bile duct (on the right) and the proper hepatic artery (on the left). Along the way it receives the left and right gastric veins, and the cystic and paraumbilical veins drain into it or its branches. At the porta hepatis it divides into right and left branches that follow the arteries and bile ducts into the portal tracts."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of portal blood","steps":["SMV and splenic vein join behind the pancreatic neck","Portal vein ascends in the hepatoduodenal ligament","Right and left branches to portal tract venules","Mixes with hepatic arterial blood in sinusoids","Central veins collect sinusoidal blood","Sublobular veins to right, middle, and left hepatic veins","Hepatic veins enter the IVC below the diaphragm"],"caption":"Blood flows from the portal tract (zone 1) toward the central vein (zone 3), while bile flows the opposite way toward the portal tract."},{"type":"p","text":"The sinusoids are fenestrated and lack a continuous basement membrane, so plasma reaches hepatocyte microvilli in the space of Disse. Because blood enters at the portal tract and leaves at the central vein, hepatocytes in zone 3 (around the central vein) receive the least oxygen. They are the first to die in shock and the first to congest when hepatic venous pressure rises in right heart failure, producing the nutmeg liver."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal portal pressure is low, about 5 to 10 mmHg, and hepatic venous pressure is near 0 mmHg, so the liver offers little resistance. The portal system has essentially no valves, so any rise in resistance between the gut and the right atrium raises pressure throughout it. Classifying the obstruction by its site explains the findings."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of portal hypertension by site","cols":["Site","Examples","Distinguishing feature"],"rows":[["Prehepatic","Portal or splenic vein thrombosis","Liver function often normal"],["Intrahepatic, presinusoidal","Schistosomiasis (periportal fibrosis)","Wedged hepatic pressure may be normal"],["Intrahepatic, sinusoidal","Cirrhosis","Most common cause; HVPG raised"],["Posthepatic","Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic vein thrombosis), right heart failure, constrictive pericarditis","Tender hepatomegaly with centrilobular congestion"]],"caption":"HVPG above about 5 mmHg defines sinusoidal portal hypertension; above about 10 mmHg it is clinically significant."},{"type":"p","text":"As portal pressure rises, splenic venous outflow is impeded, so the spleen congests and enlarges, which sequesters platelets (hypersplenism). Raised sinusoidal pressure drives fluid into the space of Disse faster than hepatic lymphatics can remove it, which contributes to ascites."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C04","obj":"GI-5.4","title":"Portosystemic anastomoses and their signs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"At a few sites, small tributaries of the portal vein and of systemic veins drain the same tissue and connect with each other. In health these connections carry little blood, because portal pressure is only slightly higher than systemic venous pressure. When portal pressure rises, blood flows down the new gradient from the portal side into the systemic side, and the thin connecting veins dilate. The same shunting lets gut blood bypass hepatocytes, so ammonia and other gut-derived compounds reach the brain and contribute to hepatic encephalopathy."},{"type":"table","title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Result in portal hypertension"],"rows":[["Distal esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins draining to the azygos and hemiazygos veins","Esophageal varices; hematemesis"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins in the falciform ligament","Superficial and deep epigastric and thoracoepigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum and anal canal","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins draining to the internal iliac veins","Anorectal varices"],["Retroperitoneum","Colic, splenic, and pancreatic veins","Lumbar, renal, and other retroperitoneal veins","Retroperitoneal collaterals seen on imaging"]],"caption":"The first three sites are the classic examination triad: top, middle, and bottom of the abdominal gut."},{"type":"p","text":"Esophageal varices are the most dangerous. They lie in the submucosa of the lower esophagus with little surrounding tissue support. By the law of Laplace, wall stress equals pressure times radius divided by wall thickness, so as a varix widens and its wall thins, stress rises until the wall ruptures; the risk is substantial once HVPG exceeds about 12 mmHg. Bleeding is brisk because the vein carries portal pressure."},{"type":"flow","title":"From cirrhosis to variceal hemorrhage","steps":["Fibrosis and sinusoidal narrowing raise intrahepatic resistance","Portal pressure rises; HVPG above about 10 mmHg","Left gastric vein flow reverses toward the esophagus","Submucosal esophageal veins dilate into varices","Wall stress rises as radius grows and wall thins","Rupture causes massive hematemesis"],"caption":"Coagulopathy and thrombocytopenia from liver disease and hypersplenism worsen the bleed."},{"type":"p","text":"The paraumbilical veins accompany the ligamentum teres in the falciform ligament from the left portal vein to the umbilicus. Portal blood reaching them flows into abdominal wall veins and radiates outward from the navel as caput medusae: upward above the umbilicus and downward below it. In IVC obstruction, by contrast, leg blood returns to the heart through abdominal wall veins, so flow below the umbilicus runs upward. Anorectal varices form where the superior rectal vein meets the middle and inferior rectal veins. They are not the same as hemorrhoids, which are enlarged anal vascular cushions caused mainly by straining and raised abdominal pressure rather than by portal hypertension."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Dilated abdominal wall veins are seen in one patient with cirrhosis and in another with IVC obstruction. How would emptying a vein below the umbilicus and watching it refill tell them apart?","explain":"In portal hypertension blood radiates away from the umbilicus, so veins below it fill from above and flow downward. In IVC obstruction, blood from the legs must return to the heart through the abdominal wall, so veins below the umbilicus flow upward, often along the flanks.","check":"The caput medusae paragraph and the umbilical row of the table."},{"type":"example","title":"Gastric varices with a normal liver","text":"A patient with chronic pancreatitis vomits blood. Endoscopy shows large varices in the gastric fundus but none in the esophagus, and liver tests are normal. Inflammation in the pancreatic bed has thrombosed the splenic vein, which runs along the back of the pancreas. Splenic blood reroutes through the short gastric veins into the submucosal veins of the fundus, which dilate, and then reaches the portal vein through the gastric veins. Portal pressure itself is normal, so the esophageal anastomosis is not stressed. This is sinistral (left-sided) portal hypertension, a prehepatic cause, and the spleen is congested and enlarged."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C05","obj":"GI-5.5","title":"The anal canal above and below the pectinate line","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pectinate (dentate) line is formed by the bases of the anal valves about midway along the anal canal. It marks the site of the embryonic anal membrane, where the endoderm of the hindgut (cloaca) met the ectoderm of the proctodeum. Tissue above the line therefore behaves like gut and tissue below it like skin, and every feature follows from that origin."},{"type":"table","title":"Anal canal by the pectinate line","cols":["Feature","Above the line (endoderm)","Below the line (ectoderm)"],"rows":[["Epithelium","Simple columnar, with a narrow transitional zone near the line","Stratified squamous nonkeratinized, becoming keratinized skin at the anal verge"],["Arterial supply","Superior rectal artery (from IMA)","Inferior rectal artery (from internal pudendal, internal iliac)"],["Venous drainage","Superior rectal vein to IMV to portal vein","Inferior rectal vein to internal pudendal and internal iliac veins to IVC (systemic)"],["Lymph drainage","Internal iliac nodes (upper canal also along superior rectal vessels to inferior mesenteric nodes)","Superficial inguinal nodes"],["Innervation","Visceral afferents with the inferior hypogastric plexus","Somatic inferior rectal nerves (branches of the pudendal nerve, S2 to S4)"],["Pain","Senses stretch only; painless to cutting or pinching","Sensitive to pain, touch, and temperature"],["Typical lesions","Internal hemorrhoids (painless bleeding); adenocarcinoma","External hemorrhoids (painful when thrombosed); fissures; squamous cell carcinoma"]],"caption":"The middle rectal arteries and veins (internal iliac) also supply and drain the region around the anorectal junction."},{"type":"p","text":"Pain is the clearest clinical consequence. Visceral afferents above the line respond to distension but not to cutting, so internal hemorrhoids, polyps, and early tumors there are painless, and bright red blood on the stool may be the only sign. Below the line, the inferior rectal nerves carry somatic sensation, so a thrombosed external hemorrhoid or an anal fissure (a tear in the squamous lining, usually in the posterior midline) causes sharp, well-localized pain, especially on defecation."},{"type":"p","text":"Drainage predicts spread. Venous blood above the line enters the portal system, so adenocarcinoma of the rectum and upper canal can metastasize to the liver, while blood below the line enters the IVC and reaches the lungs first. Lymph from below the line goes to the superficial inguinal nodes, which can be felt in the groin, whereas nodes draining the upper canal lie deep in the pelvis and are not palpable. Squamous cell carcinoma, which arises from the squamous and transitional epithelium and is strongly associated with human papillomavirus, therefore often presents with an enlarged inguinal node."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing an anal lesion","text":"A patient reports painful defecation, a firm mass at the anal verge, and a hard, enlarged node in the groin. Pain means somatic innervation, so the lesion is below the pectinate line. Squamous epithelium lines that region, so the likely cancer is squamous cell carcinoma, and its lymph drains to the superficial inguinal nodes, explaining the palpable node. A second patient has painless bright red bleeding and a mass felt above the line on digital examination. Visceral innervation explains the absent pain, the columnar lining predicts adenocarcinoma, nodal spread goes to pelvic nodes that cannot be felt, and venous spread through the superior rectal vein goes to the liver."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C06","obj":"GI-5.6","title":"Dual blood supply of the liver","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The liver receives about 1350 mL of blood per minute, roughly a quarter of resting cardiac output. About 75% arrives through the portal vein at low pressure and about 25% through the hepatic artery at systemic arterial pressure. Portal blood has already passed through the gut and spleen and is partly deoxygenated, but its larger volume means each inflow delivers roughly half of the oxygen the liver uses. Both streams enter the same sinusoids from the portal tracts, so every hepatocyte is perfused by both."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver cannot control portal inflow, which is set by the splanchnic arterioles upstream in the gut and spleen. It does control hepatic arterial inflow, and it uses this to buffer changes in portal flow."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Hepatic arterial buffer response","steps":["Portal flow falls","Less adenosine is washed from around hepatic arterioles","Adenosine accumulates and dilates the arterioles","Hepatic arterial flow rises","Total flow and adenosine washout partly recover"],"caption":"The response works in one direction only: arterial flow compensates for portal changes, but portal flow does not compensate for arterial changes."},{"type":"p","text":"These features explain why hepatic infarction is uncommon. Occluding a portal vein branch usually causes only a sharply outlined red zone of sinusoidal congestion and hepatocyte atrophy without necrosis (the infarct of Zahn, which is not a true infarct). Ligating the common hepatic artery proximal to the gastroduodenal artery is usually tolerated, because blood from the SMA reaches the proper hepatic artery backward through the pancreaticoduodenal arcades and gastroduodenal artery (C01). True infarction needs loss of arterial flow together with poor portal flow or absent collaterals."},{"type":"p","text":"Global hypoperfusion is a different problem. In shock or severe heart failure both inflows fall together, and the zone 3 hepatocytes at the end of the sinusoid die first. This ischemic hepatitis (shock liver) produces a sharp rise in aminotransferases, often above 1000 U/L, that falls quickly once perfusion is restored."},{"type":"p","text":"The bile ducts have no such backup. Their peribiliary vascular plexus is supplied only by branches of the hepatic artery, so hepatocytes may survive arterial occlusion while ducts do not. After liver transplantation, the graft has no arterial collaterals, and hepatic artery thrombosis causes ischemic cholangiopathy: duct necrosis, bile leaks, strictures, and cholangitis. A similar arterial preference explains imaging of hepatocellular carcinoma, which draws its blood mainly from the hepatic artery, so it enhances brightly in the arterial phase and appears to wash out in the portal venous phase."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After liver transplantation, one patient develops portal vein thrombosis and another hepatic artery thrombosis. Which is more likely to develop bile duct strictures, and why?","explain":"Hepatic artery thrombosis. The peribiliary plexus depends on hepatic arterial blood alone, and the graft has no collaterals, so the ducts become ischemic. Portal vein thrombosis reduces hepatocyte inflow, but the hepatic arterial buffer response raises arterial flow and the ducts keep their supply.","check":"The buffer response cycle and the bile duct paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology organizes the anatomy. The foregut, midgut, and hindgut each take one unpaired aortic branch, their borders are where collateral arcades and watershed zones form, and the junction of endoderm and ectoderm at the pectinate line divides the anal canal into a gut-like and a skin-like territory with separate vessels, nodes, and nerves. Physiology supplies the mechanisms: watershed zones fail first because pressure has fallen by the time blood reaches them, mucosa is injured first because its demand is highest, and the liver protects its hepatocytes through two inflows and the adenosine-driven buffer response while leaving the bile ducts dependent on the artery alone."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and microbiology apply the same map. Cirrhosis from alcohol, viral hepatitis, or fatty liver disease raises sinusoidal resistance, schistosome eggs block presinusoidal venules, and pancreatitis thromboses the splenic vein, and each produces a predictable pattern of varices and splenomegaly. Venous drainage explains why colorectal cancer seeds the liver, and lymph drainage explains which nodes an anal cancer reaches. Arterial branching and collateral capacity vary between individuals, so these rules predict the typical case."}],"summary":["The celiac trunk (about T12), SMA (about L1), and IMA (about L3) supply the foregut, midgut, and hindgut, with the spleen supplied by the celiac trunk.","A posterior duodenal ulcer erodes the gastroduodenal artery and bleeds; a lesser curvature gastric ulcer erodes the left gastric artery; an anterior duodenal ulcer tends to perforate.","The marginal artery links SMA and IMA, but it is weakest at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction, which are the first sites of ischemic colitis after hypotension.","The SMV and splenic vein form the portal vein behind the pancreatic neck; portal blood passes through sinusoids to central veins, hepatic veins, and the IVC.","Portal hypertension is classified as prehepatic, intrahepatic (presinusoidal or sinusoidal), or posthepatic, and cirrhosis is the most common cause.","The portosystemic anastomoses at the esophagus, umbilicus, and rectum produce esophageal varices, caput medusae, and anorectal varices.","Above the pectinate line the canal is columnar, portal-drained, internal iliac node-drained, and painless; below it is squamous, systemically drained, inguinal node-drained, and painful.","The liver receives about 75% of its blood from the portal vein and about 25% from the hepatic artery, each providing about half its oxygen, so infarction is uncommon.","The hepatic arterial buffer response raises arterial inflow when portal flow falls, through local adenosine accumulation.","The bile ducts depend on the hepatic artery alone, so hepatic artery thrombosis after transplantation causes ischemic cholangiopathy and strictures."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen: vasculature of the abdominal viscera, the hepatic portal system, and the anal canal"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Splanchnic circulation and gastrointestinal blood flow; The Liver"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, vascular disorders of the bowel; Liver and Gallbladder, circulatory disorders"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Liver Disease, section on portal hypertension"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs associated with the digestive tract: the liver"}]},"GI-6":{"unit":"GI-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01","title":"Motility, Digestion, and Absorption","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows a meal from the mouth to the anus. It explains how chewing and swallowing move food past the airway, how slow waves and enteric reflexes propel it, how the stomach makes acid, how gut hormones match secretion and motility to what reaches the duodenum, how carbohydrate, protein, and fat are digested and absorbed, and how defecation and vomiting are controlled. Board questions usually describe a meal, a receptor blocker, a nerve injury, or a set of electrolytes and ask you to predict the hormone, secretion, motor pattern, or acid-base change that follows. Each response is produced by a specific cell, nerve, or receptor, so knowing which one lets you predict the effect of losing it.","prerequisites":["Layers of the gut wall and the positions of the myenteric and submucosal plexuses","Smooth muscle excitation-contraction coupling and gap junctions","G protein signaling (Gs, Gi, Gq, cAMP, IP3, and intracellular Ca2+)","Cranial nerves V, IX, X, and XII and the sacral spinal cord (S2 to S4)","Renal handling of Na+, K+, H+, and HCO3- and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system"],"connections":[{"to":"Biochemistry of carbohydrates and lipids","how":"The monosaccharides and chylomicrons produced here are the substrates of glycolysis and of lipoprotein metabolism."},{"to":"Endocrine pancreas","how":"GIP and GLP-1 are incretins, so the gut shapes the insulin response to an oral meal."},{"to":"Neuroanatomy of the brainstem and sacral cord","how":"Swallowing and vomiting are medullary programs and defecation is a sacral reflex, so lesion level predicts the deficit."},{"to":"Renal and acid-base physiology","how":"The kidney decides whether the alkalosis of vomiting persists, through aldosterone, chloride, and potassium."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal pathology","how":"Achalasia, Hirschsprung disease, gastrinoma, and malabsorption are each the loss of one mechanism taught here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","def":"Pacemaker cells in the gut wall that generate slow waves and pass them to smooth muscle through gap junctions."},{"term":"Slow wave","def":"A rhythmic oscillation of smooth muscle membrane potential that sets the maximum frequency of contraction but does not by itself cause most contractions."},{"term":"Spike potential","def":"A Ca2+-carried action potential that fires on a slow wave crest when the membrane passes threshold and triggers contraction."},{"term":"Migrating motor complex (MMC)","def":"A cyclic fasting pattern of intense contractions that sweeps from the stomach to the ileum about every 90 to 120 minutes."},{"term":"Receptive relaxation","def":"Vagally mediated relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter and proximal stomach that lets a swallowed bolus enter with little rise in pressure."},{"term":"Potentiation","def":"A combined response larger than the sum of the separate responses, as when histamine, acetylcholine, and gastrin act together on the parietal cell."},{"term":"Enteroendocrine cell","def":"An epithelial cell that senses luminal contents and releases a hormone or paracrine signal from its basal surface."},{"term":"Brush border enzyme","def":"A hydrolase anchored in the enterocyte microvillus membrane that completes digestion next to the transporters."},{"term":"Mixed micelle","def":"A small aggregate of bile salts, monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, and phospholipid that carries lipid through the unstirred water layer."},{"term":"Rectoanal inhibitory reflex","def":"Enteric reflex in which rectal distension relaxes the internal anal sphincter."},{"term":"Chemoreceptor trigger zone","def":"Neurons of the area postrema, outside the blood-brain barrier, that detect emetic chemicals in blood and cerebrospinal fluid."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C01","obj":"GI-6.1","title":"Mastication, swallowing, and relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mastication breaks food into small particles, mixes it with saliva, and enlarges the surface that digestive enzymes can reach. The four muscles of mastication (masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid, and lateral pterygoid) derive from the first pharyngeal arch and are all supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3). The masseter, temporalis, and medial pterygoid close the jaw, while the lateral pterygoids protrude the mandible and help open it; alternating action of the two sides produces the grinding movement. Rhythmic chewing is largely a brainstem reflex: a bolus pressing on the palate inhibits the jaw closers, the jaw drops, the stretch then triggers rebound contraction, and the cycle repeats while the cortex decides when it starts and stops."},{"type":"p","text":"Swallowing has three phases. The oral phase is voluntary: the tongue (CN XII) presses the bolus up against the hard palate and back into the oropharynx. Once the bolus stimulates receptors around the pharyngeal opening, afferents mainly in CN IX and CN X reach the swallowing center in the medulla and lower pons (around the nucleus tractus solitarius and nucleus ambiguus). From that point the pharyngeal and esophageal phases run as an involuntary reflex that cannot be stopped. The center drives a fixed motor sequence through CN V, IX, X, and XII and briefly inhibits the respiratory center, so breathing pauses while the airway is sealed."},{"type":"flow","title":"The swallowing sequence","steps":["Tongue pushes bolus into oropharynx (voluntary, CN XII)","Pharyngeal afferents (CN IX, X) activate medullary swallowing center","Soft palate rises and seals the nasopharynx","Larynx rises, epiglottis tilts, vocal folds close, breathing pauses","Upper esophageal sphincter (cricopharyngeus) relaxes","Pharyngeal constrictors drive bolus into the esophagus","Primary peristalsis carries bolus toward the stomach","LES and proximal stomach relax ahead of the bolus"],"caption":"The oral phase is voluntary; every later step is a medullary reflex that protects the airway and clears the esophagus."},{"type":"p","text":"The upper third of the esophagus is skeletal muscle driven directly by vagal motor neurons of the nucleus ambiguus, the lower third is smooth muscle controlled by vagal preganglionic fibers acting through the myenteric plexus, and the middle third is mixed. The primary peristaltic wave continues the pharyngeal wave and reaches the stomach in about 8 to 10 seconds. If food remains, local distension starts secondary peristalsis through enteric and vagal reflexes without a new swallow."},{"type":"p","text":"The lower esophageal sphincter (LES) is tonically contracted circular smooth muscle that keeps gastric contents out of the esophagus. When a swallow begins, vagal preganglionic fibers activate inhibitory myenteric neurons that release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP). Nitric oxide raises cGMP and VIP raises cAMP in the muscle; both lower cytosolic Ca2+, so the sphincter relaxes before the wave arrives. The same vagovagal pathway relaxes the fundus and upper body of the stomach (receptive relaxation), so a meal is accommodated with little rise in gastric pressure."},{"type":"table","title":"Failures of swallowing and LES control","cols":["Disorder","Mechanism","Manifestation"],"rows":[["Achalasia (idiopathic, or Chagas disease from Trypanosoma cruzi)","Loss of inhibitory NO and VIP myenteric neurons in the distal esophagus","LES fails to relax and peristalsis is lost; dysphagia to solids and liquids, bird-beak narrowing on barium study"],["Systemic sclerosis","Atrophy and fibrosis of esophageal smooth muscle","Weak LES and absent distal peristalsis; reflux with dysphagia"],["Gastroesophageal reflux","Transient LES relaxations not triggered by swallowing, low tone, or hiatal hernia","Acid in the esophagus, heartburn, and mucosal injury"],["Proximal vagotomy","Loss of vagovagal receptive relaxation","Gastric pressure rises quickly with filling; early satiety"],["Medullary stroke or CN IX and X lesions","Pharyngeal phase and airway protection fail","Oropharyngeal dysphagia, nasal regurgitation, aspiration"]],"caption":"Each disorder removes one element of the sequence, and the symptom follows from that element."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C02","obj":"GI-6.2","title":"Slow waves, peristalsis, and the migrating motor complex","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Gastrointestinal smooth muscle cells are linked by gap junctions, so each layer behaves as an electrical syncytium. Their membrane potential, normally about -50 to -60 mV, oscillates in slow waves of roughly 5 to 15 mV. Slow waves are generated by interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC), pacemaker cells concentrated at the myenteric plexus between the circular and longitudinal layers, and spread to the muscle through gap junctions."},{"type":"p","text":"In most regions a slow wave by itself does not cause contraction. Contraction needs spike potentials, which fire when a slow wave crest carries the membrane past threshold (about -40 mV) and which are carried mainly by Ca2+ entering through voltage-gated L-type channels. That Ca2+ triggers contraction. Because spikes occur only near crests, slow wave frequency sets the maximum contraction rate of each region, while nerves and hormones decide whether spikes occur and how many. Acetylcholine, stretch, and parasympathetic activity depolarize the muscle and increase spiking; norepinephrine hyperpolarizes it and reduces spiking."},{"type":"table","title":"Slow wave frequency by region","cols":["Region","Approximate frequency"],"rows":[["Stomach","3 per minute"],["Duodenum","12 per minute"],["Terminal ileum","8 to 9 per minute"]],"caption":"The fall in frequency along the small intestine favors net aboral movement."},{"type":"p","text":"Peristalsis is a local reflex of the enteric nervous system, sometimes called the law of the gut. Distension stimulates mucosal enterochromaffin cells to release serotonin and activates intrinsic sensory neurons, which drive myenteric interneurons that send excitation orally and inhibition aborally. Above the bolus, excitatory motor neurons release acetylcholine and substance P and the circular muscle contracts; below it, inhibitory motor neurons release VIP and nitric oxide and the circular muscle relaxes. Because the circuit is intrinsic, peristalsis continues after vagotomy, but it fails wherever myenteric ganglia are missing."},{"type":"flow","title":"The peristaltic reflex","steps":["Bolus stretches the gut wall","Enterochromaffin cells release serotonin; intrinsic sensory neurons fire","Myenteric interneurons signal up and down the gut","Above bolus: ACh and substance P contract circular muscle","Below bolus: VIP and nitric oxide relax circular muscle","Bolus moves aborally and the reflex repeats"],"caption":"Peristalsis needs only the myenteric plexus; extrinsic nerves adjust its strength."},{"type":"p","text":"Between meals the stomach and small intestine switch to the migrating motor complex (MMC), which recurs about every 90 to 120 minutes. Phase I is quiescence, phase II brings irregular contractions, and phase III is a few minutes of intense contractions at the maximal slow wave rate that moves from the stomach or duodenum to the ileum. The pylorus opens widely during phase III, so indigestible solids, debris, and bacteria are swept onward. Motilin from M cells of the duodenum and jejunum rises cyclically during fasting and initiates gastric phase III. Eating suppresses motilin and replaces the MMC with mixing contractions. Erythromycin binds motilin receptors, which is why it provokes strong gastric contractions."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of the MMC, as in diabetic autonomic neuropathy or systemic sclerosis, lets bacteria accumulate in the small intestine (small intestinal bacterial overgrowth). In Hirschsprung disease, neural crest cells fail to colonize the rectum and a variable length of distal colon, so the aganglionic segment has no inhibitory neurons, stays contracted, and obstructs the dilated bowel above it."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After a truncal vagotomy, does peristalsis in the small intestine continue?","explain":"Yes. The peristaltic reflex is built from intrinsic sensory neurons, interneurons, and motor neurons within the myenteric plexus. Vagotomy weakens extrinsic modulation and abolishes receptive relaxation of the stomach, but the local circuit still contracts above and relaxes below a bolus. Peristalsis fails only where the plexus itself is absent, as in Hirschsprung disease.","check":"The paragraph on the peristaltic reflex and the Hirschsprung paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C03","obj":"GI-6.3","title":"Gastric acid secretion and its regulation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells in the glands of the gastric body and fundus secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor. Inside the cell, carbonic anhydrase converts CO2 and water to H+ and HCO3-. The H+/K+ ATPase pumps H+ into the lumen in exchange for K+, while HCO3- leaves across the basolateral membrane in exchange for Cl-, producing the alkaline tide in gastric venous blood after a meal. Cl- and K+ leave through apical channels, so K+ recycles and HCl accumulates. At rest the pumps sit in cytoplasmic tubulovesicles; stimulation inserts them into the apical canalicular membrane, making the pump the final common step for every stimulus."},{"type":"table","title":"Regulators of the parietal cell","cols":["Signal","Source","Receptor and messenger","Effect"],"rows":[["Acetylcholine","Vagal postganglionic enteric neurons","M3, Gq, IP3 and Ca2+","Stimulates parietal cells and ECL cells"],["Gastrin","G cells of the antrum","CCK-B (CCK2), Gq, Ca2+","Stimulates parietal cells weakly and ECL histamine release strongly"],["Histamine","Enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells","H2, Gs, cAMP","Strong stimulation; potentiates the other two"],["Somatostatin","D cells of the antrum and body","Somatostatin receptor, Gi","Inhibits parietal, G, and ECL cells"]],"caption":"Three stimulants converge on one pump; somatostatin opposes all three."},{"type":"p","text":"Histamine signals through cAMP while acetylcholine and gastrin signal through Ca2+, so together they produce more acid than the sum of their separate effects (potentiation). Much of gastrin's action is indirect, through ECL histamine. Potentiation explains why an H2 receptor antagonist also reduces acid secreted in response to acetylcholine and gastrin, whereas blocking the H+/K+ ATPase stops acid whatever the stimulus."},{"type":"p","text":"Antral D cells sense luminal acid. When antral pH falls below about 3, they release somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release and acid secretion once the meal's buffering capacity is used up. The vagus pushes the other way: it stimulates G cells through gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP) and inhibits D cells. Because the G cell transmitter is GRP rather than acetylcholine, atropine does not block vagally driven gastrin release."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Somatostatin feedback on acid secretion","steps":["Food buffers acid and antral pH rises","G cells release gastrin","ECL histamine and parietal cells increase HCl","Antral pH falls below about 3","D cells release somatostatin","Gastrin and acid secretion fall"],"caption":"Negative feedback through antral pH limits acid output once buffering is exhausted."},{"type":"table","title":"Phases of gastric acid secretion","cols":["Phase","Trigger","Main mediators","Share of meal response"],"rows":[["Cephalic","Sight, smell, taste, chewing, thought of food","Vagus: ACh to parietal and ECL cells, GRP to G cells","About 30%"],["Gastric","Distension; peptides and amino acids in the stomach","Vagovagal and local reflexes; gastrin","About 60%"],["Intestinal","Protein products in the duodenum stimulate slightly; acid, fat, and hyperosmolar chyme inhibit","Duodenal gastrin; inhibition by secretin, GIP, and the enterogastric reflex","About 10%"]],"caption":"Each phase is named for where the signal starts; the intestinal phase is mainly inhibitory."},{"type":"p","text":"Feedback explains two opposite causes of high gastrin. A gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome) secretes gastrin autonomously, so acid stays high despite low pH; the result is multiple or distal duodenal and jejunal ulcers, and diarrhea with steatorrhea because acid inactivates pancreatic lipase and precipitates bile salts. In autoimmune atrophic gastritis, parietal cells are destroyed, acid is absent, D cells are no longer stimulated, and gastrin rises steeply, which also drives ECL cell hyperplasia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"How does fasting serum gastrin change when parietal cell acid secretion is suppressed for months?","explain":"It rises. Without acid, antral pH stays high, D cells release little somatostatin, and G cells are released from inhibition. The same feedback explains the very high gastrin of atrophic gastritis, whereas in gastrinoma the gastrin is high while acid is also high.","check":"The somatostatin feedback cycle and the final paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C04","obj":"GI-6.4","title":"Gut hormones and the response to a meal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Enteroendocrine cells are scattered among the epithelial cells of the stomach and small intestine. Their apical surfaces sample the lumen, and their basal granules release hormone into the blood when they detect a particular nutrient, acid, stretch, or neural signal. Each hormone therefore reports one feature of the chyme, and its actions correct that feature: secretin answers acid, CCK answers fat and protein, and GIP answers absorbed fuel."},{"type":"table","title":"The five classic gut hormones","cols":["Hormone","Cell and site","Main stimuli","Main actions"],"rows":[["Gastrin","G cells, gastric antrum (also duodenum)","Peptides and amino acids, gastric distension, vagal GRP; inhibited by antral pH below about 3","Gastric acid secretion (directly and through ECL histamine), growth of the gastric mucosa"],["Cholecystokinin (CCK)","I cells, duodenum and jejunum","Fatty acids, monoglycerides, amino acids, and small peptides","Pancreatic enzyme secretion, gallbladder contraction, sphincter of Oddi relaxation, slower gastric emptying, satiety"],["Secretin","S cells, duodenum","Duodenal pH below about 4.5; fatty acids","Pancreatic and biliary HCO3- secretion; inhibits gastrin and gastric acid"],["Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)","K cells, duodenum and jejunum","Glucose, fatty acids, amino acids","Insulin release when glucose is high (incretin); reduces gastric acid at high levels"],["Motilin","M cells, duodenum and jejunum","Fasting, released in cycles; suppressed by eating","Initiates phase III of the migrating motor complex"]],"caption":"Source cell letters: G for gastrin, I for CCK, S for secretin, K for GIP, M for motilin."},{"type":"p","text":"The hormones fall into structural families. Gastrin and CCK share the same five C-terminal amino acids. The CCK-B receptor binds both about equally, which is how gastrin acts on parietal and ECL cells, whereas the CCK-A receptor, which mediates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic secretion, strongly prefers CCK. Secretin, GIP, VIP, and glucagon form a second family."},{"type":"p","text":"Secretin acts on pancreatic duct cells through cAMP, opening CFTR Cl- channels; luminal Cl- then exchanges for HCO3-, which pours into the duct. The response is proportional to the acid load because secretin release continues until duodenal pH rises. CCK stimulates acinar enzyme secretion, partly through vagovagal reflexes, and CCK and secretin potentiate each other on the pancreas. GIP explains why oral glucose releases more insulin than the same glucose given intravenously: GIP and GLP-1 (from L cells) amplify insulin release only when glucose is high."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the response to an acidic, fatty meal","text":"Acidic chyme rich in fat enters the duodenum. First, duodenal pH below about 4.5 stimulates S cells: secretin rises, pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate neutralize the acid, and secretin inhibits gastrin. Second, fatty acids and monoglycerides stimulate I cells: CCK rises, enzymes are secreted, the gallbladder contracts, the sphincter of Oddi relaxes, and gastric emptying slows. Third, fatty acids and any glucose stimulate K cells: GIP rises and enhances insulin release. Fourth, gastrin and acid fall, because secretin inhibits G cells, antral acid releases somatostatin, and the enterogastric reflex adds neural inhibition. Fifth, feeding suppresses motilin, so the MMC stops. Net effect: the duodenum slows delivery from the stomach and neutralizes and digests what has already arrived."},{"type":"p","text":"Measured hormone levels follow from the same logic. Excess gastrin with high acid points to a gastrinoma; in that tumor, intravenous secretin paradoxically raises gastrin, the reverse of its effect on normal G cells. Loss of duodenal mucosa in celiac disease reduces secretin and CCK release, which weakens pancreatic and gallbladder responses and adds to malabsorption."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C05","obj":"GI-6.5","title":"Digestion and absorption of carbohydrate, protein, and fat","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dietary starch is amylose (straight alpha 1,4 glucose chains) and amylopectin (alpha 1,4 chains with alpha 1,6 branches). Salivary alpha-amylase starts digestion but is inactivated by gastric acid once it mixes with chyme, so pancreatic alpha-amylase does most of the work in the duodenum. Amylase cleaves only internal alpha 1,4 bonds, leaving maltose, maltotriose, and alpha-limit dextrins that retain the branch points; it cannot cleave the beta 1,4 bonds of cellulose, so fiber reaches the colon intact. Brush border enzymes finish the job next to the transporters: lactase splits lactose, sucrase-isomaltase splits sucrose and the alpha 1,6 bonds of limit dextrins, and maltase-glucoamylase splits maltose and maltotriose. Glucose and galactose enter by SGLT1, which couples them to two Na+ ions using the gradient made by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase; fructose enters by facilitated diffusion through GLUT5; all three leave through GLUT2."},{"type":"p","text":"Protein digestion begins with pepsin, formed from chief cell pepsinogen in acid and active at pH about 2 to 3, but it is not essential. The decisive step is in the duodenum: enteropeptidase (enterokinase), anchored in the brush border, converts trypsinogen to trypsin. Trypsin then activates more trypsinogen and the other zymogens: chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, procarboxypeptidases, prophospholipase A2, and procolipase. Keeping the activator on the intestinal surface, along with storage as zymogens and trypsin inhibitor (SPINK1) in acinar cells, protects the pancreas from digesting itself; premature trypsin activation underlies pancreatitis. Brush border peptidases produce amino acids and di- and tripeptides. Peptides enter by the H+-coupled transporter PepT1 and amino acids by Na+-dependent carriers, and cytoplasmic peptidases release amino acids to the portal blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Fat is mostly triglyceride, and the problem is that lipase works only at an oil-water interface. Gastric churning and bile salts, which are amphipathic, emulsify fat into small droplets. Bile salts on the droplet surface would displace pancreatic lipase, so colipase anchors lipase to the surface. Lipase removes the fatty acids at positions 1 and 3, leaving 2-monoglyceride; cholesterol esterase and phospholipase A2 digest the other lipids. Long-chain fatty acids reach the blood by lymph, while medium-chain fatty acids enter portal blood directly."},{"type":"flow","title":"Digestion and absorption of long-chain fat","steps":["Churning and bile salts emulsify fat into small droplets","Colipase anchors pancreatic lipase to the droplet surface","Lipase releases 2-monoglyceride and two free fatty acids","Products join bile salts in mixed micelles","Micelles cross the unstirred water layer; lipids enter enterocyte","Smooth ER rebuilds triglyceride; MTP loads it onto apoB-48","Chylomicrons enter lacteals and reach blood by thoracic duct","Bile salts are reabsorbed in terminal ileum"],"caption":"Bile salts carry lipid to the brush border but are absorbed separately in the ileum and recycled by the liver."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting malabsorption from the missing step","cols":["Defect","Mechanism","Result"],"rows":[["Lactase deficiency","Lactose remains in the lumen and is fermented by colonic bacteria","Osmotic diarrhea, bloating, high breath hydrogen, acidic stool; fat absorption normal"],["Pancreatic insufficiency (chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis)","Loss of lipase, colipase, proteases, and amylase","Prominent steatorrhea, deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K; D-xylose absorption normal"],["Enteropeptidase deficiency","Trypsinogen is not activated, so no zymogen cascade","Protein malabsorption, hypoproteinemia, edema, failure to thrive"],["Terminal ileal resection or ileal Crohn disease","Bile salts lost to the colon; pool shrinks","Fat malabsorption, bile acid diarrhea, vitamin B12 deficiency"],["Abetalipoproteinemia","MTP deficiency prevents chylomicron assembly","Lipid-laden enterocytes, steatorrhea, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, acanthocytes"],["Celiac disease","Villous atrophy removes brush border enzymes and surface","Global malabsorption; D-xylose absorption low"]],"caption":"D-xylose needs no digestion, so it separates mucosal disease from pancreatic insufficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C06","obj":"GI-6.6","title":"Defecation and the gastrocolic reflex","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The rectum is usually empty until a mass movement, a strong propulsive contraction of a long colonic segment that occurs a few times a day, pushes feces into it. Two sphincters keep the anal canal closed. The internal anal sphincter is a thickening of the circular smooth muscle; it is tonically contracted, provides most of the resting anal pressure, and is not under voluntary control. The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle supplied by the inferior rectal branches of the pudendal nerve (S2 to S4); together with the puborectalis sling, which holds the anorectal angle, it is under voluntary control."},{"type":"p","text":"Rectal distension starts two reflexes. The intrinsic myenteric reflex sends peristaltic waves through the descending and sigmoid colon and relaxes the internal sphincter (the rectoanal inhibitory reflex), but it is weak alone. A parasympathetic reflex through the sacral cord amplifies it: afferents enter the cord and pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4) return to strengthen colonic contraction and internal sphincter relaxation. At the same time the sensation of rectal filling reaches the cortex."},{"type":"flow","title":"The defecation reflex","steps":["Mass movement fills and stretches the rectum","Myenteric reflex relaxes internal sphincter; urge is felt","Sacral parasympathetic reflex (pelvic nerves, S2 to S4) amplifies contraction","Cortex chooses: keep external sphincter closed or relax it","If delayed, rectum accommodates and urge fades","If allowed, puborectalis relaxes and Valsalva expels stool"],"caption":"The internal sphincter relaxes by reflex; the decision is made at the external sphincter."},{"type":"p","text":"If the time is not right, voluntary contraction of the external sphincter and puborectalis holds stool back; the rectal wall relaxes to accommodate the volume, the internal sphincter regains tone, and the urge fades until the next mass movement. When defecation is chosen, both skeletal muscles relax, the anorectal angle straightens, and a Valsalva maneuver raises intra-abdominal pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"In the gastrocolic reflex, distension of the stomach after a meal increases colonic motility and mass movements within minutes, through extrinsic autonomic nerves and enteric pathways, with gastrin and CCK possibly contributing. It explains the urge to defecate soon after eating, especially after breakfast."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting incontinence from the lost element","cols":["Lost element","Mechanism","Result"],"rows":[["Internal sphincter (surgical division, obstetric tear)","Low resting pressure","Passive leakage of stool or mucus at rest, often without awareness"],["External sphincter or pudendal nerve (obstetric injury, stretch neuropathy)","Weak voluntary squeeze","Urge incontinence: stool cannot be deferred once the rectum fills"],["Rectal sensation (diabetic neuropathy)","Filling not perceived, so the external sphincter is not contracted in time","Leakage without an urge"],["Spinal cord above the sacral segments","Sacral reflex intact, cortical control lost","Reflex emptying when the rectum fills, with no voluntary control"],["Sacral cord, cauda equina, or pelvic nerves","Reflex arc lost; external sphincter flaccid","Weak propulsion, retention with overflow leakage"],["Rectal compliance (proctitis, radiation)","Small, stiff reservoir","Urgency and frequency"]],"caption":"The internal sphincter guards against passive leakage, the external sphincter against urgency."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After a pudendal nerve stretch injury in childbirth, will the internal sphincter still relax when the rectum fills, and what symptom follows?","explain":"Yes. The rectoanal inhibitory reflex is enteric and the amplifying reflex uses pelvic splanchnic nerves, not the pudendal nerve. The internal sphincter therefore relaxes normally, but the weakened external sphincter cannot hold stool back, so she has urge incontinence.","check":"The two reflexes and the incontinence table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C07","obj":"GI-6.7","title":"Vomiting and the metabolic alkalosis it causes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vomiting is a coordinated motor program that empties the upper gut. It is organized by the vomiting center, a network in the dorsal medulla centered on the nucleus tractus solitarius and adjacent reticular formation rather than a single nucleus. The chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) lies in the area postrema in the caudal floor of the fourth ventricle. Its fenestrated capillaries leave it outside the blood-brain barrier, so it detects drugs, toxins, and uremic solutes that most neurons cannot reach and relays them to the vomiting center."},{"type":"table","title":"Inputs to the vomiting center","cols":["Input","Pathway","Typical triggers","Key receptors"],"rows":[["Chemoreceptor trigger zone","Area postrema to nucleus tractus solitarius","Opioids, chemotherapy, toxins, uremia","D2, 5-HT3, NK1"],["Gastrointestinal tract","Vagal afferents; serotonin from enterochromaffin cells","Mucosal irritation, distension, obstruction, chemotherapy","5-HT3"],["Vestibular system","Vestibular nuclei to the medulla","Motion sickness","H1, muscarinic"],["Cortex and limbic system","Descending pathways","Pain, smells, sights, anticipation","Several"]],"caption":"Blood-borne emetics act at the CTZ, gut irritants mainly through vagal 5-HT3 afferents."},{"type":"flow","title":"The act of vomiting","steps":["Nausea with salivation, sweating, pallor, and tachycardia","Retrograde contraction returns small bowel contents to stomach","Deep inspiration; glottis closes; soft palate seals nasopharynx","LES and gastric body relax","Diaphragm and abdominal muscles contract forcefully","Gastric contents are expelled through the open upper sphincter"],"caption":"Airway protection mirrors swallowing; the force comes from abdominal and diaphragmatic muscle, not the stomach."},{"type":"p","text":"For every H+ the parietal cell secretes, one HCO3- enters the blood. Normally that bicarbonate is matched when acid entering the duodenum triggers secretin and pancreatic HCO3- secretion. When gastric HCl is vomited, the pancreas is not stimulated, the bicarbonate stays in the plasma, and Cl- is lost: this generates a hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis. Gastric fluid contains little K+ (roughly 5 to 10 mEq/L), so the vomitus accounts for only a small part of the K+ deficit."},{"type":"p","text":"A healthy kidney would excrete the extra bicarbonate quickly; vomiting maintains the alkalosis through three changes. Volume depletion activates renin, angiotensin II, and aldosterone. Angiotensin II increases proximal HCO3- reabsorption through the Na+/H+ exchanger, and aldosterone increases Na+ reabsorption by principal cells, making the lumen more negative and increasing K+ secretion by principal cells and H+ secretion by alpha-intercalated cells. Chloride depletion limits bicarbonate secretion by the pendrin Cl-/HCO3- exchanger of beta-intercalated cells. Hypokalemia shifts H+ into cells and increases H+ secretion and ammoniagenesis. The resulting urine Cl- is low, often below about 20 mEq/L, and late in the course the urine can be paradoxically acidic. Respiratory compensation by hypoventilation raises PaCO2 by about 0.7 mmHg for each 1 mEq/L rise in HCO3-. Restoring volume and chloride removes the stimulus, so this alkalosis is called saline-responsive."},{"type":"flow","title":"Generation and maintenance of the alkalosis","steps":["Vomiting removes HCl from the stomach","Retained HCO3- raises pH; plasma Cl- falls","Volume loss activates renin, angiotensin II, and aldosterone","Aldosterone increases urinary K+ and H+ loss","Hypokalemia and low Cl- sustain HCO3- reabsorption","Alkalosis persists until volume and Cl- are restored"],"caption":"Loss of acid starts the alkalosis; the kidney's response to volume and chloride loss keeps it going."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the electrolytes after prolonged vomiting","text":"After four days of vomiting: Na+ 132, K+ 2.9, Cl- 86, HCO3- 34 mEq/L, arterial pH 7.48, PaCO2 47 mmHg, urine Cl- 8 mEq/L. pH above 7.45 with HCO3- above 26 mEq/L is metabolic alkalosis. Expected PaCO2 is 40 + 0.7 x (34 - 24), or 47 mmHg, so compensation is appropriate. The anion gap is 132 - (86 + 34) = 12 mEq/L, normal. Low plasma and urine Cl- mark chloride and volume loss, a saline-responsive pattern; mineralocorticoid excess would give high urine Cl-. The low K+ reflects renal wasting driven by aldosterone. Diarrhea gives the opposite: bicarbonate loss and a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology set the wiring. Chewing and swallowing depend on first arch muscles and the brainstem nuclei of CN V, IX, X, and XII, so a medullary stroke impairs swallowing while sparing the intrinsic motility of the stomach and intestine. Neural crest cells form the enteric plexuses, so their failure to migrate (Hirschsprung disease) or their destruction (achalasia, Chagas disease) removes inhibitory neurons and leaves smooth muscle unable to relax. Defecation depends on the sacral cord, so the level of a spinal lesion predicts reflex or areflexic bowel function."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology, biochemistry, and pathology then explain the outputs. G protein messengers explain potentiation at the parietal cell, and negative feedback through somatostatin explains why gastrin is high both in gastrinoma and in atrophic gastritis. Enzyme biochemistry explains which nutrient fails first in each malabsorption, and lipoprotein assembly links fat absorption to chylomicron metabolism. Renal physiology decides whether vomiting produces a lasting alkalosis. The limits are that many values here (slow wave rates, phase percentages, pH thresholds) are approximate, and that hormone actions overlap, so board questions test the dominant effect rather than every effect."}],"summary":["All muscles of mastication are supplied by CN V3; the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary, and the pharyngeal and esophageal phases are a medullary reflex that protects the airway.","Vagal inhibitory neurons release nitric oxide and VIP to relax the LES and proximal stomach; their loss causes achalasia.","Interstitial cells of Cajal set slow wave frequency (about 3 per minute in the stomach, 12 in the duodenum); contraction needs Ca2+ spike potentials on the crests.","Peristalsis is a myenteric reflex: ACh and substance P contract above the bolus, VIP and nitric oxide relax below it.","Motilin drives the fasting MMC about every 90 to 120 minutes; feeding stops it, and its loss favors bacterial overgrowth.","ACh (M3, Ca2+), gastrin (CCK-B, Ca2+, mostly via ECL histamine), and histamine (H2, cAMP) potentiate one another at the parietal cell; the H+/K+ ATPase is the final step and somatostatin is the brake.","Acid and fat in the duodenum raise secretin, CCK, and GIP, lower gastrin, acid, and motilin, and slow gastric emptying.","Enteropeptidase activates trypsinogen, trypsin activates the other zymogens, and lipase needs colipase and bile salt micelles; chylomicrons leave through lacteals.","Rectal distension relaxes the internal sphincter by reflex; the pudendal nerve gives voluntary control of the external sphincter, so internal sphincter loss causes passive leakage and external sphincter loss causes urge incontinence.","Prolonged vomiting causes hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, generated by HCl loss and maintained by volume depletion, aldosterone, hypokalemia, and chloride depletion, with low urine Cl-."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Gastrointestinal physiology: general principles of motility, propulsion and mixing of food, and secretory functions of the alimentary tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and absorption in the gastrointestinal tract; physiology of gastrointestinal disorders"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Gastrointestinal physiology: gastric function, pancreatic secretion, nutrient digestion and absorption"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Digestion of dietary carbohydrates, lipids, and proteins"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head: muscles of mastication; Pelvis and perineum: anal canal and anal sphincters"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Gastrointestinal disease; acid-base disorders"}]},"GI-7":{"unit":"GI-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01","title":"Absorption of the Products of Digestion","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the end products of digestion across the intestinal wall: monosaccharides, amino acids and small peptides, fats, and the vitamins and minerals that depend on one segment of bowel. It then covers the enterohepatic circulation of bile acids and the three mechanisms of diarrhea. Board questions rarely ask for a transporter name alone. They describe a missing carrier, a diseased or resected segment, or a toxin, and ask you to predict the stool, the deficiency, or the laboratory pattern. If you know which membrane each carrier sits on, what gradient drives it, and where along the gut it is found, you can reason through nearly all of them.","prerequisites":["Membrane transport: primary and secondary active transport, facilitated diffusion, and the Na+/K+-ATPase","Histology of the small intestine: villi, crypts, enterocytes, brush border, and lacteals (GI-2)","Luminal digestion by pancreatic enzymes and the secretion of bile (GI-6)","G protein signaling through Gs, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP, and protein kinase A","Lipoprotein structure and the roles of apoB-48 and apoB-100"],"connections":[{"to":"Digestive biochemistry and dietary requirements (GI-8)","how":"Niacin synthesis from tryptophan, fat-soluble vitamin roles, and B12 and folate metabolism explain the deficiencies that follow failed absorption."},{"to":"Deficiency and malabsorption conditions (GI-13-E)","how":"Celiac disease, ileal Crohn disease, and short bowel syndrome are predicted by where each nutrient is absorbed."},{"to":"Gallbladder diseases (GI-13-D)","how":"Loss of ileal bile acid recycling shrinks the bile acid pool and favors cholesterol supersaturation of bile."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal pathogens (GI-12)","how":"Cholera toxin and enterotoxigenic E. coli toxins hijack the crypt cell secretory pathway, while invasive organisms cause inflammatory diarrhea."},{"to":"Renal physiology","how":"The proximal tubule uses the same sodium-coupled glucose and amino acid carriers, so Hartnup disease and cystinuria affect both gut and kidney."}],"terms":[{"term":"Secondary active transport","def":"Uphill movement of one solute driven by the downhill movement of another, usually Na+ or H+, whose gradient is maintained by an ATP-using pump."},{"term":"SGLT1","def":"The apical sodium-glucose cotransporter of enterocytes that carries glucose or galactose into the cell with two Na+ ions."},{"term":"PepT1","def":"The apical H+-coupled carrier that absorbs dipeptides and tripeptides."},{"term":"Unstirred water layer","def":"A thin, poorly mixed layer of fluid next to the brush border that slows diffusion of hydrophobic molecules to the cell surface."},{"term":"Mixed micelle","def":"A small aggregate of bile acids, phospholipid, monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, and fat-soluble vitamins that carries lipids across the unstirred layer."},{"term":"Critical micellar concentration","def":"The bile acid concentration above which bile acids aggregate into micelles."},{"term":"Chylomicron","def":"The large triglyceride-rich lipoprotein made by enterocytes around apoB-48 and secreted into lymph."},{"term":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP)","def":"The endoplasmic reticulum protein that loads lipid onto apoB to assemble chylomicrons in the gut and VLDL in the liver."},{"term":"Intrinsic factor","def":"A glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells that binds vitamin B12 and is required for its uptake in the terminal ileum."},{"term":"Enterohepatic circulation","def":"The cycle in which bile acids secreted in bile are reabsorbed from the intestine, returned in portal blood, and secreted again by the liver."},{"term":"CFTR","def":"The cAMP-regulated apical chloride channel through which crypt cells secrete Cl- into the intestinal lumen."},{"term":"Stool osmotic gap","def":"290 mOsm/kg minus twice the sum of stool Na+ and K+, an estimate of stool osmolality not explained by electrolytes."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C01","obj":"GI-7.1","title":"Monosaccharide absorption and the basis of oral rehydration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Salivary and pancreatic amylase reduce starch to maltose, maltotriose, and limit dextrins, and the brush border enzymes (lactase, sucrase-isomaltase, and maltase-glucoamylase) finish the job, because enterocytes absorb only monosaccharides: glucose, galactose, and fructose. Each sugar must cross two membranes in series, the apical (luminal) membrane and the basolateral membrane that faces the blood, and the carriers on the two sides differ."},{"type":"p","text":"The basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase pumps three Na+ out for two K+ in, which keeps cytosolic Na+ near 15 mmol/L while luminal Na+ is near 140 mmol/L, and leaves the cell interior negative. SGLT1 on the apical membrane couples the entry of one glucose or galactose molecule to two Na+ ions moving down this electrochemical gradient. The coupling lets the enterocyte take up glucose against its concentration gradient, so absorption continues even when little glucose is left in the lumen. This is secondary active transport: the pump spends the ATP, and SGLT1 spends the gradient. Fructose enters through GLUT5, a facilitated diffusion carrier that needs no Na+ and moves fructose only down its gradient, so fructose uptake is slower and saturable and a large fructose load can reach the colon. All three sugars leave through GLUT2 on the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion into portal blood."},{"type":"table","title":"Monosaccharide carriers of the enterocyte","cols":["Carrier","Membrane","Substrates","Mechanism","Inherited loss"],"rows":[["SGLT1","Apical","Glucose, galactose","Secondary active, 2 Na+ per sugar","Glucose-galactose malabsorption: neonatal osmotic diarrhea, fructose tolerated"],["GLUT5","Apical","Fructose","Facilitated diffusion, Na+ independent","No classic syndrome; limited capacity explains fructose malabsorption"],["GLUT2","Basolateral","Glucose, galactose, fructose","Facilitated diffusion to portal blood","Fanconi-Bickel syndrome (mainly hepatic and renal disease)"],["Na+/K+-ATPase","Basolateral","3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in","Primary active (ATP)","Not applicable; it powers every Na+-coupled carrier"]],"caption":"Sugars enter on the apical side by SGLT1 or GLUT5 and leave on the basolateral side by GLUT2; the pump creates the Na+ gradient SGLT1 uses."},{"type":"p","text":"Water in the small intestine is never pumped; it follows solute. When Na+ and glucose are absorbed together, they raise osmolality in the lateral intercellular spaces below the tight junctions, and water follows across the leaky tight junctions and through the cells. Oral rehydration solution uses this coupling. In cholera, the toxin drives Cl- secretion through CFTR and suppresses the electroneutral NaCl absorption of villus cells (C06), but it does not touch SGLT1. Glucose given with Na+ therefore opens an absorptive route for Na+ that the toxin cannot close, and water absorption follows. Each ingredient is needed: without Na+, SGLT1 cannot move glucose, and without glucose, the main Na+ absorptive pathway left is the one the toxin has inhibited. The proportions also matter, because glucose beyond what SGLT1 can carry stays in the lumen as an osmotic load and adds to the stool volume, so effective solutions keep glucose and Na+ at similar molar concentrations and the total osmolarity at or below that of plasma."},{"type":"p","text":"Glucose-galactose malabsorption, the autosomal recessive loss of SGLT1, confirms the map: newborns have severe watery diarrhea with glucose, galactose, or lactose, yet tolerate fructose because GLUT5 and GLUT2 still work. Lactase deficiency differs in kind, since it is a failure of digestion: the disaccharide is never split, so the carriers have nothing to absorb."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would a solution of fructose and NaCl rehydrate a patient with cholera as well as a solution of glucose and NaCl?","explain":"No. GLUT5 moves fructose without Na+, so fructose uptake does not carry Na+ or water into the cell. The toxin has suppressed electroneutral NaCl absorption, so only Na+-coupled glucose (or galactose) uptake through SGLT1 restores net Na+ and water absorption.","check":"The carrier table and the paragraph on oral rehydration."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C02","obj":"GI-7.2","title":"Absorption of amino acids and small peptides; Hartnup disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pepsin begins protein digestion in the stomach, but pancreatic proteases do most of the work. Enteropeptidase (enterokinase) on the duodenal brush border converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which activates the other pancreatic zymogens, and brush border peptidases then trim the fragments. What reaches the apical membrane is a mixture of free amino acids and peptides of two or three residues. Larger peptides are not absorbed in nutritionally important amounts."},{"type":"p","text":"Free amino acids enter on several apical carriers, each serving a class of side chains rather than a single amino acid. Most are cotransporters driven by the same inward Na+ gradient that powers SGLT1, so they are secondary active. The main neutral amino acid carrier is B0AT1 (gene SLC6A19). An important exception is the carrier for cystine and the dibasic amino acids (lysine, arginine, ornithine), which exchanges them for neutral amino acids without Na+; its inherited loss causes cystinuria in kidney and gut. Amino acids then leave across the basolateral membrane on facilitated diffusion and exchange carriers into portal blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Dipeptides and tripeptides use a separate route. PepT1 is a single broad-specificity carrier coupled to H+ rather than Na+. The apical Na+/H+ exchanger (NHE3) keeps an acidic microclimate at the brush border, and that H+ gradient drives peptide entry, so peptide uptake is still powered indirectly by the Na+ gradient. Cytosolic peptidases split most absorbed peptides, which is why portal blood after a meal carries mainly free amino acids. Because one carrier accepts thousands of different peptides, PepT1 carries a large share of absorbed amino nitrogen and is a backup when a free amino acid carrier fails."},{"type":"table","title":"Main routes of amino acid and peptide entry","cols":["Substrate","Apical carrier","Driving force","Disease when lost"],"rows":[["Neutral amino acids (including tryptophan)","B0AT1","Na+","Hartnup disease"],["Cystine and dibasic amino acids (lysine, arginine, ornithine)","b0,+ exchanger (rBAT with b0,+AT)","Exchange for neutral amino acids, Na+ independent","Cystinuria (cystine kidney stones)"],["Acidic amino acids (glutamate, aspartate)","EAAT3","Na+ and H+, with K+ exit","Dicarboxylic aminoaciduria (rare)"],["Proline, hydroxyproline, glycine","IMINO and PAT1","Na+ with Cl-, or H+","Iminoglycinuria (benign, mainly renal)"],["Dipeptides and tripeptides","PepT1","H+, gradient kept by NHE3","No known human syndrome"]],"caption":"Free amino acids use several class-specific carriers, most Na+-coupled; small peptides share one H+-coupled carrier. The same free amino acid carriers work in the proximal tubule."},{"type":"p","text":"Hartnup disease is the autosomal recessive loss of B0AT1, which is expressed in both the small intestine and the renal proximal tubule. The renal sign is a neutral aminoaciduria: alanine, serine, threonine, valine, leucine, isoleucine, phenylalanine, tyrosine, tryptophan, histidine, glutamine, and asparagine appear in urine, while proline, glycine, and the dibasic amino acids are normal because they use other carriers. That selective pattern separates Hartnup disease from the generalized aminoaciduria of Fanconi syndrome. Unabsorbed tryptophan reaching the colon is converted by bacteria to indole compounds, which are absorbed and excreted as urinary indican."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical findings come from tryptophan, the precursor of part of the body's niacin (NAD+) by the kynurenine pathway, at roughly 60 mg of tryptophan per 1 mg of niacin. With tryptophan lost in urine and poorly absorbed, a marginal dietary niacin intake becomes inadequate and pellagra-like findings appear: photosensitive dermatitis on sun-exposed skin, diarrhea, intermittent cerebellar ataxia, and psychiatric change. Most people with the genotype stay well, because PepT1 still absorbs tryptophan inside peptides and a niacin-adequate diet covers the shortfall; symptoms tend to appear with poor diet, illness, or growth."},{"type":"flow","title":"From B0AT1 loss to pellagra-like disease","steps":["B0AT1 absent in gut and proximal tubule","Neutral amino acids poorly absorbed and lost in urine","Less tryptophan enters the kynurenine pathway","Less niacin (NAD+) synthesized from tryptophan","Marginal dietary niacin no longer sufficient","Dermatitis, diarrhea, ataxia, psychiatric change"],"caption":"Hartnup disease is a transport defect whose symptoms are those of niacin deficiency; peptide uptake by PepT1 limits severity."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C03","obj":"GI-7.3","title":"Fat absorption, chylomicron assembly, and abetalipoproteinemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dietary fat is mostly triglyceride, which does not dissolve in water. Bile acids and phospholipid emulsify it into droplets, and pancreatic lipase, anchored at the droplet surface by colipase, removes the fatty acids at positions 1 and 3, leaving 2-monoglyceride and two free fatty acids. Cholesterol esterase and phospholipase A2 digest cholesterol esters and phospholipids."},{"type":"p","text":"These products are poorly soluble, and the brush border is covered by an unstirred water layer through which hydrophobic molecules diffuse slowly. Above their critical micellar concentration, bile acids form mixed micelles that carry monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, lysophospholipid, and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K across this layer. Micelles are not absorbed intact. In the slightly acidic microclimate at the cell surface, fatty acids become protonated and leave the micelle, entering the cell by diffusion and by membrane proteins such as CD36 for long-chain fatty acids and NPC1L1 for cholesterol. The bile acids stay in the lumen to be reused farther down the gut and are reclaimed in the terminal ileum (C05)."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the enterocyte, smooth endoplasmic reticulum rebuilds triglyceride from 2-monoglyceride and fatty acyl-CoA, and cholesterol is re-esterified. Re-esterification keeps free fatty acid and monoglyceride low in the cytosol, preserving the inward gradient. Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) then loads triglyceride onto apolipoprotein B-48 to form the chylomicron. ApoB-48 is made only in the intestine: it comes from the same gene as hepatic apoB-100, but intestinal mRNA editing converts a cytidine to uridine and creates a stop codon, so the protein is 48% of full length and lacks the LDL receptor binding region. Chylomicrons pass through the Golgi and are exocytosed basolaterally. They are too large to enter blood capillaries, so they enter the central lacteal through its loose endothelial junctions and travel through the thoracic duct into the venous system at the junction of the left internal jugular and subclavian veins, bypassing the liver on first pass. Medium-chain fatty acids (about 6 to 12 carbons) are more water-soluble, are largely not re-esterified, and pass directly into portal blood bound to albumin."},{"type":"flow","title":"Dietary triglyceride from lumen to blood","steps":["Bile acids emulsify fat; lipase with colipase forms 2-monoglyceride and fatty acids","Mixed micelles cross the unstirred water layer","Lipids leave micelles and enter the enterocyte","Smooth ER re-esterifies them to triglyceride","MTP loads triglyceride onto apoB-48, forming chylomicrons","Exocytosis into the lacteal, then thoracic duct lymph","Entry into blood at the left venous angle"],"caption":"Long-chain fat needs bile acids, MTP, and apoB-48 to reach the blood; medium-chain fatty acids go straight to portal blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Abetalipoproteinemia is the autosomal recessive loss of MTP (gene MTTP). Without MTP neither the enterocyte nor the hepatocyte can assemble apoB-containing lipoproteins, so chylomicrons, VLDL, and LDL are essentially absent and plasma cholesterol and triglyceride are very low. Each feature follows from that single defect, as the table shows."},{"type":"table","title":"Abetalipoproteinemia: each finding and its mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Steatorrhea, failure to thrive, lipid-laden enterocytes on biopsy","Triglyceride enters the enterocyte but cannot leave without chylomicrons"],["Very low plasma cholesterol and triglyceride; no apoB","No chylomicrons from the gut and no VLDL (hence no LDL) from the liver"],["Acanthocytes (spiculated red cells)","Abnormal plasma lipids alter red cell membrane lipid composition"],["Ataxia, loss of position and vibration sense, areflexia","Vitamin E deficiency (spinocerebellar and dorsal column degeneration); vitamin E needs apoB lipoproteins for absorption and delivery"],["Pigmentary retinopathy","Vitamin E and vitamin A deficiency"],["Prolonged prothrombin time in some patients","Vitamin K malabsorption"]],"caption":"One missing protein, MTP, explains the gut, blood, and neurologic findings."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In a child with abetalipoproteinemia, would fat from medium-chain triglycerides be absorbed into the blood?","explain":"Largely yes. Medium-chain fatty acids enter portal blood bound to albumin without re-esterification or chylomicrons, so they do not need MTP or apoB-48.","check":"The paragraph on chylomicron assembly and the end of the fat flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C04","obj":"GI-7.4","title":"Where iron, folate, vitamin B12, and bile acids are absorbed","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The whole small intestine absorbs, but several nutrients depend on carriers concentrated in one segment. The duodenum takes up most iron and most actively transported calcium, the duodenum and proximal jejunum take up folate, the jejunum (with the tallest villi and largest surface) absorbs most sugar, amino acid, and fat, and the terminal ileum is the only site of vitamin B12 uptake with intrinsic factor and of active bile acid reabsorption. The ileum can take over bulk absorption after jejunal loss, but no segment can replace ileal B12 and bile acid uptake, so the site of disease predicts the deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"Iron absorption is tied to the duodenum by its chemistry. Nonheme iron is mostly ferric (Fe3+) and must be reduced to ferrous (Fe2+) by duodenal cytochrome b and dietary ascorbate before DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1) carries it in with H+. Gastric acid keeps iron soluble, and the acidic microclimate of the proximal duodenum supplies the H+ for DMT1. Iron leaves through ferroportin, is oxidized by hephaestin, and binds transferrin; hepcidin from the liver degrades ferroportin, so body iron is controlled at this exit step. Active, calcitriol-induced calcium uptake is likewise concentrated in the duodenum, while passive paracellular calcium absorption continues along the jejunum and ileum."},{"type":"p","text":"Food folate is a polyglutamate. Brush border folate conjugase (glutamate carboxypeptidase II) trims it to the monoglutamate, which the proton-coupled folate transporter carries in best at the slightly acidic pH of the duodenum and proximal jejunum. Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) follows the longest route, shown below, and its final receptor, cubilin with amnionless, is found only on terminal ileal enterocytes. Body stores of about 2 to 5 mg, mostly in the liver, cover daily losses for several years, so B12 deficiency appears long after the ileum is lost."},{"type":"flow","title":"Vitamin B12 from food to tissues","steps":["Acid and pepsin free B12 from food protein","B12 binds haptocorrin (R binder) in the stomach","Pancreatic proteases digest haptocorrin in the duodenum","Freed B12 binds intrinsic factor from parietal cells","Cubilin-amnionless in terminal ileum endocytoses the complex","B12 enters blood bound to transcobalamin"],"caption":"Absorption needs stomach, pancreas, and terminal ileum, so B12 deficiency can start at any of the three."},{"type":"table","title":"Absorption sites and the deficiencies that follow their loss","cols":["Nutrient","Main site","Key apical step","Result of loss"],"rows":[["Iron","Duodenum","Fe3+ reduced to Fe2+; DMT1 with H+","Microcytic, hypochromic anemia with low ferritin"],["Calcium (active)","Duodenum","TRPV6, induced by calcitriol","Low bone mineral, secondary hyperparathyroidism"],["Folate","Duodenum and proximal jejunum","Deconjugation, then proton-coupled folate transporter","Megaloblastic anemia; high homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid"],["Vitamin B12","Terminal ileum","Intrinsic factor-B12 taken up by cubilin-amnionless","Megaloblastic anemia; high homocysteine and methylmalonic acid; neuropathy"],["Bile acids","Terminal ileum","ASBT with Na+","Bile acid diarrhea or steatorrhea; fat-soluble vitamin loss (C05)"]],"caption":"Proximal disease costs iron, calcium, and folate; ileal disease costs B12 and bile acids."},{"type":"p","text":"Duodenal disease or bypass, as in celiac disease (most severe proximally) or a gastric bypass that diverts food past it, causes iron deficiency first, often with folate and calcium deficiency. Terminal ileal disease or resection, as in Crohn ileitis, causes B12 deficiency and bile acid loss but spares iron and folate. The two megaloblastic anemias separate biochemically: both deficiencies raise homocysteine, because both vitamins are needed to remethylate it, but only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid, because B12 is the cofactor of methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. When resection removes the ileocecal valve, colonic bacteria can colonize the small bowel, consume B12, and make folate, so serum folate may be normal or high while B12 is low."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Proximal celiac disease or a 100 cm terminal ileal resection: which causes iron deficiency, and which causes B12 deficiency?","explain":"Celiac disease causes iron (and often folate) deficiency because DMT1 and folate uptake are proximal. Resection causes B12 deficiency because cubilin-amnionless is only in the terminal ileum.","check":"The absorption site table and the final paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C05","obj":"GI-7.5","title":"Enterohepatic circulation of bile acids and the effects of ileal loss","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes make the primary bile acids, cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid, from cholesterol; the rate-limiting enzyme is cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP7A1). They are then conjugated with glycine or taurine. Conjugation lowers the pKa, so the bile acids stay ionized at intestinal pH and diffuse back across the jejunal membrane only poorly. That keeps them in the lumen above the critical micellar concentration while fat is absorbed, so their return needs a dedicated carrier in the terminal ileum."},{"type":"p","text":"The apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter (ASBT) on terminal ileal enterocytes takes up conjugated bile acids with Na+. They cross the cell and leave through the basolateral organic solute transporter (OST alpha and beta) into portal blood. Hepatocytes extract most of them on first pass with another Na+-coupled carrier, NTCP, and secrete them into bile again. Colonic bacteria convert the fraction that escapes to the secondary bile acids deoxycholic and lithocholic acid, some of which is absorbed passively. Overall about 95% of secreted bile acid is reclaimed. The pool is only about 2 to 4 g but cycles several times a day, so the gut sees far more bile acid than the liver makes, and the roughly 0.5 g lost in stool each day is replaced by new synthesis."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Enterohepatic circulation of bile acids","steps":["Liver makes bile acids from cholesterol and conjugates them","Bile acids secreted in bile form micelles in the jejunum","ASBT reabsorbs about 95% in the terminal ileum","Portal blood returns bile acids to the liver","NTCP takes them up; FGF19 signal limits new synthesis"],"caption":"The pool cycles several times daily; synthesis replaces only stool losses."},{"type":"p","text":"The returning bile acids also regulate synthesis. In ileal enterocytes they activate the nuclear receptor FXR, which induces FGF19; FGF19 reaches the liver in portal blood and represses CYP7A1, and bile acids taken up by hepatocytes suppress CYP7A1 through hepatic FXR as well. When ileal return falls, this brake is released and synthesis rises, but hepatic capacity can increase only a few-fold, so it can make up for moderate losses but not large ones."},{"type":"p","text":"The effect of ileal disease therefore depends on the amount lost. Excess bile acids entering the colon, especially the dihydroxy bile acids chenodeoxycholic and deoxycholic acid, stimulate colonocyte Cl- and water secretion and speed transit, causing watery diarrhea. Once fecal loss exceeds maximal synthesis, luminal bile acid falls below the critical micellar concentration and micelles fail."},{"type":"table","title":"Limited versus extensive terminal ileal loss","cols":["Feature","Limited (less than about 100 cm)","Extensive (more than about 100 cm)"],"rows":[["Bile acid pool","Maintained by increased synthesis","Depleted; losses exceed maximal synthesis"],["Micelle formation","Adequate","Inadequate"],["Main stool problem","Watery bile acid diarrhea","Steatorrhea"],["Fat-soluble vitamins","Usually adequate","A, D, E, and K deficiency"]],"caption":"The 100 cm boundary is approximate: where synthesis can no longer match fecal loss."},{"type":"p","text":"Gallstones follow from the same shrinking pool. Bile holds cholesterol in solution only within mixed micelles and vesicles of bile acid and phosphatidylcholine. With less bile acid secreted per unit of cholesterol, bile becomes supersaturated, cholesterol crystals nucleate, and cholesterol gallstones form, more readily when gallbladder emptying is slow. With an intact colon, fat malabsorption also causes calcium oxalate kidney stones: unabsorbed fatty acids bind luminal calcium, leaving oxalate free for colonic absorption."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the result of two ileal resections","text":"Patient A loses 50 cm of terminal ileum; patient B loses 180 cm. Both lose cubilin-bearing ileum, so both risk B12 deficiency once stores run down. In A, fecal bile acid loss rises, FGF19 falls, and CYP7A1 activity increases enough to keep the pool and micelles adequate, so fat is absorbed, but excess bile acids reaching the colon cause watery diarrhea. In B, loss exceeds maximal synthesis, the pool shrinks, micelles fail, and steatorrhea appears with vitamin A, D, E, and K deficiency. B also has the higher risk of cholesterol gallstones and, with an intact colon, oxalate kidney stones."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C06","obj":"GI-7.6","title":"Secretory, osmotic, and inflammatory diarrhea","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"About 8 to 9 L of fluid enters the small intestine each day and all but about 100 to 200 mL is absorbed, so a small shift from absorption (villus cells, colonocytes) toward secretion (crypt cells) causes diarrhea. Crypt cells secrete Cl- with three carriers: basolateral NKCC1 loads Na+, K+, and 2 Cl- into the cell, basolateral K+ channels recycle K+ and keep the cell negative, and the apical CFTR channel releases Cl- into the lumen when protein kinase A opens it. Na+ follows through the paracellular pathway and water follows the NaCl."},{"type":"p","text":"Secretory diarrhea comes from turning this secretion on (and absorption off) by a second messenger inside the epithelial cell. Cholera toxin is the model (figure); it also inhibits villus NaCl absorption but spares SGLT1 (C01). Enterotoxigenic E. coli heat-labile toxin acts the same way; its heat-stable toxin raises cGMP through guanylyl cyclase C, and VIP from a VIPoma raises cAMP. Because the stimulus is inside the cell, not in the food, secretion continues during fasting and electrolytes account for nearly all stool osmolality."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cholera toxin to secretory diarrhea","steps":["B subunit binds GM1 ganglioside on enterocytes","A subunit ADP-ribosylates Gs alpha, blocking its GTPase","Adenylyl cyclase stays active and cAMP rises","Protein kinase A opens CFTR in crypt cells","Cl- secreted; Na+ and water follow","Large-volume isotonic stool and volume depletion"],"caption":"Persistent Gs activation drives Cl- secretion; Na+-glucose cotransport is spared."},{"type":"p","text":"Osmotic diarrhea comes from a poorly absorbed solute that holds water in the lumen. In lactase deficiency, undigested lactose draws water into the small bowel, and colonic bacteria ferment it to short-chain fatty acids, hydrogen, and carbon dioxide, giving acidic stool (pH below about 6), bloating, and hydrogen in breath. Magnesium salts and sorbitol act the same way. Because the solute comes from what is eaten, the diarrhea stops with fasting, and stool Na+ and K+ are low because the unabsorbed solute, not electrolyte, fills stool osmolality."},{"type":"p","text":"Inflammatory diarrhea comes from mucosal injury by invasive or cytotoxin-producing organisms (Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella, Clostridioides difficile, Entamoeba histolytica) or by inflammatory bowel disease. Damaged epithelium absorbs less, exudes protein, blood, and leukocytes into the lumen, and releases prostaglandins and cytokines that stimulate secretion. Stools contain blood and leukocytes (or raised fecal calprotectin), with fever and tenesmus. Its osmotic gap varies with the mix of mechanisms."},{"type":"p","text":"The stool osmotic gap separates the first two. As stool passes through the colon, its osmolality equilibrates with plasma at about 290 mOsm/kg. Measured stool osmolality rises after collection as bacteria ferment, so 290 is used. Doubling stool Na+ and K+ accounts for their anions, and the gap is 290 minus 2 times (stool Na+ plus stool K+). A gap below about 50 mOsm/kg means electrolytes explain the stool, which is secretory; a gap above about 100 to 125 mOsm/kg means an unmeasured solute is present, which is osmotic."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the three types of diarrhea","cols":["Feature","Secretory","Osmotic","Inflammatory"],"rows":[["Classic causes","Cholera, ETEC, VIPoma","Lactase deficiency, magnesium, sorbitol","Shigella, Campylobacter, C. difficile, IBD"],["Stool osmotic gap","Below about 50 mOsm/kg","Above about 100 to 125 mOsm/kg","Variable"],["Effect of fasting","Continues","Stops","Often continues"],["Stool features","Large volume, watery, high Na+","Watery, low Na+; acidic with lactose","Blood, leukocytes, mucus; fever"]],"caption":"Gap and fasting separate secretory from osmotic; blood and leukocytes mark inflammatory."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating the stool osmotic gap","text":"Stool 1 has Na+ 110 mmol/L and K+ 30 mmol/L. The sum is 140, doubled is 280, and 290 minus 280 gives a gap of 10 mOsm/kg. Electrolytes explain almost all stool osmolality, so the diarrhea is secretory, as in cholera, and will persist during a fast. Stool 2 has Na+ 25 mmol/L and K+ 35 mmol/L. The sum is 60, doubled is 120, and the gap is 170 mOsm/kg, an unmeasured solute such as lactose or magnesium, so the diarrhea is osmotic and should stop with fasting."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology explain where things happen: villus height, the brush border, and segment-specific carriers place iron and folate uptake proximally and B12 and bile acid uptake in the terminal ileum, and lacteals give long-chain fat a lymphatic route that bypasses the liver. Biochemistry explains why carriers matter: the Na+/K+-ATPase powers nearly every apical uptake step directly or through NHE3, mRNA editing makes apoB-48, and tryptophan feeds niacin synthesis, which is why Hartnup disease looks like pellagra. The same carriers in the proximal tubule explain why Hartnup disease and cystinuria show up in urine."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and pathology show what happens when the system is pushed. Toxins turn on crypt secretion through cAMP or cGMP, invasive organisms and inflammatory bowel disease destroy absorptive surface, and resection or Crohn disease of the ileum breaks the enterohepatic circulation, leading to diarrhea, steatorrhea, gallstones, and oxalate stones. The limits of these rules matter: the 100 cm resection boundary and the osmotic gap cutoffs are approximate, inflammatory and mixed diarrheas do not fit the gap neatly, and the remaining bowel adapts over months, so findings change with time after injury."}],"summary":["SGLT1 moves glucose and galactose in with 2 Na+ (secondary active), GLUT5 lets fructose in by facilitated diffusion, and GLUT2 releases all three to portal blood.","Oral rehydration works because cholera toxin spares SGLT1, so Na+-coupled glucose uptake restores Na+ and water absorption.","Free amino acids enter mostly on Na+-coupled class-specific carriers; dipeptides and tripeptides enter on H+-coupled PepT1.","Hartnup disease is loss of the neutral amino acid carrier B0AT1 in gut and kidney, giving neutral aminoaciduria and pellagra-like findings from low tryptophan-derived niacin.","Long-chain fat needs micelles, re-esterification, MTP, and apoB-48 to leave the enterocyte as chylomicrons into lacteals; medium-chain fatty acids go directly to portal blood.","Abetalipoproteinemia (MTP loss) causes steatorrhea, absent apoB lipoproteins, acanthocytes, and vitamin E deficiency with ataxia and retinopathy.","Iron and active calcium are absorbed in the duodenum, folate in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, and B12 and bile acids only in the terminal ileum.","About 95% of bile acids are reclaimed by ileal ASBT; limited ileal loss causes bile acid diarrhea, extensive loss causes steatorrhea, and both raise the risk of cholesterol gallstones.","Secretory diarrhea has a low stool osmotic gap and continues during fasting; osmotic diarrhea has a high gap and stops with fasting; inflammatory diarrhea has blood, leukocytes, and fever."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Nutrient digestion and absorption; intestinal fluid and electrolyte movement"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Metabolism of dietary lipids; digestion of dietary proteins; niacin and Hartnup disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, sections on malabsorption and diarrhea"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Vibrio cholerae and cholera toxin"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Gastrointestinal Disease, sections on diarrhea and malabsorption"}]},"GI-8":{"unit":"GI-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01","title":"Digestive Biochemistry and Dietary Requirements","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers what the body must obtain from food and what happens when intake, absorption, or storage fails. It begins with essential amino acids, essential fatty acids, and the two forms of severe undernutrition, then follows the fat-soluble vitamins, vitamin B12, the water-soluble cofactor vitamins, and the minerals iron, zinc, and copper, and closes with vitamin A and D toxicity. A section on fed and fasting metabolism explains how the liver keeps fuel flowing between meals. Board questions usually describe a deficiency picture and ask for the missing nutrient, the enzyme it serves, or the laboratory value that separates two look-alikes. Linking each finding to the enzyme or protein that fails is the skill that answers them.","prerequisites":["Glycolysis, the TCA cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation","Lipid digestion: pancreatic lipase, bile salt micelles, and chylomicrons (GI-6, GI-7)","Gastric parietal cell secretion and pancreatic exocrine enzymes (GI-6)","Insulin and glucagon signaling through receptor tyrosine kinase and cAMP pathways","Collagen structure and the steps of collagen synthesis"],"connections":[{"to":"Products of digestion (GI-7)","how":"Micelle formation and chylomicron assembly are the steps that, when lost, take vitamins A, D, E, and K down together."},{"to":"Liver functions (GI-9)","how":"The liver stores vitamins A and B12, makes albumin, clotting factors, and transport proteins, and runs gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis."},{"to":"Deficiency and malabsorption conditions (GI-13-E)","how":"Celiac disease, pancreatic insufficiency, and ileal disease produce the nutrient deficiencies explained here."},{"to":"Hematology (HE-3, HE-9-A)","how":"Iron, copper, B6, B12, and folate deficiencies are the major nutritional causes of microcytic, sideroblastic, and megaloblastic anemia."},{"to":"Neurology (NE-14, NE-20-D)","how":"Thiamine, B12, vitamin E, and copper deficiencies damage the brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves through defined enzyme failures."}],"terms":[{"term":"Essential nutrient","def":"A nutrient the body cannot synthesize in adequate amounts and must obtain from the diet."},{"term":"Coenzyme","def":"An organic molecule, often derived from a vitamin, that binds an enzyme and carries electrons or chemical groups during the reaction."},{"term":"Kwashiorkor","def":"Severe malnutrition from inadequate protein relative to energy, marked by edema, fatty liver, and skin and hair changes."},{"term":"Marasmus","def":"Severe malnutrition from inadequate total energy, marked by wasting of muscle and fat without edema."},{"term":"Micelle","def":"A small aggregate of bile salts, fatty acids, and monoglycerides that carries lipids and fat-soluble vitamins to the enterocyte surface."},{"term":"Intrinsic factor","def":"A glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells that binds vitamin B12 and allows its uptake in the terminal ileum."},{"term":"Methylmalonic acid (MMA)","def":"An organic acid that accumulates when vitamin B12-dependent methylmalonyl-CoA mutase cannot convert methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA."},{"term":"Gluconeogenesis","def":"Synthesis of new glucose from noncarbohydrate precursors such as lactate, alanine, and glycerol, mainly in the liver and to a lesser extent the kidney."},{"term":"Ketone bodies","def":"Acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate, water-soluble fuels made from fatty acid-derived acetyl-CoA in liver mitochondria."},{"term":"Metallothionein","def":"A cysteine-rich intracellular protein that binds zinc and copper; induced by zinc, it traps copper in enterocytes."},{"term":"Gamma-carboxylation","def":"Vitamin K-dependent addition of a carboxyl group to glutamate residues, which lets clotting factors bind Ca2+ and phospholipid."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C01","obj":"GI-8.1","title":"Essential amino acids, essential fatty acids, and protein-energy malnutrition","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An amino acid is essential when humans cannot build its carbon skeleton at all, or not fast enough to meet need. Nine are essential in adults: phenylalanine, valine, threonine, tryptophan, isoleucine, methionine, histidine, leucine, and lysine. Others are conditionally essential. Tyrosine is made from phenylalanine, so it becomes essential in phenylketonuria; cysteine needs sulfur from methionine; and arginine synthesis may not keep up with need during growth or severe stress. Leucine and lysine are purely ketogenic, so their carbon skeletons cannot be used to make glucose during a fast."},{"type":"p","text":"Humans can desaturate fatty acids only up to carbon 9 from the carboxyl end and lack the enzymes that insert double bonds nearer the methyl end. They therefore cannot make linoleic acid (an omega-6 fatty acid, 18 carbons, two double bonds) or alpha-linolenic acid (an omega-3 fatty acid, 18 carbons, three double bonds), and both must come from food. Linoleic acid is elongated and desaturated to arachidonic acid, the precursor of prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes, and it is a component of the ceramides that seal the epidermal water barrier. Alpha-linolenic acid is converted, inefficiently, to eicosapentaenoic and docosahexaenoic acids, which are concentrated in retina and brain. Deficiency, seen with long-term fat-free parenteral feeding or severe fat malabsorption, causes scaly dermatitis, hair loss, and poor wound healing."},{"type":"table","title":"Essential dietary building blocks","cols":["Nutrient","Why it is essential","Key product or role"],"rows":[["Nine essential amino acids","Carbon skeleton cannot be synthesized","Protein synthesis; tryptophan also feeds niacin and serotonin"],["Linoleic acid (omega-6)","No desaturase acts beyond carbon 9","Arachidonic acid, eicosanoids, epidermal barrier lipids"],["Alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3)","Same desaturase limit","EPA and DHA for retina, brain, and membranes"]],"caption":"Essentiality reflects missing synthetic enzymes, not importance; many nonessential nutrients are equally vital."},{"type":"h","text":"Kwashiorkor and marasmus"},{"type":"p","text":"Protein-energy malnutrition has two classic poles. In marasmus, total energy intake is too low. The body adapts by burning fat and then muscle, so the child is severely wasted, but the amino acids released from muscle keep hepatic protein synthesis going, serum albumin stays near normal, and there is no edema. In kwashiorkor, energy intake may be adequate but protein is insufficient, often after weaning onto a starchy diet or during an infection. The liver lacks amino acids to make albumin and apolipoprotein B-100. Low albumin lowers plasma oncotic pressure, so fluid leaves capillaries as pitting edema and ascites. Without enough apolipoprotein B-100, triglyceride cannot be packaged into VLDL, so it accumulates in hepatocytes as fatty liver."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why kwashiorkor causes edema and fatty liver","steps":["Dietary protein falls while carbohydrate intake continues","Hepatic amino acid supply limits protein synthesis","Albumin falls, lowering plasma oncotic pressure","Fluid filters into tissues: edema and ascites","Apolipoprotein B-100 falls, so VLDL export fails","Triglyceride accumulates in hepatocytes: fatty liver"],"caption":"Both hallmark findings of kwashiorkor trace to reduced hepatic protein synthesis."},{"type":"table","title":"Kwashiorkor compared with marasmus","cols":["Feature","Kwashiorkor","Marasmus"],"rows":[["Main deficit","Protein, with energy relatively preserved","Total energy"],["Edema and ascites","Present, from hypoalbuminemia","Absent"],["Serum albumin","Low","Normal or mildly low"],["Liver","Enlarged, fatty","Usually normal"],["Muscle and fat","Muscle loss masked by edema; some fat kept","Severe wasting of both"],["Skin and hair","Flaky, peeling dermatosis; thin, depigmented hair","Loose, wrinkled skin"]],"caption":"Edema is the single most useful discriminator between the two."},{"type":"p","text":"Children with either form have impaired cell-mediated immunity, thymic atrophy, and frequent infections, and many have mixed features. Oxidative stress and infection may also contribute to kwashiorkor, but the protein-albumin-edema mechanism remains the standard explanation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C02","obj":"GI-8.2","title":"Fat malabsorption and deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamins A, D, E, and K are hydrophobic, so they travel the same route as dietary fat. Pancreatic lipase and colipase digest triglyceride, bile salts gather the products and the vitamins into micelles, the micelles deliver them to the jejunal enterocyte, and the enterocyte packages them into chylomicrons that enter lymph. A defect at any step lowers all four vitamins together, along with steatorrhea (bulky, pale, foul-smelling stool with excess fat)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Three routes to fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","steps":["Pancreatic insufficiency: too little lipase to digest triglyceride","Cholestasis: too little bile salt to form micelles","Celiac disease: villous atrophy reduces absorptive surface","Micelle uptake or chylomicron formation fails","Steatorrhea with low vitamins A, D, E, and K"],"caption":"Different lesions converge on the same outcome because all four vitamins depend on intact fat absorption."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin A (retinol) is converted to 11-cis-retinal, the chromophore of rhodopsin in rods, and to retinoic acid, which binds nuclear receptors that maintain specialized epithelia. Rods fail first because they work in dim light, so night blindness is the earliest sign. Loss of retinoic acid signaling causes squamous metaplasia: a dry, keratinized conjunctiva (xerophthalmia), Bitot spots, follicular hyperkeratosis of skin, and weakened epithelial defenses against infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D is hydroxylated in the liver to 25-hydroxyvitamin D and in the kidney to calcitriol, which raises intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption. When it is deficient, a slight fall in ionized calcium raises PTH, which restores calcium by resorbing bone and wasting phosphate in urine. The low calcium-phosphate product leaves newly formed osteoid unmineralized: osteomalacia in adults (bone pain, pseudofractures) and rickets in children."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) is a lipid-soluble antioxidant that stops chain reactions of lipid peroxidation in membranes rich in polyunsaturated fatty acids. Red cell membranes then break down under oxidant stress (hemolytic anemia), and long sensory axons degenerate, giving dorsal column loss, spinocerebellar ataxia, and peripheral neuropathy. This resembles B12 deficiency but without megaloblastic anemia or raised MMA."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin K in its reduced form is the cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver. Uncarboxylated factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S cannot bind Ca2+ and phospholipid, so they are secreted but nonfunctional. Factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 4 to 6 hours), so the prothrombin time (PT) prolongs before the partial thromboplastin time. Factor V is not vitamin K dependent: it is normal in vitamin K deficiency but low in liver failure, which separates the two causes of a long PT."},{"type":"table","title":"Fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies","cols":["Vitamin","Active form and role","Deficiency findings","Mechanism of findings"],"rows":[["A","11-cis-retinal in rhodopsin; retinoic acid for epithelial differentiation","Night blindness, xerophthalmia, Bitot spots, follicular hyperkeratosis, infections","Rod pigment cannot regenerate; squamous metaplasia"],["D","Calcitriol increases gut Ca2+ and phosphate absorption","Osteomalacia, rickets; low or normal Ca2+, low phosphate, high PTH and alkaline phosphatase","Secondary hyperparathyroidism wastes phosphate; osteoid stays unmineralized"],["E","Chain-breaking antioxidant in membranes","Hemolytic anemia, dorsal column loss, ataxia, neuropathy","Peroxidation of red cell and axonal membranes"],["K","Cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of II, VII, IX, X, C, S","Bruising, bleeding, prolonged PT","Factors cannot bind Ca2+ and phospholipid"]],"caption":"All four fall together in steatorrhea; the clinical picture depends on which deficit dominates."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with chronic cholestasis has a prolonged PT. What would a factor V level show, and why?","explain":"Factor V is made by the liver but is not gamma-carboxylated, so it stays normal when the long PT is caused by vitamin K malabsorption and falls only if hepatocyte synthetic function itself fails.","check":"Compare with the vitamin K paragraph and the table row for K."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C03","obj":"GI-8.3","title":"Vitamin B12 absorption and the distinction from folate deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is made only by microorganisms and reaches humans mainly in animal foods, where it is bound to protein. Its absorption requires a relay of binding proteins, each made in a different organ, and failure at any step causes deficiency. Body stores of about 2 to 5 mg, mostly in the liver, cover several years of loss, so deficiency appears long after absorption fails."},{"type":"flow","title":"Vitamin B12 from food to tissue","steps":["Gastric acid and pepsin release B12 from food protein","Free B12 binds haptocorrin (R binder) from saliva and stomach","In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases digest haptocorrin","Released B12 binds intrinsic factor from parietal cells","Cubilin receptors in the terminal ileum take up IF-B12","Transcobalamin II carries B12 in plasma to tissues and liver"],"caption":"Each step depends on a different organ: stomach, pancreas, and terminal ileum."},{"type":"p","text":"Haptocorrin protects B12 in the acid stomach, where B12 binds it more avidly than intrinsic factor; only after trypsin and other pancreatic proteases degrade haptocorrin in the alkaline duodenum can intrinsic factor take over. This is why pancreatic insufficiency can lower B12 absorption even though the stomach is normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Where B12 absorption fails","cols":["Step","Cause of failure"],"rows":[["Release from food","Atrophic gastritis, gastrectomy, long-term acid suppression"],["Intrinsic factor","Pernicious anemia (autoantibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor), gastrectomy"],["Haptocorrin cleavage","Pancreatic insufficiency"],["Ileal uptake","Crohn disease of the ileum, ileal resection"],["Competition in the lumen","Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth, fish tapeworm (Diphyllobothrium latum)"],["Intake","Strict vegan diet without supplementation"]],"caption":"Locating the failed step guides the search for the underlying disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Two enzymes, two metabolites"},{"type":"p","text":"B12 serves only two human enzymes. Methionine synthase, in the cytoplasm, uses methylcobalamin to move a methyl group from N5-methyltetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, forming methionine and regenerating tetrahydrofolate (THF). Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, in mitochondria, uses adenosylcobalamin to convert methylmalonyl-CoA, derived from odd-chain fatty acids and some amino acids, to succinyl-CoA."},{"type":"p","text":"Folate is needed only for the first reaction. A lack of either vitamin therefore raises homocysteine, but only B12 lack raises MMA. In B12 deficiency, folate also becomes trapped as N5-methyl-THF, which cannot be converted back to other forms (the methyl-folate trap). Both deficiencies therefore starve thymidylate synthesis, slowing DNA replication while RNA and cytoplasm keep growing, which produces megaloblastic anemia with hypersegmented neutrophils. B12 deficiency alone causes subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts and peripheral neuropathy, attributed to impaired myelin methylation and abnormal fatty acids built from excess methylmalonyl-CoA. Folate stores last only months, so folate deficiency appears sooner, in alcohol use disorder, pregnancy, and poor diets."},{"type":"table","title":"Laboratory distinction of B12 and folate deficiency","cols":["Finding","B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency"],"rows":[["Megaloblastic anemia","Yes","Yes"],["Homocysteine","High","High"],["Methylmalonic acid","High","Normal"],["Neurologic disease","Subacute combined degeneration, neuropathy","Absent"],["Body stores","Several years","About 3 to 4 months"]],"caption":"MMA is the deciding test because only B12 serves methylmalonyl-CoA mutase."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading homocysteine and MMA","text":"A 62-year-old with macrocytic anemia has a borderline serum B12, high homocysteine, and high MMA. Step 1: high homocysteine means methionine synthase is failing, which fits B12 or folate deficiency. Step 2: high MMA means methylmalonyl-CoA mutase is also failing, and only B12 serves that enzyme. Step 3: conclude B12 deficiency; the metabolites show intracellular function better than the serum level. With normal MMA, folate deficiency would be the answer. Kidney failure raises both metabolites, so renal function must be known first."},{"type":"stop","predict":"B12 and folate deficiency both cause megaloblastic anemia. Why does only B12 deficiency damage the spinal cord?","explain":"Both vitamins are needed for methionine synthase and thymidylate supply, so both impair DNA synthesis. Only B12 serves methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, and its failure, together with impaired myelin methylation, is linked to demyelination of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the two enzymes and the methyl-folate trap."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C04","obj":"GI-8.4","title":"Thiamine, niacin, pyridoxine, and vitamin C as cofactors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Water-soluble vitamins are stored in small amounts and turned over quickly, so deficiency develops within weeks to months of poor intake or absorption. Each deficiency syndrome follows from the reactions its coenzyme supports: the tissues that depend most on those reactions fail first."},{"type":"p","text":"Thiamine (B1) is converted to thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP), the cofactor for oxidative decarboxylation of alpha-keto acids by pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase, and for transketolase in the pentose phosphate pathway. Without TPP, pyruvate cannot enter the TCA cycle and the cycle itself slows, so ATP production falls and lactate accumulates. Neurons, which rely on glucose oxidation, are injured first, especially in the mammillary bodies, medial thalamus, and periaqueductal gray, giving Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia). A glucose load in a thiamine-depleted person increases flux toward the blocked enzymes and can precipitate it. Dry beriberi is a symmetric peripheral neuropathy; wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from peripheral vasodilation and impaired myocardial energy production. Alcohol use disorder is the usual setting, because intake, absorption, and storage are all reduced."},{"type":"p","text":"Niacin (B3) forms NAD+ and NADP+, which carry electrons in hundreds of redox reactions. Some niacin is made from tryptophan, a pathway that needs B6 and riboflavin. Tissues with high turnover and energy demand fail first, giving pellagra: photosensitive dermatitis on sun-exposed skin (including a collar around the neck), diarrhea, and dementia. Pellagra follows diets based on untreated maize, Hartnup disease (loss of neutral amino acids including tryptophan through a defective intestinal and renal transporter), carcinoid syndrome (tryptophan diverted to serotonin), and isoniazid exposure, which depletes B6 and so blocks niacin synthesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Pyridoxine (B6) forms pyridoxal phosphate (PLP), the cofactor for transamination, many decarboxylations, and the first step of heme synthesis. Erythroid ALA synthase needs PLP, so heme production falls while iron keeps arriving; iron accumulates in mitochondria around the nucleus, forming ring sideroblasts, and the anemia is microcytic. Glutamate decarboxylase needs PLP to make GABA, so deficiency lowers inhibitory tone and causes seizures, most often in infants. Peripheral neuropathy, glossitis, and cheilosis also occur."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin C (ascorbate) keeps the iron in prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases in the reduced Fe2+ state. These enzymes hydroxylate procollagen in the rough endoplasmic reticulum; hydroxyproline stabilizes the triple helix by hydrogen bonding, and hydroxylysine allows glycosylation and cross-linking. Without them, collagen is unstable and is degraded or secreted in weak form, so blood vessel walls, gums, and healing wounds fail. Scurvy presents with perifollicular hemorrhages, corkscrew hairs, swollen bleeding gums, easy bruising, poor wound healing, and subperiosteal or joint bleeding. Ascorbate also reduces dietary Fe3+ to absorbable Fe2+ and is a cofactor for dopamine beta-hydroxylase."},{"type":"table","title":"Cofactor roles and deficiency findings","cols":["Vitamin","Coenzyme","Key enzymes","Deficiency findings"],"rows":[["B1, thiamine","TPP","Pyruvate, alpha-ketoglutarate, and branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenases; transketolase","Wernicke encephalopathy, Korsakoff amnesia, dry and wet beriberi"],["B3, niacin","NAD+, NADP+","Dehydrogenases and other redox enzymes","Pellagra: dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia"],["B6, pyridoxine","PLP","ALA synthase, glutamate decarboxylase, transaminases, kynureninase","Sideroblastic anemia, seizures, neuropathy, cheilosis"],["C, ascorbate","Ascorbate (reducing agent)","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases, dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Scurvy: bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, poor healing"]],"caption":"Kynureninase in the tryptophan-to-niacin pathway explains why B6 deficiency can cause pellagra."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C05","obj":"GI-8.5","title":"Fuel supply in the fed and fasting states","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blood glucose must stay near about 70 to 100 mg/dL (3.9 to 5.6 mmol/L) between meals because red cells use only glucose and the brain uses mainly glucose. The liver manages this supply, switching between storage and release under the control of the insulin-to-glucagon ratio."},{"type":"p","text":"In the fed state, insulin dominates. Hepatocytes take up glucose through GLUT2, trap it with glucokinase, and store it as glycogen. Insulin-activated phosphatases switch on glycogen synthase and raise fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, which stimulates glycolysis, so excess carbon becomes fatty acid and leaves the liver as VLDL."},{"type":"p","text":"A few hours after a meal, glucagon dominates. It raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates the same enzymes in the opposite direction: glycogen phosphorylase is activated, glycogen synthase is inhibited, and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate falls, so gluconeogenesis is favored over glycolysis. The liver has glucose-6-phosphatase and can release free glucose; muscle lacks it, so its larger glycogen store serves only muscle. Liver glycogen is largely exhausted after about 24 hours."},{"type":"p","text":"Gluconeogenesis then becomes the main source. It reverses glycolysis using four bypass enzymes: pyruvate carboxylase (biotin dependent, activated by acetyl-CoA), PEP carboxykinase, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, and glucose-6-phosphatase. Lactate from red cells and muscle returns to the liver and is converted to glucose (Cori cycle). Alanine from muscle protein breakdown is transaminated to pyruvate, and its nitrogen goes to urea (glucose-alanine cycle). Glycerol from adipose lipolysis enters as dihydroxyacetone phosphate. Even-chain fatty acids cannot supply net glucose, because pyruvate dehydrogenase is irreversible and the TCA cycle releases two carbons as CO2 for each acetyl-CoA that enters; only the propionyl-CoA from odd-chain fatty acids is glucogenic."},{"type":"table","title":"Fuel sources across a fast","cols":["Phase","Dominant hormones","Main source of blood glucose or fuel"],"rows":[["Fed (0 to about 4 hours)","Insulin","Dietary glucose; liver stores glycogen and makes fat"],["Postabsorptive (about 4 to 24 hours)","Glucagon","Hepatic glycogenolysis, with rising gluconeogenesis"],["Early fast (about 1 to 3 days)","Glucagon, cortisol","Gluconeogenesis from alanine, lactate, and glycerol; fatty acids for muscle"],["Prolonged fast (days to weeks)","Glucagon, low insulin","Ketone bodies for brain; gluconeogenesis and muscle protein breakdown decline"]],"caption":"Time boundaries are approximate and vary with prior diet and activity."},{"type":"h","text":"Ketogenesis and why the liver cannot use ketones"},{"type":"p","text":"With low insulin, falling malonyl-CoA releases carnitine palmitoyltransferase I, so fatty acids from adipose lipolysis enter liver mitochondria for beta-oxidation. Because oxaloacetate is drawn off for gluconeogenesis, the acetyl-CoA cannot all enter the TCA cycle and is converted by mitochondrial HMG-CoA synthase (rate limiting) to acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate. Extrahepatic tissues reactivate acetoacetate with succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (thiophorase). The liver lacks this enzyme, so its ketones are exported rather than burned, and red cells, lacking mitochondria, cannot use them. After a week or more, ketones supply up to about two thirds of the brain's energy, lowering its glucose need and so the muscle protein broken down to make glucose."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ketone production and use in prolonged fasting","steps":["Low insulin and high glucagon activate adipose lipolysis","Beta-oxidation produces acetyl-CoA faster than the TCA cycle uses it","HMG-CoA synthase forms acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate","Liver lacks thiophorase, so ketones enter the blood","Brain, heart, and muscle convert ketones back to acetyl-CoA"],"caption":"Ketogenesis exports fat-derived fuel from the liver to tissues that cannot use fatty acids directly."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why fat cannot make glucose","text":"Why does muscle protein break down during a five-day fast when fat stores are large? Step 1: red cells and brain still need glucose. Step 2: acetyl-CoA from fatty acids joins oxaloacetate, and two carbons leave as CO2 before oxaloacetate is regenerated, so no net oxaloacetate or glucose is gained. Step 3: only glycerol, a small part of each triglyceride, is glucogenic. Step 4: the rest must come from amino acids such as alanine, until ketones lower the brain's glucose need. Fatty acid oxidation still helps by supplying the ATP that gluconeogenesis consumes and the acetyl-CoA that activates pyruvate carboxylase."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C06","obj":"GI-8.6","title":"Iron, zinc, and copper deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Iron, zinc, and copper are absorbed mainly in the duodenum and proximal small intestine, so they share risk from celiac disease, gastric surgery, and bypass of the proximal bowel. They also interact: copper enzymes are needed to move iron, and zinc can block copper absorption."},{"type":"p","text":"Dietary nonheme iron is reduced from Fe3+ to Fe2+ by duodenal cytochrome b on the brush border, a step helped by gastric acid and ascorbate, and enters the enterocyte through divalent metal transporter 1 (DMT1). Heme iron is absorbed separately and more efficiently. Iron leaves the cell through ferroportin, is oxidized to Fe3+ by the copper-containing ferroxidase hephaestin, and binds transferrin. Hepcidin from the liver degrades ferroportin and so limits absorption. The body has no regulated route for iron excretion, so balance is set at absorption."},{"type":"p","text":"In iron deficiency, stores are lost first (low ferritin), then transport iron falls (low serum iron and transferrin saturation, with high total iron-binding capacity because the liver makes more transferrin), and only then does anemia appear. Developing red cells keep dividing until their hemoglobin concentration reaches a threshold, so too little heme produces extra divisions and small, pale (microcytic, hypochromic) cells. In adults, iron deficiency usually reflects chronic blood loss, from menstruation in premenopausal women and most often from the gastrointestinal tract in men and postmenopausal women."},{"type":"p","text":"Zinc is a structural or catalytic cofactor for hundreds of enzymes (including alkaline phosphatase, carbonic anhydrase, and matrix metalloproteinases) and for zinc-finger transcription factors. Deficiency therefore strikes rapidly dividing tissues: poor wound healing, dermatitis around the mouth and anus and on the hands and feet (acrodermatitis), alopecia, diarrhea, impaired T cell immunity, dysgeusia, hypogonadism, and growth failure in children. Acrodermatitis enteropathica is an inherited defect of the intestinal zinc transporter ZIP4; acquired deficiency follows alcohol use disorder, malabsorption, and diets high in phytate."},{"type":"p","text":"Copper is a cofactor for ceruloplasmin and hephaestin (ferroxidases that allow iron export), cytochrome c oxidase, superoxide dismutase, lysyl oxidase, and dopamine beta-hydroxylase. Deficiency causes anemia, because iron is trapped in enterocytes and macrophages, and neutropenia. It also causes a myelopathy of the dorsal columns with sensory ataxia that closely mimics B12 subacute combined degeneration, probably from failed mitochondrial and antioxidant copper enzymes. Copper deficiency follows gastric bypass, which removes the proximal absorptive surface, and excess zinc intake."},{"type":"flow","title":"How excess zinc causes copper deficiency","steps":["High zinc intake from supplements or zinc-containing products","Zinc induces metallothionein in enterocytes","Metallothionein binds copper more tightly than zinc","Copper is trapped and lost as enterocytes are shed","Low copper: anemia, neutropenia, myelopathy"],"caption":"Zinc excess causes copper deficiency by sequestering copper inside the intestinal lining."},{"type":"table","title":"Mineral deficiencies","cols":["Mineral","Key proteins or roles","Deficiency findings","Characteristic laboratory clues"],"rows":[["Iron","Hemoglobin, myoglobin, cytochromes","Microcytic hypochromic anemia, fatigue, pica, koilonychia","Low ferritin, high TIBC, low transferrin saturation"],["Zinc","Zinc enzymes and zinc-finger proteins","Poor wound healing, dysgeusia, acrodermatitis, alopecia, hypogonadism","Low plasma zinc; low alkaline phosphatase may accompany"],["Copper","Ceruloplasmin, hephaestin, cytochrome c oxidase","Anemia, neutropenia, dorsal column myelopathy","Low serum copper and ceruloplasmin; normal B12 and MMA"]],"caption":"Normal B12 and MMA with a cord syndrome and neutropenia point to copper rather than B12."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient two years after gastric bypass has anemia, neutropenia, and a dorsal column sensory ataxia with normal B12 and MMA. What is the likely deficiency, and why are the blood counts low?","explain":"Copper deficiency; without copper ferroxidases, iron cannot leave enterocytes and macrophages to reach marrow, and copper-dependent enzymes in marrow precursors fail, lowering red and white cell production.","check":"Compare with the copper paragraph and the table row for copper."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C07","obj":"GI-8.7","title":"Vitamin A and vitamin D toxicity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fat-soluble vitamins are stored in liver and adipose tissue and are not excreted readily in urine, so excess intake accumulates. Toxicity follows high-dose supplements, retinoid drugs, or, for vitamin A, eating very large amounts of liver."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver stores vitamin A as retinyl esters in hepatic stellate (Ito) cells. When intake exceeds storage capacity, the stellate cells become engorged and activated, laying down collagen in the space of Disse; this causes hepatomegaly, perisinusoidal fibrosis, and in severe cases noncirrhotic portal hypertension or cirrhosis. Excess retinoid also raises intracranial pressure, thought to reflect impaired CSF absorption, giving headache, vomiting, papilledema, and in infants a bulging fontanelle (pseudotumor cerebri). Chronic excess also causes dry, peeling skin, alopecia, bone and joint pain, and increased bone resorption with fracture risk and sometimes hypercalcemia. Retinoic acid controls HOX gene expression along the embryonic axis, so excess early in pregnancy disrupts cranial neural crest development: craniofacial and ear malformations, conotruncal heart defects, and central nervous system defects. Isotretinoin, a synthetic retinoid, carries the same teratogenic risk, which illustrates the receptor mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D toxicity comes from very high intake, which raises 25-hydroxyvitamin D. At high concentrations, 25-hydroxyvitamin D is thought to activate the vitamin D receptor directly and to displace calcitriol from vitamin D-binding protein, raising free calcitriol. Intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption and osteoclastic bone resorption both rise, producing hypercalcemia and sometimes hyperphosphatemia. The high calcium acts on the parathyroid calcium-sensing receptor to suppress PTH, so renal calcium reabsorption falls and filtered calcium spills into urine (hypercalciuria). This causes calcium stones, nephrocalcinosis, and polyuria, because hypercalcemia blunts the collecting duct response to ADH (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus). Hypercalcemia also causes constipation, nausea, confusion, a shortened QT interval, and calcification of soft tissues when the calcium-phosphate product is high."},{"type":"p","text":"Laboratory values separate the causes of hypercalcemia. Vitamin D toxicity gives high 25-hydroxyvitamin D and low PTH. Granulomatous diseases such as sarcoidosis also give low PTH, but macrophages convert 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol without feedback control, so calcitriol is high while 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. Primary hyperparathyroidism gives high PTH with low phosphate."},{"type":"table","title":"Vitamin A and D toxicity compared","cols":["Feature","Vitamin A toxicity","Vitamin D toxicity"],"rows":[["Main storage site","Hepatic stellate cells","Adipose tissue and liver"],["Nervous system","Raised intracranial pressure, papilledema","Confusion, lethargy from hypercalcemia"],["Liver","Stellate cell activation, fibrosis, portal hypertension","Not a feature"],["Kidney","Not a main feature","Hypercalciuria, stones, nephrocalcinosis, polyuria"],["Calcium and PTH","Calcium sometimes high from bone resorption","High calcium, low PTH, high 25-hydroxyvitamin D"],["Pregnancy","Strong teratogen","Hypercalcemia risk"]],"caption":"Vitamin A toxicity centers on brain, liver, skin, and embryo; vitamin D toxicity centers on calcium."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a hypercalcemic patient with low PTH","text":"A 48-year-old taking high-dose supplements has serum calcium 12.8 mg/dL (reference about 8.5 to 10.5), low PTH, and kidney stones. Step 1: low PTH means the parathyroids are responding normally, so the calcium source lies elsewhere. Step 2: check vitamin D metabolites. A very high 25-hydroxyvitamin D with only modestly raised calcitriol points to exogenous vitamin D. Step 3: if 25-hydroxyvitamin D were normal and calcitriol high, unregulated 1-alpha-hydroxylase in granulomas would be more likely. Step 4: stones follow because low PTH and a high filtered load both increase urinary calcium."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The deficiencies in this chapter cluster by anatomy. Gastric disease removes acid, pepsin, and intrinsic factor and impairs iron and B12 absorption; pancreatic and biliary disease remove lipase and bile salts and take down vitamins A, D, E, and K; duodenal and proximal jejunal disease or bypass lowers iron, zinc, copper, and folate; and terminal ileal disease removes B12 and bile salt recycling. Reading a patient's surgical history or gut disease therefore predicts which nutrients will run short. Biochemistry then explains the findings: each vitamin or mineral serves specific enzymes, and the tissues most dependent on those enzymes fail first."},{"type":"p","text":"Several deficiencies imitate one another, and metabolites or companion tests separate them. B12, copper, and vitamin E deficiency all damage the dorsal columns, but only B12 raises MMA and only copper lowers ceruloplasmin; iron and B6 deficiency both produce microcytosis, but only B6 deficiency leaves iron stores full with ring sideroblasts. Serum levels of vitamins and minerals can mislead, because ferritin and zinc shift with inflammation and serum B12 may stay borderline, so functional markers and the whole clinical pattern carry more weight than any single value."}],"summary":["Essential nutrients are those whose synthesis humans lack: nine amino acids and the omega-6 and omega-3 fatty acids linoleic and alpha-linolenic acid.","Kwashiorkor is protein lack with low albumin (edema) and failed apolipoprotein B-100 export (fatty liver); marasmus is energy lack with wasting and no edema.","Fat malabsorption from pancreatic, biliary, or mucosal disease lowers vitamins A, D, E, and K together: night blindness, osteomalacia, hemolysis with neuropathy, and a prolonged PT.","B12 absorption needs acid and pepsin, haptocorrin, pancreatic proteases, intrinsic factor, and the terminal ileum; homocysteine rises in B12 or folate deficiency, MMA only in B12 deficiency.","Thiamine (TPP) serves alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenases, niacin forms NAD+, B6 (PLP) serves ALA synthase and GABA synthesis, and vitamin C keeps collagen hydroxylases active.","Glycogenolysis supports glucose for about a day, then gluconeogenesis from lactate, alanine, and glycerol; even-chain fatty acids cannot make net glucose.","The liver makes ketones from fatty acid acetyl-CoA but lacks thiophorase, so it exports them to brain, heart, and muscle.","Iron deficiency gives microcytic anemia with low ferritin and high TIBC; zinc deficiency gives poor healing, dysgeusia, and acrodermatitis; copper deficiency gives anemia, neutropenia, and myelopathy.","Excess zinc induces metallothionein that traps copper; gastric bypass removes the proximal surface that absorbs iron and copper.","Vitamin A excess causes intracranial hypertension, stellate cell liver injury, and birth defects; vitamin D excess causes hypercalcemia with low PTH, hypercalciuria, and stones."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins; Minerals; Metabolic effects of insulin and glucagon; The feed-fast cycle"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Dietary balances, regulation of feeding, obesity and starvation, vitamins and minerals"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Nutrient digestion and absorption; energy metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the exocrine pancreas; gastrointestinal disease"}]},"GI-9":{"unit":"GI-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01","title":"Liver Detoxification, Bilirubin, and Gland Functions","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the work the liver and digestive glands do beyond digestion itself. It traces bilirubin from heme to stool and uses that pathway to read jaundice panels, explains how hepatocytes transform drugs and toxins and how that same chemistry injures zone 3 in acetaminophen and carbon tetrachloride poisoning, and links the loss of hepatic synthesis and ammonia disposal to the findings of liver failure. It closes with the protective chemistry of saliva and the way the gallbladder concentrates and releases bile. Board questions usually give a lab panel, an exposure, or a failing organ and ask which step broke; each section is built so you can work back to that step.","prerequisites":["Hepatic lobule and acinus, including zones 1 to 3 and portal blood flow (GI anatomy units)","Red cell life span and splenic removal of senescent red cells","Enzyme cofactors NADPH, UDP-glucuronic acid, and glutathione","Starling forces and plasma oncotic pressure","Composition of bile and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts"],"connections":[{"to":"Hematology (hemolytic anemias)","how":"Hemolysis raises bilirubin production, which explains unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, high urine urobilinogen, and black pigment gallstones."},{"to":"Pathology (cell injury)","how":"Acetaminophen and carbon tetrachloride are the standard models of bioactivation, glutathione depletion, and free radical membrane injury."},{"to":"Biochemistry (nitrogen metabolism)","how":"The urea cycle in periportal hepatocytes disposes of ammonia, so its failure explains hyperammonemia and hepatic encephalopathy."},{"to":"Hemostasis","how":"Hepatic synthesis and vitamin K-dependent carboxylation of clotting factors explain why the prothrombin time is the earliest marker of synthetic failure."},{"to":"Immunology and oral health","how":"Secretory IgA, lysozyme, and lactoferrin in saliva are mucosal defenses whose loss in Sjogren syndrome leads to rampant caries."}],"terms":[{"term":"Unconjugated bilirubin","def":"The water-insoluble product of heme breakdown that travels in plasma bound to albumin and is reported as indirect bilirubin."},{"term":"Conjugated bilirubin","def":"Bilirubin linked to glucuronic acid by UGT1A1, which makes it water-soluble, excretable in bile, and filterable into urine; it is reported approximately as direct bilirubin."},{"term":"Urobilinogen","def":"Colorless products formed when gut bacteria deconjugate and reduce bilirubin; a small reabsorbed fraction appears in urine."},{"term":"Biotransformation","def":"Enzymatic conversion of a lipophilic drug or toxin into a more polar product that can be excreted in urine or bile."},{"term":"Cytochrome P450 (CYP)","def":"A family of heme-containing monooxygenases in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum that use NADPH and O2 to carry out most phase I oxidations."},{"term":"Bioactivation","def":"Metabolism of a relatively harmless compound into a reactive, toxic product, such as NAPQI from acetaminophen or the trichloromethyl radical from carbon tetrachloride."},{"term":"Glutathione","def":"A tripeptide of glutamate, cysteine, and glycine whose thiol group conjugates electrophiles and reduces peroxides inside cells."},{"term":"Lipid peroxidation","def":"A free radical chain reaction that oxidizes polyunsaturated fatty acids in membrane phospholipids and destroys membrane integrity."},{"term":"Centrilobular (zone 3) necrosis","def":"Death of hepatocytes around the central vein, the region with the most CYP2E1 and the least oxygen."},{"term":"Hepatic encephalopathy","def":"Reversible brain dysfunction caused mainly by ammonia and other gut-derived toxins that bypass or overwhelm a failing liver."},{"term":"Xerostomia","def":"Dry mouth from reduced salivary flow, which removes buffering, remineralization, and antimicrobial protection of the teeth."},{"term":"Cholecystokinin (CCK)","def":"A hormone released by duodenal and jejunal I cells in response to fat and protein that contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C01","obj":"GI-9.1","title":"The bilirubin pathway from heme to stool and urine","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bilirubin is the end product of heme breakdown. An adult makes about 250 to 350 mg per day, roughly 80% from senescent red cells removed by macrophages of the spleen, liver, and bone marrow, and the rest from ineffective erythropoiesis and other heme proteins such as myoglobin and cytochromes. Each step of the pathway changes the solubility of the pigment, and solubility decides where bilirubin can travel and where it appears when a step fails."},{"type":"h","text":"Formation in the macrophage"},{"type":"p","text":"Heme oxygenase, a microsomal enzyme that uses O2 and NADPH, opens the porphyrin ring of heme in the rate-limiting step of heme breakdown, releasing green biliverdin, ferrous iron that is stored in ferritin or returned to transferrin, and carbon monoxide, which is exhaled. Biliverdin reductase then uses NADPH to reduce biliverdin to yellow unconjugated bilirubin. Internal hydrogen bonds fold this molecule so that its polar groups are hidden, making it nearly insoluble in water. It therefore travels in plasma tightly bound to albumin, which keeps it out of the glomerular filtrate and, normally, out of the brain."},{"type":"h","text":"Uptake, conjugation, and excretion in the hepatocyte"},{"type":"p","text":"At the sinusoid, bilirubin dissociates from albumin and enters the hepatocyte by facilitated transport (the carriers are not fully defined), then binds cytosolic ligandin, a glutathione S-transferase that limits its escape back to blood. In the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1 (UGT1A1) transfers glucuronic acid from UDP-glucuronic acid, forming mainly bilirubin diglucuronide. Conjugation disrupts the internal hydrogen bonds and makes bilirubin water-soluble. Multidrug resistance-associated protein 2 (MRP2), an ATP-dependent pump on the canalicular membrane, then exports it into bile. Export is the rate-limiting step of hepatic bilirubin handling, which is why diffuse hepatocyte injury raises conjugated bilirubin: the injured cell can still conjugate but cannot excrete, and conjugated pigment refluxes into blood."},{"type":"h","text":"Fate in the intestine"},{"type":"p","text":"Conjugated bilirubin is not absorbed by the small intestine. In the terminal ileum and colon, bacterial beta-glucuronidases remove the glucuronides and bacterial reductases convert bilirubin to colorless urobilinogen. Most urobilinogen is oxidized to stercobilin, the brown pigment of stool. A small fraction (about 10 to 20%) is reabsorbed into portal blood; the liver re-excretes most of it, and the remainder is filtered by the kidney and oxidized in urine to yellow urobilin. This small enterohepatic loop is the basis of the urine urobilinogen test."},{"type":"flow","title":"Bilirubin from heme to stool and urine","steps":["Macrophage heme oxygenase: heme to biliverdin, Fe2+, and CO","Biliverdin reductase: biliverdin to unconjugated bilirubin","Albumin carries unconjugated bilirubin to the liver","Hepatocyte uptake; ligandin binding","UGT1A1 adds glucuronic acid (conjugated bilirubin)","MRP2 pumps conjugated bilirubin into bile (rate-limiting)","Gut bacteria deconjugate and reduce it to urobilinogen","Most oxidized to stercobilin in stool; some reabsorbed","Small fraction excreted in urine as urobilin"],"caption":"Solubility changes at conjugation: before UGT1A1 bilirubin needs albumin and cannot enter urine; after it, bilirubin is water-soluble and can be filtered if it backs up into blood."},{"type":"p","text":"The newborn is the main physiologic exception. A high red cell mass with a shorter life span, low UGT1A1 activity, and a sterile gut in which mucosal beta-glucuronidase deconjugates bilirubin for reabsorption instead of conversion to urobilinogen together raise unconjugated bilirubin, typically peaking about day 3 to 5. If free unconjugated bilirubin exceeds albumin binding, or a drug such as a sulfonamide displaces it from albumin, the lipid-soluble pigment crosses the blood-brain barrier and stains the basal ganglia (kernicterus)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A large bruise resolves over two weeks. Predict its color changes and which serum bilirubin fraction, if any, rises.","explain":"Tissue macrophages degrade the heme in place, making green biliverdin and then yellow bilirubin, so the bruise turns from purple-blue to green to yellow. Pigment made there is unconjugated, so any serum rise is in the unconjugated (indirect) fraction.","check":"Formation in the macrophage and the albumin transport step."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C02","obj":"GI-9.2","title":"Reading jaundice panels: hemolytic, hepatocellular, and obstructive","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Serum bilirubin is reported as total and direct. Direct bilirubin reacts with the diazo reagent without an accelerator and approximates the conjugated fraction; indirect bilirubin (total minus direct) approximates the unconjugated fraction. Typical adult values are a total of about 0.3 to 1.2 mg/dL with a direct fraction under about 0.3 mg/dL, and jaundice becomes visible, first in the sclerae, above about 2 to 3 mg/dL."},{"type":"p","text":"Two urine tests complete the panel. Urine bilirubin appears only when conjugated bilirubin is raised, because unconjugated bilirubin is bound to albumin and not filtered; dark, tea-colored urine with a positive dipstick places the problem in the liver or biliary tree. Urine urobilinogen reflects how much bilirubin reaches the gut and how well the liver reclaims what is reabsorbed. It rises when more bilirubin is made or when injured hepatocytes fail to re-extract it, and it falls toward zero when bile cannot reach the intestine."},{"type":"p","text":"In hemolytic (prehepatic) jaundice, production outruns hepatic uptake and conjugation, so unconjugated bilirubin rises, urine bilirubin is absent (acholuric jaundice), and urine urobilinogen rises because more conjugated bilirubin enters the gut. Because a normal liver conjugates a large load, hemolysis alone seldom raises total bilirubin above about 4 to 5 mg/dL. In hepatocellular jaundice, uptake, conjugation, and especially excretion fail together, so both fractions rise and aminotransferases are markedly raised. In obstructive (posthepatic) jaundice, conjugated bilirubin refluxes into blood, little or none reaches the gut, and retained bile induces canalicular and bile duct cells to make and release alkaline phosphatase (ALP) and gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT)."},{"type":"table","title":"Jaundice by mechanism","cols":["Pattern","Serum bilirubin","Urine bilirubin","Urine urobilinogen","Stool","Enzymes"],"rows":[["Hemolytic (prehepatic)","Unconjugated high","Absent","Increased","Normal or dark","Normal liver enzymes; LDH up, haptoglobin down"],["Hepatocellular","Both fractions high","Present","Normal or increased","Normal or pale","ALT and AST markedly high"],["Obstructive (posthepatic)","Conjugated high","Present, dark urine","Decreased or absent","Pale, clay-colored","ALP and GGT markedly high"]],"caption":"Urine bilirubin tells you whether the conjugated fraction is up; urine urobilinogen tells you whether bilirubin is reaching the gut."},{"type":"p","text":"Prolonged conjugated hyperbilirubinemia explains a common puzzle: some conjugated bilirubin binds covalently to albumin (delta bilirubin), measures as direct, is not filtered, and clears with the roughly 3-week half-life of albumin, so after an obstruction is relieved the urine clears quickly while scleral icterus fades slowly. Inherited disorders act at single steps and leave other liver tests normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Inherited hyperbilirubinemias","cols":["Disorder","Defective step","Fraction raised","Distinguishing feature"],"rows":[["Gilbert syndrome","Reduced UGT1A1 expression (promoter variant)","Unconjugated","Mild jaundice with fasting, illness, or exertion; common and benign"],["Crigler-Najjar type I","Absent UGT1A1 activity","Unconjugated","Severe from birth; kernicterus"],["Crigler-Najjar type II","Markedly reduced, inducible UGT1A1","Unconjugated","Milder; kernicterus uncommon"],["Dubin-Johnson syndrome","MRP2 canalicular export","Conjugated","Black liver from retained pigment"],["Rotor syndrome","OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 reuptake","Conjugated","Normal liver color; milder"]],"caption":"Defects before conjugation raise unconjugated bilirubin; defects after conjugation raise conjugated bilirubin."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing jaundice from the panel","text":"A 58-year-old has jaundice, total bilirubin 9 mg/dL with direct 7 mg/dL, dark urine positive for bilirubin, absent urine urobilinogen, clay-colored stools, ALP four times normal, and ALT mildly raised. Step 1: direct makes up most of the total, so the liver is conjugating and the problem lies at or after excretion. Step 2: positive urine bilirubin confirms a raised water-soluble conjugated fraction. Step 3: absent urobilinogen and pale stool mean bilirubin is not reaching the gut, so the flow of bile is blocked. Step 4: high ALP with modest ALT points to bile duct injury rather than hepatocyte necrosis. Conclusion: obstructive jaundice, as from a common bile duct stone or a tumor of the pancreatic head. Hemolysis would instead show indirect predominance, no urine bilirubin, and high urobilinogen."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C03","obj":"GI-9.3","title":"Phase I and phase II biotransformation and acetaminophen toxicity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lipophilic drugs and toxins cross membranes easily, so the kidney would filter and then reabsorb them almost indefinitely. The liver converts them into polar products that stay in urine or bile. This biotransformation takes place mainly in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum and cytosol of hepatocytes and runs in two phases."},{"type":"p","text":"Phase I reactions (oxidation, reduction, and hydrolysis) introduce or expose a functional group such as hydroxyl, amine, or thiol. Most oxidations are done by cytochrome P450 monooxygenases, which receive electrons from NADPH through NADPH-cytochrome P450 reductase and insert one oxygen atom from O2 into the substrate while reducing the other to water. CYP activity can be induced (CYP2E1 by chronic ethanol) or inhibited, which changes how much of any metabolite forms. Phase I products are often only slightly more polar and sometimes more reactive than the parent compound, which is the basis of bioactivation."},{"type":"p","text":"Phase II reactions attach a large polar donor group, which usually inactivates the molecule and makes it water-soluble. Glucuronidation has high capacity, sulfation has high affinity but limited capacity and saturates first, and glutathione conjugation captures electrophiles. Glutathione, a tripeptide of glutamate, cysteine, and glycine, is made in the hepatocyte at a rate limited by cysteine supply. Conjugates leave through canalicular and basolateral transporters such as MRP2."},{"type":"table","title":"Phase I and phase II biotransformation","cols":["Feature","Phase I","Phase II"],"rows":[["Reactions","Oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis","Glucuronidation, sulfation, glutathione conjugation, acetylation, methylation"],["Main enzymes","Cytochrome P450 monooxygenases","UGTs, sulfotransferases, glutathione S-transferases, N-acetyltransferases"],["Cofactor or donor","NADPH and O2","UDP-glucuronic acid, PAPS, glutathione, acetyl-CoA"],["Location","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Mostly cytosol; UGTs in endoplasmic reticulum"],["Effect on product","Small rise in polarity; may become reactive","Large rise in polarity; usually inactive"]],"caption":"Phase I can create a toxic metabolite; phase II usually removes it."},{"type":"p","text":"Acetaminophen shows how the two phases balance. At therapeutic doses, most is glucuronidated and sulfated, and only about 5 to 10% is oxidized, mainly by CYP2E1 with contributions from CYP3A4 and CYP1A2, to N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI), an electrophile that glutathione neutralizes at once. In overdose, sulfation and then glucuronidation saturate, more drug flows through CYP oxidation, and glutathione is consumed faster than it is made. When hepatic glutathione falls to roughly 30% of normal, free NAPQI binds cysteine residues on cellular proteins, especially mitochondrial proteins, producing oxidative stress, ATP depletion, and necrosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Acetaminophen overdose to zone 3 necrosis","steps":["Overdose saturates sulfation, then glucuronidation","More drug is oxidized by CYP2E1 to NAPQI","Glutathione conjugates NAPQI until stores are depleted","Free NAPQI binds cysteine groups on mitochondrial proteins","Oxidative stress and ATP failure cause hepatocyte necrosis","Necrosis concentrates in zone 3 around the central vein"],"caption":"The toxic step is not the drug itself but the reactive metabolite left over once glutathione is exhausted."},{"type":"p","text":"Necrosis is centrilobular (zone 3) because hepatocytes around the central vein hold the most CYP2E1, receive the least oxygenated blood, and have smaller glutathione reserves than periportal cells. Aminotransferases often climb into the thousands of units per liter and peak about 3 to 4 days after ingestion. Chronic alcohol use (CYP2E1 induction and low glutathione) and fasting or malnutrition (low glutathione) lower the toxic threshold. N-acetylcysteine protects because it supplies cysteine for new glutathione, which confirms that glutathione depletion is the critical step."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two people take the same large acetaminophen dose. One drinks heavily every day; the other has been fasting for several days. Who is at greater risk, and through which step?","explain":"Both are at higher risk than a healthy person. Chronic drinking induces CYP2E1, so more NAPQI forms, and also lowers glutathione; fasting lowers glutathione and the sulfate and glucuronide donors, so less NAPQI can be neutralized. Either change tips the balance toward free NAPQI.","check":"The acetaminophen flow and the paragraph on zone 3 and risk factors."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C04","obj":"GI-9.4","title":"Carbon tetrachloride: free radical injury in the liver","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Carbon tetrachloride (CCl4), a former dry-cleaning solvent, is the classic model of free radical injury. CCl4 itself is relatively inert. In the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, CYP2E1 transfers an electron to it, releasing a chloride ion and forming the trichloromethyl free radical (CCl3•). A free radical has an unpaired electron in its outer orbital, which makes it highly reactive and able to start chain reactions."},{"type":"p","text":"CCl3• reacts with O2 to form the trichloromethyl peroxyl radical (CCl3OO•). These radicals pull hydrogen atoms from polyunsaturated fatty acids in membrane phospholipids, starting lipid peroxidation. The lipid radicals that result react with O2 to form lipid peroxyl radicals, which attack neighboring fatty acids, so damage spreads autocatalytically through the endoplasmic reticulum membrane until vitamin E or glutathione peroxidase ends the chain. Peroxidation fragments fatty acids into reactive aldehydes, such as malondialdehyde and 4-hydroxynonenal, that cross-link proteins. The radicals also inactivate the cytochrome P450 that produced them."},{"type":"p","text":"Fatty change comes first. Within hours, the injured endoplasmic reticulum swells, ribosomes detach, and protein synthesis falls, including synthesis of apolipoproteins. Without apolipoprotein, the hepatocyte cannot package triglyceride into very low density lipoprotein (VLDL) for export, so lipid collects as cytoplasmic vacuoles. Injury then spreads to the plasma membrane and mitochondria. Ca2+ enters through the damaged membrane and activates phospholipases, proteases, and endonucleases, and mitochondrial injury stops ATP production, so the cells swell, lose membrane integrity, and die by necrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The necrosis is centrilobular for the same reason as in acetaminophen injury: CYP2E1 is concentrated in zone 3, where oxygen is lowest. Chronic ethanol use, by inducing CYP2E1, increases CCl4 toxicity, and repeated exposure causes fibrosis, which is why CCl4 is used experimentally to produce cirrhosis. The two toxins differ in the reactive species and in what protects against them."},{"type":"flow","title":"Carbon tetrachloride to centrilobular necrosis","steps":["CYP2E1 reduces CCl4 to the CCl3• radical","CCl3• plus O2 forms the CCl3OO• radical","Radicals start lipid peroxidation in endoplasmic reticulum membranes","Ribosomes detach; apolipoprotein synthesis falls","Triglyceride cannot leave as VLDL: fatty change","Membrane and mitochondrial injury; Ca2+ influx, ATP loss","Zone 3 necrosis; fibrosis with repeated exposure"],"caption":"A single enzymatic step turns an inert solvent into a radical whose chain reaction does the damage."},{"type":"table","title":"Acetaminophen and carbon tetrachloride compared","cols":["Feature","Acetaminophen overdose","Carbon tetrachloride"],"rows":[["Activating enzyme","CYP2E1 (also CYP3A4, CYP1A2)","CYP2E1"],["Toxic species","NAPQI, an electrophile","CCl3• and CCl3OO• free radicals"],["Primary target","Cysteine groups on cell proteins","Polyunsaturated lipids in membranes"],["Key protection","Glutathione conjugation","Antioxidants that end radical chains"],["Early morphology","Necrosis","Fatty change, then necrosis"],["Zone injured","Zone 3","Zone 3"]],"caption":"Both are bioactivation injuries in zone 3; they differ in the chemistry of the toxic product."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C05","obj":"GI-9.5","title":"Hepatic synthesis, ammonia disposal, and the findings of liver failure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes make most plasma proteins, and portal blood passes through the liver before reaching the systemic circulation, so liver failure produces too little of what the liver makes and too much of what it should remove."},{"type":"h","text":"Albumin and clotting factors"},{"type":"p","text":"Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein (about 3.5 to 5.0 g/dL). The liver makes about 10 to 15 g per day, and its half-life is about 20 days. Albumin supplies roughly 75 to 80% of plasma oncotic pressure, so a low level favors filtration, causing edema and, with portal hypertension and renal Na+ retention, ascites. Because of its long half-life, albumin reflects chronic rather than acute synthetic failure."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver makes fibrinogen, prothrombin (factor II), factors V, VII, IX, X, XI, XII, and XIII, the anticoagulants protein C, protein S, and antithrombin, and plasminogen. Factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S need vitamin K-dependent gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues to bind Ca2+ and phospholipid. The prothrombin time (PT, about 11 to 13.5 s; INR about 1.0) measures factor VII and the common pathway (X, V, II, fibrinogen). Factor VII has the shortest half-life, about 4 to 6 hours, so the PT lengthens within a day of acute failure and is the earliest marker of synthetic function. In cholestasis the PT is long from vitamin K malabsorption and corrects with parenteral vitamin K; in hepatocellular failure it does not, because synthesis itself has failed. Factor VIII, made mainly by endothelial cells, stays normal or high in liver disease but is consumed in disseminated intravascular coagulation."},{"type":"h","text":"Ammonia and the urea cycle"},{"type":"p","text":"Ammonia comes from colonic bacterial urease, enterocyte glutamine breakdown, and amino acid catabolism. Periportal (zone 1) hepatocytes convert it to urea, and perivenous (zone 3) hepatocytes trap what remains as glutamine. In mitochondria, carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (CPS I), activated by N-acetylglutamate, combines NH3 and HCO3- into carbamoyl phosphate, the committed step; ornithine transcarbamylase forms citrulline, aspartate adds the second nitrogen in the cytosol, and arginase releases urea for renal excretion. Urea cycle failure therefore lowers BUN while raising ammonia."},{"type":"p","text":"When hepatocyte mass falls or portosystemic shunts carry portal blood around the liver, ammonia reaches the brain. Astrocytes lack a urea cycle and detoxify it with glutamine synthetase; glutamine accumulates, draws in water, and swells the astrocytes, and neurotransmission is disturbed. Alkalosis shifts NH4+ to freely diffusible NH3, hypokalemia increases renal ammonia production, and gastrointestinal bleeding adds a protein load, so each can precipitate encephalopathy."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hepatic encephalopathy","steps":["Gut bacteria and amino acid breakdown make ammonia","Portal blood carries ammonia toward the liver","Low urea cycle capacity or portosystemic shunts let it pass","Ammonia crosses the blood-brain barrier","Astrocytes make glutamine from glutamate and ammonia","Astrocyte swelling and disturbed neurotransmission","Confusion, asterixis, then stupor and coma"],"caption":"Asterixis is a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands caused by brief lapses in postural tone."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings of liver failure and the function lost","cols":["Finding","Lost function"],"rows":[["Prolonged PT, bleeding (factor VII falls first)","Clotting factor synthesis"],["Hypoalbuminemia, edema, ascites","Albumin synthesis"],["Hyperammonemia, low BUN, encephalopathy","Urea cycle"],["Jaundice","Bilirubin uptake, conjugation, and excretion"],["Hypoglycemia","Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis"],["Gynecomastia, spider angiomas","Hormone inactivation"]],"caption":"Each finding traces to a specific hepatic function."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: timing of PT and albumin","text":"Two days after an acetaminophen overdose, a patient has a PT of 28 s and an albumin of 4.1 g/dL. Step 1: factor VII (half-life about 4 to 6 hours) is nearly gone after two days without synthesis, so the PT lengthens sharply. Step 2: albumin (half-life about 20 days) falls well under 10% in two days, so it stays normal. Step 3: a normal albumin does not mean the liver is working. In long-standing cirrhosis both are abnormal, because months of reduced synthesis have lowered albumin."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C06","obj":"GI-9.6","title":"Protective functions of saliva and caries when flow falls","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The salivary glands secrete about 1 to 1.5 L per day. The parotid makes serous saliva rich in alpha-amylase, the submandibular a mixed secretion, and the sublingual mostly mucus. Secretion is driven chiefly by parasympathetic fibers (facial nerve to the submandibular and sublingual glands, glossopharyngeal nerve through the otic ganglion to the parotid) acting on M3 muscarinic receptors; sympathetic stimulation adds a smaller, protein-rich flow. Acinar cells secrete an isotonic, plasma-like fluid, and the ducts reabsorb Na+ and Cl- and secrete K+ and HCO3-, so resting saliva is hypotonic. Stimulated flow carries more HCO3-, so buffering capacity is greatest during meals, when acid challenge is highest."},{"type":"p","text":"Enamel is mostly hydroxyapatite, a calcium phosphate crystal. After sugar intake, plaque bacteria such as Streptococcus mutans ferment carbohydrate to lactic acid, and plaque pH falls within minutes. Below a critical pH of about 5.5, the fluid around the enamel is undersaturated with hydroxyapatite and mineral dissolves. Saliva reverses this three ways: its flow clears sugar and acid, its bicarbonate (with phosphate and proteins) buffers plaque acid back toward neutral, and because it is supersaturated with calcium and phosphate, mineral redeposits in early lesions once pH recovers (remineralization). Proteins such as statherin keep calcium phosphate in solution without letting it precipitate, and fluoride taken up during remineralization forms fluorapatite, which resists acid better."},{"type":"table","title":"Antimicrobial and protective components of saliva","cols":["Component","Action"],"rows":[["Secretory IgA","Dimeric IgA from local plasma cells, carried across the epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor; blocks bacterial adherence"],["Lysozyme","Hydrolyzes the bond between N-acetylmuramic acid and N-acetylglucosamine in peptidoglycan"],["Lactoferrin","Binds iron, depriving bacteria of an essential nutrient"],["Lactoperoxidase","Oxidizes thiocyanate to hypothiocyanite, which inhibits bacterial metabolism"],["Histatins","Antifungal peptides active against Candida"],["Mucins","Lubricate and coat the mucosa and aggregate bacteria for swallowing"]],"caption":"These defenses act together with flow, buffering, and remineralization."},{"type":"p","text":"Caries form when demineralization outpaces remineralization over time. When flow falls (xerostomia from Sjogren syndrome, radiation to the head and neck, dehydration, or drugs with anticholinergic action that block M3 receptors), sugar and acid stay on the teeth longer, buffering and remineralization fail, and antimicrobial proteins are lost. Caries then progress rapidly and appear at sites that saliva normally protects well, such as the cervical margins at the gumline, exposed root surfaces, and incisal edges. Loss of these defenses also favors oral candidiasis and, with slow flow through the ducts, ascending bacterial parotitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C07","obj":"GI-9.7","title":"Gallbladder concentration, contraction, and gallstone formation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The liver secretes about 600 to 1,000 mL of bile per day, but the gallbladder holds only about 30 to 60 mL. Between meals the contracted sphincter of Oddi diverts hepatic bile into the gallbladder, which stores it and concentrates it, usually about 5-fold and up to about 12 to 20-fold."},{"type":"p","text":"Concentration is driven by active Na+ transport. Na+ enters the apical membrane in exchange for H+, Cl- enters in exchange for HCO3-, and the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase pumps Na+ into the lateral intercellular spaces, with Cl- following. The local osmotic gradient draws water across the epithelium, so absorption is nearly isotonic. Bile salts, cholesterol, phospholipid, and bilirubin are large or held in micelles and are not absorbed, so they become concentrated, and the exchange of Na+ for H+ acidifies gallbladder bile, which keeps calcium salts more soluble."},{"type":"p","text":"Fatty acids and amino acids in the duodenum stimulate I cells of the duodenal and jejunal mucosa to release cholecystokinin (CCK). CCK acts on CCK1 receptors on gallbladder smooth muscle, and through vagal pathways, to contract the gallbladder, and it relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, so concentrated bile arrives when there is fat to emulsify. Vagal acetylcholine causes modest contraction in the cephalic phase, and the gallbladder partly empties with the migrating motor complex between meals."},{"type":"p","text":"Cholesterol is insoluble in water and stays in solution only in mixed micelles with bile salts and phosphatidylcholine. Bile becomes supersaturated when the liver secretes too much cholesterol (obesity, estrogen, aging) or too little bile salt (ileal disease or resection that breaks the enterohepatic circulation). Supersaturation alone is not enough: cholesterol monohydrate crystals must nucleate, a step promoted by mucin from the gallbladder wall, and they must remain long enough to grow. Concentration raises the levels of cholesterol and calcium, and stasis provides the time. Stasis follows fasting and total parenteral nutrition (no fat in the duodenum, so little CCK), pregnancy (progesterone relaxes smooth muscle), rapid weight loss, and spinal cord injury."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cholesterol gallstone formation","steps":["Excess cholesterol or too little bile salt reaches bile","Micelles cannot hold all the cholesterol: supersaturation","Gallbladder absorbs water, concentrating cholesterol and calcium","Low CCK or impaired motility leaves bile stagnant","Mucin promotes nucleation of cholesterol crystals","Crystals aggregate into sludge, then stones"],"caption":"Stones need supersaturation, nucleation, and stasis together."},{"type":"p","text":"Pigment stones follow the same logic for bilirubin. In chronic hemolysis more bilirubin enters bile, some is deconjugated, and unconjugated bilirubin precipitates with calcium as black calcium bilirubinate stones in the gallbladder. In biliary infection, bacterial beta-glucuronidase deconjugates bilirubin in the ducts, forming soft brown stones."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient on total parenteral nutrition for six weeks develops gallbladder sludge. Which step of normal gallbladder function has been lost, and why does that matter?","explain":"With nothing entering the duodenum, I cells release little CCK, so the gallbladder rarely contracts. Bile keeps being concentrated and stays put, giving cholesterol crystals and calcium salts time to nucleate within mucin and form sludge.","check":"The CCK paragraph and the gallstone flow."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and biochemistry explain where injury lands. Blood enters the acinus at the portal tract and leaves at the central vein, so zone 1 hepatocytes receive the most oxygen and do most urea synthesis, while zone 3 cells, low in oxygen and rich in CYP2E1, take the brunt of bioactivated toxins and of ischemia. The same solubility chemistry links hematology to the liver and gallbladder: hemolysis raises unconjugated bilirubin and urine urobilinogen and can form black pigment stones, while obstruction of the duct that drains a stone-filled gallbladder causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with pale stool."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology explain the lab pattern. Each finding of liver failure traces to a lost function, and the time course follows protein half-lives, so the PT reports acute failure while albumin reports chronic disease. The limits matter too: direct bilirubin only approximates conjugated bilirubin, albumin also falls in malnutrition, nephrotic syndrome, and inflammation, serum ammonia correlates only loosely with the depth of encephalopathy, and reference ranges vary by laboratory, so values are read together rather than one at a time."}],"summary":["Heme oxygenase makes biliverdin, iron, and CO; biliverdin reductase makes unconjugated bilirubin, which rides on albumin and cannot enter urine.","UGT1A1 conjugates bilirubin with glucuronic acid; MRP2 export into bile is rate-limiting; gut bacteria make urobilinogen and stercobilin.","Urine bilirubin means conjugated hyperbilirubinemia; urine urobilinogen rises in hemolysis and disappears in complete obstruction.","Hemolytic jaundice is unconjugated and acholuric; hepatocellular raises both fractions and ALT; obstructive is conjugated with pale stool and high ALP.","Phase I (CYP450, NADPH, O2) adds a reactive group and can bioactivate; phase II conjugation makes products polar and usually inactive.","Acetaminophen overdose saturates conjugation, NAPQI depletes glutathione and binds proteins, and necrosis is centrilobular (zone 3).","CYP2E1 converts CCl4 to the trichloromethyl radical, which drives lipid peroxidation, fatty change from failed VLDL export, and zone 3 necrosis.","The PT (factor VII, half-life about 4 to 6 hours) is the earliest marker of synthetic failure; albumin (about 20 days) reflects chronic disease.","Ammonia normally becomes urea in zone 1; in liver failure it reaches astrocytes, forms glutamine, and causes encephalopathy with asterixis.","Saliva buffers acid, remineralizes enamel, and kills bacteria; xerostomia causes rapid cervical and root caries. The gallbladder concentrates bile by Na+ absorption, contracts with CCK, and forms stones from supersaturation, nucleation, and stasis."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract; The Liver"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Hepatobiliary function, bilirubin metabolism, and salivary secretion"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Heme degradation and bilirubin; Urea cycle and ammonia metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell injury, chemical (toxic) injury and free radicals; Liver and Gallbladder"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Liver Disease"}]}});
